      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Terribly Weak, Bloodless and Nerve-shaken.</head>
        <p>Terribly Weak, Bloodless and Nerve-shaken.</p>
        <p>"I.am 21 years of age. About a year ago, when I went to business I had health and energy on my side. But after a few months my appetite began to fail, and after taking anything to eat I felt as though I had swallowed something which was burning me. "I began to have fearful headaches, and was so weary that I could only get through my work with difficulty. Yet when I got to bed at night I hardly got a wink of sleep. I started taking tonics, but these inflamed my stomach, and I had to give them up. "Then I became so weak and nerve-shaken that I was compelled to leave business. I used to faint and never felt warm. My ankles swelled and I had little or no feeling in my limbs. I had other advice but for nearly ten months I had been gradually getting worse. "I happened to read a report in the newspaper of a woman who had been cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. So I got a supply and after a few boxes of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills there was a won- derful change for the better. To start with, I became more cheerful and was able to get about without being fatigued. Then, after further supplies my appetite returned, and I could take meals without pain after. I slept better and my nerves were steady. So I went on getting better, for Dr. "Williams' Pink Pills were giving me New Blood. Now I feel well and strong and fit for work." Sufferers needing further information should write to the address below for free booklet Diseases of the Blood." Not only Anmmia, but the many disorders that arise from Impure Blood and weak Nerves, have been cured in countless cases by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. These Pills are sold by most dealers or can be obtained direct, price 2s. 9d. a box or 13s. 9d. for six boxes post free from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. Never accept the shopman's " something else" ; insist on having what you ask for, -viz. :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills</p>
        <p>THE PILLS THAT STRENGTHEN.</p>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. - NOTES FOR lITIii READERS.</p>
        <p>Peer, Lord Londonderry, or contumacious countesses. _ _ .</p>
        <p>But, of course, the Belfast meeting dodge has already failed to realise the hopes of the Belfast rebels. It is now discerned that Unionist opinion in Ireland is manipulated by a small Council of place-seekers and notoriety-hunters. Incitements are addressed to the Orange mob by gentlemen who would when the fighting came, keep well out of the firing line. When instigating violence the agitators do not concern themselves with political issues, but fling to the crowd a few I catchcries to inflame religious prejudice. The Belfast supporter of Carson and London- derry is not a political enthusiast.A quarter of an hour in Belfast will suffice to shew that he is too ignorant to deserve that description. The real tactical blunder to our mind however, of the so-called " Ulster " Council, is that they have served the cause of Home Rule by this display of what a lead- ing English journal calls "lawless insolence." Nationalists would be sorry if these Belfast fanatics attempt to make good their words. The row in Sidney-street is still fresh in the memory of a law-abiding community, and if Belfast stoops to ruffianism at the call of this Council, so much the worse for the political cause of that Council. Whether they do or they do not act, their words have now gone into history. It was their clearly announced desire to destroy a public meeting even though this should involve the loss of life. The point will be emphasised no matter what may be the circumstances under which the meeting will be held. An effort is to be ' made to deny the use of the Ulster Hall to the promoters of the meeting. By the time this article is in print that point will have been settled one way or the other. But if it should succeed, we venture the hope here that the gathering will be held as near the Ulster Hall as possible, no matter what precautions may be neces- sary to preserve the public peace, and whatever place may be selected by Carson and his lieutenants in this approach- ing event, which the whole world will watch, nothing is so sure as the courageous stand which Mr. Churchill, with his acknowledged dauntlessness, will assume on that day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>APOSTLES OF VIOLENCE.</head>
        <p>THE APOSTLES OF VIOLENCE.</p>
        <p>Too much importance has been attached to the pious homilies addressed by some Unionist journals to the gentlemen who are planning a breach of the peace on the occasion. of a visit to Belfast by Mr. Winston Churchill, together with Mr. Redmond and Mr. Devlin, It is now evident that no objection to the design was entertained by any Unionist newspaper, and that the only cause for criticism was the premature disclosure of the method by which Unionist Belfast was to declare itself on the Bth of February. Even the "Morning Post" which at first appre- hended the measures suggested by the Stand- ing Committee of the Ulster Unionist Council as a gross outrage on a Constitu- tional principle, and therefore a danger to the Unionist cause, is no longer at any pains to conceal its approval of the plans of the organisers of riot. The mob, inflamed by the speeches of Sir Edward Carson and the resolution adopted by a meeting at which an ex-Viceroy of Ireland presided, will be glad to learn from a counsellor posing as "moderate " that "self-preservation is the highest law, and in, face of a danger threatening everything they hold dear, the people of Ulster are resolved to over-ride traditions and conventions which are respected in ordinary times." The "Morn- ing Post" adds: "Nothing is to be gained by ranting about Ulsteria, or by pretending that the Ulstermen are not prepared to make good in words." In all this, as anyone can plainly see, the incitement to violence is very thinly disguised. The appeal will be trans- lated by the mob to mean that they should get their revolvers ready and prepare for the:throwing of " kidneys " and the wreck- ing of the Nationalist property.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TifE TRUE SIGNIFICANCE.</head>
        <p>TifE TRUE SIGNIFICANCE.</p>
        <p>Unionist criticism on the decision of Lord Londonderry and his colleagues in Ulster Unionist Council may be considered within brief compass. There is a show ei regret that Ulster terrorists should regard such action as necessary ; but it is hoped that the moral of this display of violent and uncon- stitutional action will not be lost upon the British public. The true significance of the Ulster attitude is ignored. Why should not the Lancashire textile workers, for instance, claim the right the Carsonites are endeavouring to assert? If one political body contemplates and publicly proclaims its intention to organise a riot, why should not other political bodies follow suit? If the authorities which are responsible for the maintenance of order, about which Unionists shouted their throats dry years agoin the days of Davitt and Parnell, enforce the lawless akase of the Ulster Unionist Council they e.ould not logically hold back when mvi.ted by other organisations to enforce similar decrees. There is no likelihood of such a course being " taken. A more probable and natural development is that steps should be taken at once to shew the Executives sense of the action of the organisers of breaches of the peace. In other times the advocates of intimidation and riot would have been promptly flung into prison, but even in these tolerant days it is not too much to submit that those who urge the suppression of public meeting and free speech by mob law are not entitled to hold the Commission of th 3 Peace. Perhaps this element of the situation may be borne in mind by the Lord Chancellor. when con- sidering candidates for J.P.ships on the recommendation of the peace-disturbing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE IN AUSTRALIA</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY SERVICE IN AUSTRALIA</p>
        <p>Modifications In the System.</p>
        <p>The new system of compulsory military training for youths between the ages of 14 and 18 in Australia (says the Melbourne correspondent of the " Manchester Guardien,") has needed considerable amending. The registration and organisation work has on the whole been satisfactorily done, hut the drill obligations have proved too severe. Surprise visits to drill centres by the Inspector General have resulted in the discovery that the voluntary absentees averaged over 30 per cent, and in several instances exceeded 40 per cent. Protests poured in upon the Government, with the result that a few dais ago, with the practically unanimous consent of all parties, a short amending bill was put through both Houses materially reducing the hours of drill, and modifying the system under which non attendants at drill could be imprisoned in barracks. Under the new Act the training is to consist of drill extending over 64 hours in each year, as against 96 hours. It will not be necessary to insist on three hours' continucns grind at elementary work or to penalise lads who are prevented by bad weather from fulfilling their obligatioLs. Labour menthers not in the official circle are very nervous about the liability of the nous forces to serve under arms in times of industrial unrest. Ssnator Rae lately described the fear Ma bogey which was exploited by those who were against universal training for any purpose. He concluded : " There was not th 3 slightest intention to use the citizen forces for other purposes than defence against foreign aggression."</p>
        <p>FOUR NEGROES LYNCHED. HAMILTON kGeorgia), Jan, 22,</p>
        <p>_ Three rnen and a woman, all negroes, have been lynched here. The mob over-powered the gaoler and took the four prisoners out of the prison Their bodies were riddlsd with bullets. They were charged with the murder of a white farmer.—Reuter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
