      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WARD SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>An excellent tea followed by a meeting and concert were held in the Brick-street schoolroom on Tuesday. The affair was promoted by the St. John's Ward Branch of the Warrington Worrea's Liberal Associa- tion and the Ward captain (Mrs. Dutton) along with i her auxiliaries are to be congratulated on baying prepared and conducted a most successful function. About 400 persons sat down to a substantial tea and at the subsequent meeting this number was consider- ably augmented. An excellent programme had been arranged and the following artistes contributed solos, humorous song, duets, trips and recitations, the Misses Spruce, Jones and Woodland, Mr. Ellison, Mr. Lowe and the Misses Lomas and Ridgway. Mrs. John Crosfield (Colwyn Bay), pres decl over the meting and she was supported on the platform by the Rev W and Mrs Cooper, Mr H New, Mr and Mrs P Peacock, Mrs Walker, Mrs Pemberton, Miss Booth, Mrs Osborne, Mrs Dutton, Mrs Arthur Peacock, Mrs F Peacock, Mrs Brocklehurst, Mrs Langley and others. •</p>
        <p>Mrs. CEOSFIELD, who was accorded a hearty reception, in opening the proceedings said she could assure them it gave her the greatest pleasure to pre- side over such a cheerful gathering. She was sure they would al join hearti!y in congratulating Mrs. Dutton for the way in which she had worked in the preparation of such a gathering. (Hear, hear.) She (the speaker) was given to understand that the St. John's Ward was one of the poorest in Warrington and, therefore, it must be one of the most difficult to work. (Hear, hear.) The Warrington Women's Liberal Association was to be congratulated on its increasing membership and the fine band of lady , captains, with Mrs. Osborne at the head, were deserv- ing cf praise. The membership had intarased by 500 during the year and she thought it would be nice if each member would bring along a friend. They should try to bring pressure to bear and reach a membership of 2,000 to commence 1913. (Applause.) They, women, were citizens to be remembered as , much as men were, for they had the interests of a great empire at heart just as much as the men. (Hear, hear and applause). The Rev. W. COOPER, in an interesting address, said be was sure they were all delighted to see Mrs. Crosfield in the chair. (Hear, hoar) Her appearance in Warrington was an inspiration and a help to the Interests wnich weie so dear to their hearts. He was also pleased to see Mr. New on the platform. (Loud applause.) He was glad to see him there because he had been to the Dumping Exhibition. ("Oh.") If they had seen the courteous manner in which be bad been treated they would understand what a relief it was for him to see hiss there. (Laughter.) They ventured to ask one or two civil questions and expected to get civil and straightiorward answers—and it would have been pt seible to give them— but no, they would Le prepared to pay £lOO and that was all they could get out of them. (Laughter). It was one of the most pleasant experiences he had had for some time and at the close be said " Let us go where people can be civil " so they came to a Liberal meeting. (Applause). Continuing, the rev, gentleman said be was glad to be there because Mrs. Dutton had invited him and in consideration of her efforts he was glad to consent. Those functions were doing much to strengthen the back-bone of Liberalism because they got to know each other, aud by mixing together they got more faith in the cause. Those efforts evidently disturbed their opponents so much that some of them took them to task. They</p>
        <p>would observe the letters which had been in the paper, and he (the speaker) was wai ting for a straightforward argumentative reply. (A VOICE: "You will have to wait.") I believe so.' There had been a good deal of personal abuse—(hear, hear)—but one expected that,and It had come as expected. One of the correspondents who wrote in the paper finished his letter with "flee bee', haw, haw." He knew the Tory party were short of brain power but he never knew they would come down to braying at an opponent. (Laughter.) He would speak of the state of affairs that had existed for a long time under the Tory regime. He would contrast things which obtained when the House of Lords were in power and when they had a good Liberal Government. (Applause.) Ile supposed that some of those people were a trifle sorry because their old privileges had gone and they realised that the views of the people must prevail. Some of them were called "last ditchers" and some of the party seemed to be in the last ditch and they would resort to any measure to extricate themselves. They heard of Dumping Exhibitions and lavish expense in connection with elections. Some of those people now found that if they wanted their old privileges they must capture the vote of the people. Let them not be hoodwinked. When the Insurance Bill came into force they were bound to have a lot to go through. He would bring out one or two points in the Insurance Act and show how the things obtained to-day and how they did in the good Aci times—so called. When be was seven years old his people move] into Lancashire and when his father was 94 years of age he was thrown out of employment and had a breakdown in health. Tao farmer who had employed him at its. per week said he could not do with him any longer. He lent them a cart to take him away and because there was a house famine they could not get a house and a friend took them in for a time. After four years his;father was not able to work and then he died at the age of 48. That was simply how the Tory party regarded the so-called poor but now the voice of Lloyd-George bad been beard. (Loud and continued applause.) Had the Insurance Act been in vogue than, instead of his father being roughly turned out and left to tette care of himself, they would have had 10s. per week coming in for the father for 26 weeks in addition to the free attendance of a doctor and free medicire. If at the end of that period it had been discovered that the father ought to g ) to a sanatorium he would have been taken there free of cost and would not have to pay his own expenses. He would have received the very best treatment, because the State now recognised that he had done his best and deserved some reward. While the family was in want, they would have hod 103. per week coming in, during the breadwinner's incapacitation. If there had been sickness in the family they would have had free medical attendance. It would mean a great deal to that audience composed of women, some of whom were mothers. He was there to claim that the Government who had put that Act on the Statute Book deserved the very best support. (Applause). It had proved itaelf to be the GOOD SAMARITAN Ok' TIIE POOR.</p>
        <p>In years gone by the rich bad the privileges, but it was time th 3 poor had a look in. (Applause.) The Government had a long programme of work before it, and he was sorry their representative was not there also. (hear, bear.) He had seen a gruesome sight that afternoon at the Dumping exhibition. They had a coffin on which was a label which read : " Free Trade in Warrington died Decem- ber IWO." If Free Trade had died, then why that exhibition ?If Free Trade were dead the xhibition would be stir et fluous. At the next election they would reverse that. The coffin would contain something other than Free Trade. (Hear, hear and applause). / Mr. NEW, who was well received, said he had that afternoon been introduced to Lady Greenall. She said she was coming to some of their meetings later on and remarked that when she did come she hoped they would say the same of her as Ahen she was not there. He had replied that the day had not come when he dare not I repeat what he had said. (Hear, hear.) Continuing, Mr. New said that when one came to look at the faces of the ladies present and looked at some of those who wore the Union Jack, there was a striking contrast. He wondered if there was another organisation in the town to equal theirs. There might be, hut he had his doubts about it. The essence of Liberalism was freedom and when the working people of the country had reached that period of life when they could enjoy freedom, they should have it. Liberalism taught them to think of others as well as themselves and to take a deep earnest interest in the lives of those around them and have a care for the children. Speaking on the Insurance Act, Mr. New gave many illustrations and said that the old people were at present living in fear. If they wanted to lay down their work their only rest was the workhouse. W hen the Liberal party brought forth that measure which gave them fresh air and freedom it was a boon. He was proud of the Children's Bill for it meant such a lot. Tne Liberal Women's organisation had done much which the men could not do. They bad led the way and shown that if the men were not successful, they (the women) were. Continuing he said there were 50,000 men, women and children who suffered from consumption. Mr. Lloyd George had said " enough." When a criminal escaped they spent money to get him back to justice. If a man had nom- mitted murder and tried to commit suicide they gave him the best attention and brought him back to health so that they could hang him. Was it not worth spend- ing money to save the poor sufferers from c msumption? Alluding to the Dumping Exhibition, Mr New said the fabrics used at that exhibition were common trash. It was commoner than any he had seen in the smallest shops in Warrington. They did not fear Tariff Reform at the next election. The people who were proud of the Union Jack were patriots. A patriot was one who desired to see a peaceful country and seelthat all lived under conditions which were happy and sweet. Tariff reformers could no longer cry Look at Germany " because it had gone red. The work of Liberalism was a noble work. It meant freedom and better conditions. The solution of the Housing problem would soon be an accomplished fact. In speaking of Mrs. Osborne he said she did a good work and was worthy of all praise. If they would work hard and stand for freedom their reward would be a happy, prosperous country. (Applause). Mrs. OSBORNE then announced that the winnPr of the prize for the best 21b. loaf was Mrs. liewitt,of John- street. Mrs. CROSFIEILD congratulated the lady who was 70 years of age. (VOICES;" One for the Old Age</p>
        <p>Pensioners," and another," That's Free Trade andj a the big loaf." (Laughter). Mr. PEAcocx, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfield, the ward captain, and the artistes, said he would like also to congratulate the St. John's Ward. They had increased in numbers more than any other. (Applause.) It needed no advertisements to tell how much Mrs. Crosfleld did for the poor of Warrington. (Applause.) He would like them to go forward and return Mr. A. H. Crosfleld at the next election and be that would be real and true thanks to their true friend Mrs. Crosfield. (Applause.) _... BRIDGE seconded and in response Mrs. Oros- field said she thanked them heartily for their kind reception. She also thanked the speakers for their eloquent addresses. She knew that ward was one of the poorest in Warrington and she hoped it would not he the last time she would be there. (Hear, hear, and applause.) She (the speaker) had been enlightened as to the Insurance Act by the speakers. She did approve of the Bill and believed it would be a great boon when it became law, especially among the poorer classes. (Loud applause.) A most etjo3 able evening was brcught to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
