      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PREACHING REBELLION.</head>
        <p>PREACHING REBELLION.</p>
        <p>Sir George Doughty, M.P., Encourages</p>
        <p>Ulster Violence.</p>
        <p>There was much vigorous beating of the Ulster drum and fiery talk about violence and revolution at the anti-Home Rule meeting held in the Parr Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday evening. The determination of the Ulster Unionists to prevent free speeeh in Belfast was not only condoned but commended, and the audience was treated to a lurid fore- cast of the shooting down of valiant "loyalists," at the behest of Mr. Winston Churchill. Despite the bellicose tone of the speeches, the meet- passed off very pracefully. There was one indication of the presence of an opposition element. Although the meeting was ostensibly called for the purpose of denouncing Home Pule, the principal speaker—Sir George Doughty, MP.—could not resist the temptation to say hard things about Mr. Lloyd George's Budget. The mention of the Chancellor's name evoked some applause, followed by an interruption and cries of "Chuck him out." "No don't chuck him out" said Sir George, "He never was in such good company in his life "—a sally which restored the good humour of the meeting. The chair was taken by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and on the platform was the usud array of party leaders. When Sir George Doughty made his appearance, the baud from Walker's Brewery hailed him with the strains of "See the conquering hero comes."</p>
        <p>Of ruddy, beaming countenance, Sir George has a genial way of ingratiating himself with an audience. He is also a hard hitter and denounced with much vehemence the Government and its works and, in particular, the Nationalist party. He did not Pc rupl o to charge Mr. Churchill with arranging to visit Belfast with the deliberate intention of creating a riot and he boldly baczed up the Mister men in offeringresistance to the" traitors," as he termed them.</p>
        <p>" The next two or three years " said Sir George at the outset "may determine whether we are to be a great united Empire or a number of little kingdoms scattered about the world. Britishers who believe in the history of their nation, who are proud of all that has been done to place us where we are to-day, if they have got any grit in them, will shoulder every instru- ment—l had almost said musket—and use every means in their power to see that we continue and increase our power of unity in the interest of those who live to-day and of the future of our Empire. We are living in revolutionary times and when you are in the midst of a revolution you cannot act as you would—and indeed you ought not to act as you would under ordinary, sensible, rational government. We are not living under rational government to-day. We are being governed by a lot of place-seekers and time servers who will sell their country to keep themselves in power." Continuing, he said : Whatever happens in Belfast mist be upon the head of the First Lord of the Admiralty, for deliberately and designedly arranging that serious happenings may occur there. If Mr. Churclall wants to express his opinion on the qeustion of Hama Rule, let him come to Warrington—(cheers)—and I ventuie to Bay that, although some of you would like a bit of fun with him —(laughter)—as Englishmen you will give him a fair hearing. That is a different matter altogether to going deliberately into the camp of those who feel that their best interests in life are being sacrificed, in order that they may create such a disturbance as probably history will, with regret, record. If he is going to foist himself upon those people and regiments of soldiers and police are to be taken there, who is going to pay the bill ? (Laughter.) It will be la vast cost upon the taxpayers of this country and it could and ought to be avoided if an ordinary and sensible Minister of the Crown acted as a wise and prudent man should do. Is it fair and just for a Minister of the Crown to put our soldiers in the position of being rsquired to shoot down their own nationality? Surely any man realizing the temper of the people of any country, would not go and put his head into the noose and, having injured probably him- self, risk the injury of brave and noble men who are serving their King and country and who ought not to be required to obey orders to shoot citizens dawn if they try to keep Mr. Churchill out of Belfast." Sir George next advanced the familiar argument that the Government had " sold " the electcrs of England to the Irish party in order to secure the votes of that party to keep themselves in office. The veto of the House of Lords, Sir George declared, was the trust which that House held in the people's interest. Was it fair and just that the Government should impose upon the country legislation against which it had emphatically spoken in years gone by ? He believed that if we could have an election on the question of Home Rule, pure and simple, there were not ten con- stituencies in the whole of England and Wales that would vote for such a treacherous and wicked measure. Sir George characterised as " bluff " the statements of the Nationalists that they were friendly to England and desired only a subordinate Parliament. The Nationalist policy was so black and so full of wicked deeds that one hardly dare print it in a book, for the record of their transactions towards all men who were aginst them had been nothing short of murder, many and many a time. They told us that Ireland had been down-trodden and that the reason was because the landlords were such wicked men. His view was—and history would strengthen the position—that the real cause of the great suffering of Ireland and the loss of its position and of many of its people was the introduction of Free Trade more than anything else. Whatever advantage or disadvantage Free Trade bad been to England, it certainly ruined Ireland. Home Rule would make it possible for the men who created the Irish Land League, and who terrorised the whole of Ireland to coerce one third of the population into carrying out their behests. After dealing with Conservative legislation on Irish affairs, Sir George said that Ireland to-day was as prosperous as England. The star of hope had risen over that land, and she had as bright a prospect, attached to the British Crown and Parliament, as any other part of our Kingdom or Empire could have. Ireland already had as much self- government as we in:England had got but, the fact that there were only 16 Unionists on all the County Councils as compared with 63i Nationalists, showed how they gave the minority a chance. Home Rule meant the separation of Ireland from Great Britain and the establishment of a nation alongside ours, that must be a source of danger to us. Sir George concluded by painting a glowing picture of a federal union of all the British dominions.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD SMITH, , who seconded the resolu- tion of protest against Home Rule, had a tilt against Mr. Tom Wing, the successful opponent of Sir George Doughty in the flrat 1910 election. "We have had Mr. Wing in Warrington "said Mr. Smith. "His sole claim to fame is that he beat Sir George Doughty and, if I may use the expression, be has been flying about ever since, telling everybody how he did it. This political bird of Paradise has sung the same monotonous song with only one verse and with the chorus the same as the verse—' Flow I beat Sir George Doughty.'" (Laughter.) In opposing Home Rule, continued Mr. Smith, they were fighting not only the Liberal Government or John Redmond and company, but they were fighting England's enemies who hid behind the protection of a foreign power. It was only when the last election was almost over and Mr. Asquith saw it was impossible for him to have a majority independent of the Irish vote thatoin answer to a Scottish heckler, he announced that it was the settled intention of the Government to introduce a Home Rule measure. " I would be glad r o face my opponent at any time he likes" said Mr. Smith "and to ask the electors of Warrington to give their votes, 'Aye or 'No,' on this subject alone. I would be glad if that day of battle could be joined to-morrow. I have no doubt what the men of Warrington would say and I never had the slightest doubt as to what the women of Warrington would say." (Laughter and applause). The resolution of protest was carried with one dissentient.</p>
        <p>_ A vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert Greenall was proposed by Mr. W. Furness and after the meetlng Sir George Doughty headed a procession to the dumping exhibition.</p>
        <p>Dr. Henry Bentley, of Stockport, who is charged with manslaughter in connection with the recent motor fatality, was at Stockport on Tuesday committed to Chester Assizes, bail being allowed as before. He pleaded not guilty, and reserved his defence.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND with warm water is an excellent for sore Throat, Colds, Coughs, &amp;c.—Advt.</p>
