      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>I was sure real and Mr.</head>
        <p>I was sure real and Mr.</p>
        <p>THE NEW HEREDITY.</p>
        <p>Paper by Dr. Manson</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Field Club, under the presidency of Mr. A. J. Jolley, J.P., was held in the Museum lecture room on Saturday evening, when 'a paper on "Mendelian Heredity," was read by Dr. Manson. The LECTURER gave a brief biographical sketch of Mendel, who was the abbot of Brum, in Austria, and whose dissatisfaction with the Darwinian theory of the origin of species, caused him to make a series of breed- ing experiments with the edible pea. The results of his researches were published in 1866. These results, confirmed by others, have led to the establishment of what is known as the Mendelian Theory of Heredity. Dr. MANSON then graphically explainet that the chief princple of this theory, is knot% n as the segrega- tion or separation of characters in the germ cell, and that the treet'ng of like characters in the fertilized germ, all gives rise to the homozygous or pure type, while the meeting of unlike characters in the fertilized germ cell, gives rise to the heterozygous or impure type. By means of diagrams, the lecturer showed in a most lucid and interesting manner, the results of some of Mendel's experiments. The crossing of the tall and dwarf pea plants, resulted in a generation of all tall plants, no dwarfs appearing at all. Tallness, in this instance, Mendel called the dominant character, and dwarfness, the recesive character. When this post, hybrid generation fertilized itself, there resulted 25 per cent, of pure tails, 50 per cent. of impure tells, and 25 per cent, of pure dwarfs. In this generation the recessive character reappeared. For these phenomena to occur, there must have been a separation of the characters, in the germ cells of the second hybrid generation. Many other characters in peas and in otter plants and animals, have been studied on Mendelian lines, and have been found ts accord with Mendelian principles. Man also has teen subjected to Mendelian scrutiny, and by means of a clever and scientifically valuable (tiagram, prepared by Dr. Manson himself, from cases of hereditary cataract, which have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>come under his own observation, he showed that many diseases and defects follow, to a certain extent, Mende- lian lines, although in man, there are great difficulties and complications that can be eliminated in the experi- mental studies of chants and aaimals. In discussing the ultimate utility of the study of Mendelian principles, Dr. Manson contended that the establishment of Mendelian heredity, gives a basis for more scientific practice in the arts of the breeder and I horticulturist ; it is modifying the conceptions of the process of evolution, in the production of the variety of types in living beings, and in man, it is likely to give a more scientific basis to sociology, so that the distinctions between nature and nurture may be more fully understood. The science of eugenics or race hygiene has the latter object in view, and the lecture- advocated the establishment of a branch of the Eugenics Education Society in Warrington. On the motion of Mr. CHARLES ArADELEY, seconded by Mr. WILFRID ROBINSON, B.Sc., a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. Manson for his valuable paper, and a lively and critical discussion of the paper followed, in which Messrs. Jolley, Madeley, Robinson, Scholefield, Woodcock, Greening, Webster, Dunlop,, Ridyard, Dr. Nader), and W. Mounfield took part. The latter gentleman brought a case of lepidoptera, show- ing melanic forms grossulariata betularia and liturata,, in the breedingZof which the !Mendelian theory was apparently proved. It may be of interest to note that two of our local lepidopterists, Messrs. Mounfield, and F. Womersley were closely associated with Doncaster and Raynor in their experiments with the currant moth (abraxas- grossularista) and its variety lacticolor. These experi- ments may now be regarded as classical, owing to their important bearing on the theory of sex.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DEATH OF THE HON. W. LOWTHER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE HON. W. LOWTHER.</p>
        <p>nao 3-3 -mmaci-ozixi PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. M</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>The Hon. William Lowther of Campsea As‘ie, father- of the Speaker of the House of Commons, died rather suddenly on Tuesday at Felixstowe. He reached big 90th birthday on December 11th, when several members of his family were present. Tbe death has taken place at Ballymahon, Co. Long- ford, of Michael Gannon, at the age of 103. The deceased was a journeyman tailor, and was at work until quite recently.</p>
        <p>from a reliable utility strain. A good clear. bright, eye and a red comb is a sure ir,dication of good health. The breast bone should also be straight and he should also be well set up on a good straight pair of sturdy legs. As the Cheshire farmers say, he should have some Bant 'about him. Carefully observe all these points as they are essential to the breeding of good stock. We now come to the female occupants of the breeding pen.</p>
        <p>THE HENS.</p>
        <p>These should be selected with very great care. Seeing that we are dealing solely with utility stock,. the question of colour markings is not of primary importance. Of course if you can get usefulness and beauty combined so much the better, because the more taking your stock is to the eye, so• much more will they appeal to would-be purchasers of eggs for hatching. The salient points of the female occupants of the breeding pen are health and vigour. There are, however, other points to observe, when taking into consideration the various breeds. For instance, supposing you are mating up a pen of any of the Mediterranean breeds, great care should be taken in not selecting those with combs too large, and also they should not be too much after the exhibition type. Deeper the keel and longer the body so. much the better. A.s a matter of fact, it is well to observe here that you should avoid specimens which have been inbred for exhibition purposes. It's a full eug basket you are after and not a challenge cup. Exhibition poultry farming can be dealt with later on. It is only with some of the old and well established breeds that you can combine utility with exhibition points. But we must got bick to our subject. After you have carefully selected your breeding stock, allow them to occupy the pens for a week or two before finally mating up. During that time they should be fed Judiciously so as to avoid over-fatness. What you want in a layiag hen is flesh and bone and not internal fat Therefore foods which tend to produce this should be avoided, except in very severe weather when they may have an occasional feed of maize, say about twice a week. Green food and grit should be supplied in abundance. Carefully observe also that a constant supply of clean fresh water is always to hand. If you cannot so arrange your pens to have a rustling supply, then renew the water vessel at least twice a day.</p>
        <p>NUMBER OF BIRDS IN A PEN. Now this always depends on the variety you intend mating. If they are of tha Asiatic breeds, Eitch as Brahmav, Cochins, Langsbans and the like,then five hens to one male bird will be plenty, but if on the other hand if you intend breeding from the Mediterranean or non-sitting varieties, such a Legborns' Minorcas, Campines, Anemias, &amp;c. then you could safely run seven or even nine females to one male. But if you want to be certain of vigcroa progeny then I would not advise ycu to exceed these numbers.</p>
        <p>RESERVE MALES. It is always a good plan to have a reserve male bird at hand. One never know what's to happen. and if through sickness, or it may be accident, you should lose the head of the harem, then ycur operations are temporarily stunted. Then again you have always to remember that when we are well on into the hatching season it is not always easy to obtain reliable st ck birds. Still further, a reserve bird is useful for the purpose of resting the original one. When von have run the pen for five or six weeks it is a good plan to rest the stock hens for a week, then you may use the, reserve bird with profit. All these hints carefully' observed tend to assist you in having a success— ful hatching season, and I am certain that/ if farmers would only go to the trouble - of so mating a few pens, they would have. less to say about their hens not paying. Next week I shall give you a list of what breeds to mate up, both. for egg-production and table purposes. Meanwhile. get your pens in readh.eis t ) commence serious; operations. The snow has come and almost gone and we may now look forward to more congenial weather. No time should therefore be lost in making every necessary preparation for breeding operations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OUR ILLUSTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>OUR ILLUSTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>For the first time in the history of our p ou'try column I am able to give you a weekly illustration of one of the many different breeds of poultry. This week's illustration is that of a Redcap cock. May I add also that I purpose giving a description of each breed illustrated.</p>
        <p>Prom Hz TIM REDCAP FOWL</p>
        <p>Worll.</p>
        <p>The Redcap is specially adapted for a free range. The birds certainly do better on such than in a con- fined area, and when they can be so accommodated they give an excellent return in eggs. A very special point in favour of the Redcap is their wonderful endurance as layers. It is quite commonly understood amongst breeders of Redcaps that the hens lay better than pullets. A friend of mine, who has been a breeder of this variety for 40 years, tel's me that they are wonderful layers even when well advanced in age; many of his stock are well over:two and three years old. Then, again, the Redcap is a handsome fowl, much more so than many of the new varieties. It breeds very true to colour and markings. To those who have a free and well-wooded range I know of no more useful breed for egg-production than the Redcap.</p>
        <p>1.U91, 4...iy. jo` itir iik.,,!•44;`,Jii,r,:. ligA.,:t•l';,, t`tilifilVl; ',',,, •, '''(,i''''.(l.l:(,:.l;l;/::;.:::,,;::;,•::,.;:i...;,...:‘,,,,\‘t\.\\.0'---;‘,', '. •••,.:..,‘'.'-'!•:•.. ),il4\\,', s .'. s ':';:',.•!..',,--i I 4., \P.. , . •,, - , , , i] --&gt;------ .„..---:----,.--_- -,- . ... ..- ...- ..---- , e Feathered</p>
