      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>now 10 AVOID FAILURE.</head>
        <p>now 10 AVOID FAILURE.</p>
        <p>A visit to almost any farmyard will prove at once the prevailing lack of system in mating and breeding operations. There you can see fowls of all ages and breeds mixed together to breed at will. And it is because of such indifference that 90 per cent. of the failures in the culture of poultry cin be traced. There is no just cause to believe, because the poulti y section of farming has failed in the past, that it cannot be made more proatable in the future. My desire is that these remarks shall apply locally, for this reason— nearly all our local farmers ignore the poultry section of their farming pursuits. A farmer said to me not long ago that he could not tell how it was his hens did not pay. In further explanation he remarked : "I generally get about three pure bred male birds and run them with the stock. And what is morel pay is. Gd. a piece for them, and yet I don't seem to get any better results." Dear me ! I wonder who ever heard of a farmer breeding a Derby winner by such haphazard methods of mating. It is jolly hard lines expecting these high-prieed pure-bred male birds to revolu- tionise (in one season) the idle methods of years. It is therefore because of this prevalent indifference to proper methods and systems, that I propose dealing with the subject of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MATING THE STOCK.</head>
        <p>MATING THE STOCK.</p>
        <p>First let ma remind you that in doing so, I intend dealing only with the utility side of poultry farming. Generally speaking most breeding pens are mated next month so these remarks will be quite seasonable and of good service at this juncture. The male birds intended to head the pens should be of a very vigorous nature so that if they have been having a free range during the past, they should at once be isolated from the rest of the stock, and fed liberally on good sound food. They ought to be given a jest of at least three weeks before being put down in the breeding pen. When selecting a male bird for breeding purposes you should carefully note the following points. The male bird should never be less than one year old. If mated to pullets thou a two-year-old cock is this best. See that he is thoroughly healthy. And bred</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</head>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>NEW LAID EGGS, direct from the farms, l -LI per 120, 260 'box, carriage paid. Butter Is. 3d. I wholesale onIy.—FARMER, 67, Albert rd.,Morecambe.</p>
        <p>IZXHIBITION and Utility White Wyandottes • bit'° 12.4 from les. 6d. each ; eggs 215., les. Gd. and is. Ga• per dozen ; unfertiles replaced U returned.—Tog Merrilock's Poultry Farm, 13:unidel5ands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
