        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January, 27, 1912. BURNS' ANNIVERSARY. I</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows:— For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. U. T. P. G. T. P. c'nt'ge Huddersfrld22 19 2 1...82 138 578... 27 31 147 8863 Wigan 92 19 3 0... 62 78 358... 22 21 107 86•3 G Hunslet....-22 15 6 1... 96 80 312... 36 35 177 7045 Oldham 22 15 6 1... 62 84 376... 37 96 212 70.45 Hull K. R2O 14 6 0... 61 GI 311... 26 36 160 70.00 St. Helens 21 14 7 0... 47 72 310... 31 36 176 6666 Halifax 21 13 6 2... 40 64 272... 20 36 148 6666 Wakefield T2l 12 8 1... 40 55 245._ 41 57 259 5952 Batley 2l 12 9 0... 36 41 195... 21 29 126 5714 Broughton-22 12 9 1... 30 41 183... 28 32 152 56.81 Hull 25 13 10 2... 46 74 314... 31 49 209 5600 Widnes 2O 10 8 2... 30 37 171... 18 31 129 5500 Dewabury 21 11 9 1... 46 GO 272... 36 47 213 51.76 Rochdale H. 21 31 10 0... 39 45 213... 33 57 237 52.38 Leigh 93 11 11 1... 40 48 224... 33 44 208 5000 Warrington.22 10 11 1... 25 46 188... 38 46 214 4772 •Keighley -.18 8 9 1... 33 43 195... 23 59 233 47-22 Leeds 22 10 12 0... 38 61 268... 36 53 246 45.45 Swinton York l9 8 11 0... 27 34 156._ 46 51 245 4210 Salford 21 7 11 3... 27 36 162.- 37 39 191 40.47 Harrow l9 6 12 1... 19 33 137... 47 57 265 34.21 Runcorn 2O 514 1... 20 2$ 124... 38 48 220 27.50 Coventry -21 4 16 2... 27 26 132... 51 96 390 2272 . Ebbw Vale 19 1 15 31- 14 16 76... 54 81 851 13.15 1B amley l9 017 2... 14 16 76... 68115 491 5.26 Bradford 2l 120 0... 19 21 107... 63 97 417 4'76</p>
        <p>1.1 For Weak Lungs 1 661 am very liable to Colds." Devonshire. Dear Sirs,—l should like you to know the great benefit I have derived from 0 Angier's Emulsion. I have a delicate chest; and, having to live in a damp climate, I am very liable to catch colds, which leave me with a troublesome cough. Your Emulsion never fails to rid me of the cough, for which lam very thankful, as it is the only preparation of the kind I can digest. My husband, who is a clergyman, also uses it, and praises it highly, as he finds it very soothing to the throat. (Signed)—(Mrs.) E. I. LANE. 4 , ly 1,11 , . I e .-`;'‘ 13 ' uisl • NGIER9 ~,,,, : _ Angier's Emulsion is a pleasant, ;It": remedy that can be taken alike by young and old with perfect safety and with certainty of good results. Soothing • to throat, lungs, stomach and intestines, an aid to appetite and digestion, and an 6' I invigorating tonic and restorative, it is unequalled for lung affections, digestive 0 - disorders and wasting diseases. Anr,ier's Emulsion is endorsed by the medical i profession and used in the hospitals. Of Chemists, 1/I±, 219 and 416. , A FREE SAMPLE II .. 0 sent on receipt of 3d. postage. Mention this paper. • LTHE ANGIER CHEMICAL CO., Ltd., 83 Clerkenwell Road, London, E.G. 04111/111101/11N10011/11/1111/111100•111111MINSIIMMINIIIMINIMO</p>
        <p>,N/IN/ANNImi</p>
        <p>holding a large-sized candle to his fellow-sportsman of the rod and line. At present, however, the latter is believed, the world over, to have the pre-eminence. For instance, ex-President Roosevelt tells this : " In Africa I knew two farmers whose farms lay side, by side. A river ran through both properties aml it was full of fish. One day these two farmers fell into conversation. CC How is it,' the first asked. that we always catch bigger fish in our part]of the river than you do in yours?' " Well,' replied the second farmer, " I 'guess it's because you're bigger liars up there.' " To be serious, the sport under considera- tion at the Gladstone Arms, when our local walking- men anglers elected their officers for the current year, has much to commend it. That is flirly obvious to many. The Bishop of Manchester is among the multi- tude enamoured of it. Like many anglers, he reflects, and some of his reflactions have appeared in The Treasury." The unsuccessful fisherman (he remarks) can at all events console himself with the thought that he is one of the few men who, in a bt.y.y restless age, find time for meditation. The fisherman who has nothing to write about has plenty to think about. He has cherished memories of some of the most lovely sights and sounds that this earth affords ; if he loves his craft he is never dull, never so much disappointed as to be cured of his passim. He never sees a running river or lake with- out a sense of enchantment to which the rest of the world are strangers. When be fails he has not wasted his day, and can always find excellent reason to prove that the failure was not due t 3 his lack of skill ; while, if he succeeds, and when he succeeds, there is no limit to the credit which he can take to himself, or to the stories which he can narrate, provided only that heihas a congenial audience. A NATURAL TRANSITION.</p>
        <p>! Without effort, of cours,+, one pases from angling ito oysters. The story in last week's " Notes and 'Notions," of the sixth successive individual t 3 make an unsuccessful attempt to swallow an abnormal oyster brought to my mind two or three that run on similar lines; but when it COM 39 to reducing these to cold print, my otherwise willing hand is restrained by fear lest they should prove rather too full-flavoured for the more sensitive section of my readers. Apropos of the item suggesting these is the following from a newspaper of 100 years ago:—" A blacksmith at Bury, undertook for a bet of two guineas, to swallow 1,000 oysters within an hour. He succeeded in getting down 650 in half the time, when a sharp fragment of the shell attached to one of the oysters lodged in his throat, and he fell down senseless. A_ neighbouring surgeon spsedily gave him relief, but neither time nor inclination would admit of his resuming the undertaking." Several similar stories have been " resurrected" of late, and should any Oliver Twist among my Warrington friends call fur more, I will multiply instances. CLERGY AND MATRIMONY.</p>
        <p>I claim to rank among diligent readers of "Women's Chat," and the opening paragraph lf last week's con- tribution under that lnading paiticularly interested me. In a bit of parochial history which I compiled for a private patron and which had to be submitted to his vicar for revision, I strongly depre- cated the growing tendency to celibacy among clergy of the Church of England ; but the reverend critic objected to my dogmatism, holding that abstention from marriage might be good and wise or undesirable according to varying coaditions of work. About the same time, a clerical correspondent went still further in relation to the point. He said that, though marriage and large families are generally associated with the clergy of the Church of England, yet a very slight knowledge of her chequered history would show that her course has been chiefly shaped by unmarried men. Lancelot Andrews was unmarried, and f2O was Arch- bishop Laud. It was Ken who wrote :—"The virgin state, the altar best attends; this state the Lord cora- mends not, but commends." In the years that followed Ken's death, only one man made an impres- sion on the Church by his writings, and that was Lacy the non-juror. Bishop Butler's wiitings helped religion in general rather than the Church of Englani. Mawr think that the Church owed a vast debt to John Wesley, who quickeLed her with now life, and John Wesley, though not unmarried, yet led so unhappy a domestic life at home that, to all intents and purposes, be enjoyed the freedom of an unmarried man. During the last century, of course, Newman was unmarried, and so was Liddon,to who= the Church owes (whether for better or worse may be a question) the retention of the Athanasian Creed. It is true that Pusey was married,but it is known that he had grave doubts as to the wisdom of it. I may just I add the reminder that many of the leading positions in the Church of England are in the hands of bachelors. Not only are several of the Bishops' Palaces without a lady, but the Archbishop of York, whom I knew as a curate, is still unmarried and bids fair to remain so. Moreover (to return to my clerical authority), the best preacher among the Canons of York is a bachelor. The most prominent members of York Convocation, and the best known clergyman in Yorkshire, is a bachelor. The majority of those advertising for curacies in any week's " Guardian " evidently think it is a feather in their cap that they are single. Tne groat crux of the present day is how to get hold of the young men, and f r this purpose unmarried curates and vicars without home tier are considered the best. In one Episcopal Palace it is said the lowest places have to be taken by the married and moustachiod clergy ; above them it the shaven and married ones ; but the choice seats, near the bishop himself, are reserved for the shaven and celibate. Doubtless this is said by way of joke, but even a caricature gives some idea of the real state of things, and the above is not without a substratum of truth.</p>
        <p>„,•• OOTITALL 7star„„ arta it , 1-11,1tTI • t• C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's snow and slush resulted in a serious curtailment of the Rugby programme, and in very few districts was there any contest for spectators to enthuse over. The Warrington match with Salford was abandoned, and efforts are being made to re-arrange the fixture for Easter Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CUP DRAW.</head>
        <p>THE CUP DRAW.</p>
        <p>Put, for the moment, interest in League games pales be:ore the luminous attractions of the draw for Round 1 of the Northern Union Cup, which took place on Tuesday, and is as follows :</p>
        <p>IN YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Huddersfield v. Swinton. Bradford v. Rochdale Hornets. Normanton St. John's or Featherstone v. Warrington Batley v. Runcorn. Beverley v. Bull Kingston Rovers. Dewsbury v Lane Ends United. Halifax v Ebbw Vale. Hull v. York. Wakefield Trinity v. Leeds. IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Bramley. Widnes v. Salford. Wigan v. Wigan Ilighfteld or Asbton-under-Lyne. Barrow v. St. Helens. Leigh v. Hunslet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>OTHER 'I lES.</head>
        <p>OTHER 'I lES.</p>
        <p>llom or Fothergill v. Keighley. Coventry v. Oldham. Ties to be played on February 17th, the club first named having choice of ground. Warrington partisans have awaited the result of the draw with more than usual anxiety. It was an admitted fact that unless the draw was kind to the Wirepullers their interest in the great competition would soon be that of mere lookers-on. In plain language they needed to be drawn at home or against some very modest opposition to have any hope of reporting progress. Fortune has favoured them, and anxiety is allayed for the present. Warrington are drawn to meet either Normanton St. John's or Featherstone, on the ground of the winner of this junior tie. In all probability, Warrington will arrange for the opposing side td viAt Wilderspool and thus share a decent gate.</p>
        <p>The two teams whose fate Warrington are concerned with are both Yorkshire juniors and may be relied upon to give Warrington a hearty gime without causing any nervousness in the home camp. Entry into Round 2 should be comfortably assured, and with another home draw Warrington will begin to ltok up. The prospects of the Wirepullera are never to be despised when they have once got " under way " in the cup ties. Past history has proved their prowess as cup-fighters and I am not going t admit, yet awhile, that they are entirely a spent force.</p>
        <p>Nevertheless one hopes desperately, that before the time for registration expircs on February Ist, the Warrington executive will succeed in enrolling a player or two who will strengthen the weaker places in the team. Frawley's failure to carry out his agree- ment makes the position worse than was anticipated, for the services of the speedy Colonial would have been of inestimable advantage in the Cup-ties. A really class back, or another robust forward, would make considerable difference to the Warrington team. Cannot such a player be got for " love or money ?"</p>
        <p>Warrington's opponents have some disputes to settle before they are actually sorted out. At a meet- ing of the Northern Union Cup Committee on Tllo3- day in Manchester, a protest lodged by the Feather- 1 stone club against the result of their cup-tie with Normanton St. John's was heard. The chief ground of complaint was that the official referee did not attend, and that it was impossible for the officials to f _Dow the game. As the referee was unable to appear at the meeting, and in view of the importance of his evidence, it was resolved to postpone the further hearing of the case to Tuesday next to give the referee the opportunity to be present. In connection with the cup-tie, David Holmes, of Featherstone, was repotted for misconduct, and was severely cautioned.</p>
        <p>Taking the draw as a whole it is rather tame. There are a few games of outstanding interest, but in nearly all the successors to the second round can be predicted pretty confidently. In Lancashire the leading matches are Leigh v. Hunslet—a very open affair—and Widnes v. Salford, with slight odds on the Chemicals. Wigan and Oldham both have opponents whom they can afford to treat comparatively lightly. Round 2 looks like furnishing some stiff propositions, for all the leading clubs will be in opposition.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union League team to meet the Australasians at Central Park. Wigan, on Wednesday next, was selected at Manchester on Tuesday evening as follows:</p>
        <p>E Clarkson (Hull). S Moorhouse (Huddersfield), H Wagstaff (Hudders- field), W Batten (Hunslet), and A. Jenkineon (Hunslet). F Smith (Hunslet), and J R hilton (Halifax). J Winstanley (Wigan), F Harrison (Leeds), T Woods (Rochdale Hornets), J L Clampitt (Broughton Rangers), D °roam (Huddersfield) and D Clark (Huddersfield). Reserves: E Davies (Oldham), and A. Johnson (Widnes). Referee: R Robinson (Bradford).</p>
        <p>It is a bit of a novelty to find all the English bacts in Yorkshire clubs. The days of Lancashire' superiority in three-quarter play have suffered an eclipse which I hope is only temporary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>VISIT OF WIDNES'.</head>
        <p>THE VISIT OF WIDNES'.</p>
        <p>To-day's eng:gement at Wilderspool should do something towards replenishing the Club exchequer. In recent years Widnes have provided one of the best games of the season at the Warrington headquarters, and everything points to another great struggle. A year ago the Chemicals made a draw here by a sensational try in the last five minutes, and on current form they are capable of repeating the performance. Warrington certainly ought to win, however, for Widnes are hardly as strong as when they defeated the Wirepullers at Lowerhousedane earlier in the season. Warrington will have the following strong side in the field.</p>
        <p>Mclntyre, full back ; Bradshaw, Dickenson, Renwick, and Woodburn, three-quarters ; Creevey and Brooks, half backs; Thomas, Shugars, Chesters, Skelhorn, Fearnley and Tranter, forwards.</p>
        <p>The " A" team to play at Widnes will be: Brown full back; Waywell, Smith, Vernon, and Clare, three- quarters; Daintith and Jordan, half backs; Harmer, P. McCabe, P. Boardman, Bradshaw, Arnold and Critcbley, forwards.</p>
        <p>Watrington had two international players last Sattitrday, when Thomas and Shugars filled vacancies in the Welsh pack at Oldham against England. The Welshmen received a heavy beating, but the Warrington men were among the best in a pack of forwards too light for the job. Thomas dropped a penalty goal, a feat he performed on the same ground a week before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MATCHES POSTPONED.</head>
        <p>MATCHES POSTPONED.</p>
        <p>Owing to the conditions prevalent on Saturday, the majority of the League fixtures were unable to be played, many of the grounds being under water. The game at Ebbw Vale against Hull Kingston Rovers was declared off as early as Thursday, and the Bramley v. Bradford Northern on Friday. The others postponed were :—Warrington v Salford, Widnrs v Oldham, Huddersfield v Ilunslet, Keighley v Runcorn, Dewsbury V St. Helens and York v Halifax.</p>
      </div>
