      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs end Colds. A proven efficacy; 9d. Zt Is. lid. Of Chemistsonly.-A dvt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>INTERESTING LOCAL CELEBRATIONS.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING LOCAL CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>" Miler Scots " and " freens " assembled in goodly and representative force at the Lion Hotel, Warrington, on Thursday evening, to celebrate the anniversary of the birth of Robert Burns. Mr. J. Fulton Smith presided amongst those present were :—The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Alderman Forshaw), Mr. J. Rymer Young, Mr. A. Bone Glasgow,) the Rev. G. Erskine Nicol Manchester) , tLe Rev. W. Mid (Warring- ton), Dr. Naden and Dr. Manscn. The occasion was marked by the customary social observances, and the proceedings altogether were of a most enjoyable character.. After a national repast in which haggis was presented with the Burns quotation. "Fair fa' your honest sonsie fece , Great chieftain o' the puddin' race." the company enjoyed a musical programme which embraced a number of Scottish songs.interpreted by Mr J McArthur and Mr J A Wescott, both of whom were in fine voice. By the kind permission of the management of the Hippodrome Miss Florence Esdaile vocalist and Mr S W Wyndham, comedian, gave songs which added to the evening's entertain- ment. Mr H M Ashton and Dr Law gave recitals in their own inimitable styles. The duties of ace;m- panist were capably undertaken by Mr. Ton Taylor. . The loyal toasts having been duly honoured, The Rev. G. Erskine Nicol in proposing the toast of "The Immortal Memory of Burns" said he deemed it no small distinction, to have been allowed to share in the hospitality of the Warrington Association, which bore suc'a an honoured name, and had done such brotherly work of helpfulness in Warrington. He had no particu'ar qualification to speak to them on this very great subject, only that be had felt the laughter creep up his old cheeks, and the sob gather in his throat as he read the fruits of Burns' genius. He had a heart which had been warmed by his bongs and invigorated by the music of his words. He had tasted the rich red wine which flowed from the big heart which Burns proclaimed in " 'ram 0' Ranter. He was rather disappointed to notice the absence of the ladies at that gathering, but he was quite sure their absence was not due to any harshness amongst Scotchmen in Warrington. There was a danger of allowing their great old language Ito lapse into oblivion. The wonder of their language was, that he did not know any other language in which a long discussion could be carried on without ever using a consonant. Scotsmen that night, all the world over, were addressing themselves to that toast. It Was the anniversary of that day on which within the " auld clay biggin "near "the banks and braes o' BonnieDoon" their peasant poet was born. Burns was born in a tempest, which threatened to destroy the " auld clay biggin," and mother and child had to be removed to a safer place in another cottage. That natal storm never seemed to abate. It followed him all through his too short life. Adverse winds blew fiercely on him all the time yet like the Alpine flower he increased in grace and beauty mid all the hail and storm. Finally, however, that tempest succeeded in beating him down in the humble lodging in Dumfries at the early age of thirty-seven. Until his death, his life was a struggle and a failure, but ever since it had been a triumph and a marvellous success. They fondly called him our Scottish Bard but was he not infinitely more.? Our little country gave him birth, and poverty, and a neglected deathbed. But Burns made Scotland, the wicket gate through which he passed to win a universal empire for his genius. He began as a local poet but very soon transcended all narrow limits and his splendid genius made the local universal. Wherever Scotsmen were that night they could close their eyes and think of the lovely moral embedded in the humorous story:</p>
        <p>" Pleasures are like poppies spread You seize the flower the bloom is shed, Or, like the snowflake in the river A moment white, then gone for ever." By his humble birth and life of toil, Burns refuted the idea that labour debased the mind and enchained the aspirations of tian.flis own struggles ' taught him veneration for genuine manhood. He was a man enormously in advance of his time. Before the French Revolution he had preached the prieciples of liberty, equality and fraternity. He taught that if the labouring classes would but respect themselves and be industrious and thoughtful they might rise to undreamt of social progress and social powers and make it an anachronism for the people to be misgoverned. As a minister of religion he claimed Burns as a prophet. What was the condition of Europe at the time of his birth ? Sunk in Atheism, flooded with the shallow irreligion of Voltaire, and shaken by the scepticism of our own Hume. Against this unholy flood Burns rose and drove the deluge back before the burning passion of his soege and poems. Burns was a man of unbounded sympathy and passionate love. Few could deny the soft impeachment that love made poets of us till,but they needed a Burns to exprass the feelings for them. He had pity for the mountain daisy, the timorous mouse, the silly sheep, and unlike many parsons he had pity for the de'il himself. They gloried in Burns because he inspired them with the love of nature and of country, the love of liberty, and the love of humanity. They gloried in him because he had sung their nation into glory. They loved him for he loved the people and protested against their wrongs, sung their sorrows and their joys, sympathised with them in their toils, and strove for their elevation. They ' celebrated not a dead hero but the presence of a living genius. Let them show their raverenze by drinking to his immortal memory in solemn silence. Dr. Melesorf, in submitting the toast of " Our Guests," said it waS peculiarly appropriate that all nationalities of this island should assemble to celebrate the birth of Burns, because he was not only a national poet but a universal poet, who uttered in the Scottish tongue the deepest stirrings of the human heart. On such occasions they were particularly pleased to hold out the hand of gcod fellowship to their English friends. It was a standing joke told more or less seriously) that Scotchmen as a race were mean, stingy, close and grasping. Of course what was really meant was that Scotsmen were prudent, careful, thrifty and enterprising. Another charge against the Scotch ens that they were a clannish race. Personally he hal not found it to be the case. Perhaps the only instance of that clannish spirit was shown on the Burns night and St. Andrew's Day celebrations, when it was intended to keep alive the national sentiment. He had great pleasure in proposing the prosperity of the guests and coupling with it the name of Mr. Bone, who had achieved considerable distinction in the literary world. Replying to the Xoact,l slr. BONS said he had bad many experiences in different parts of the world but he had never had the experience of replying for the guests in what he might call a "foreign "country. He had been to many Burns' celebrations, but had never attended one in England. Burns sometimes was a guest himself but he did not always respond very happily. In several parts of Northern Ayrshire there were instances in which he expressed himself dissatisfied with the hospi- tality. When he went as a guest to several of the big houses in Edinburgh, it was there he lowered himself from the high pinnacle of fame. The drinking habits of the time were then abnormal and when considering Burns' weaanesses they must remember the times in which he lived. On behalf of the guests he returned sincere thanks for their very kind hospitality. The toast of " The Caledonian Association" was in the hands of Mr. J. RYMER YOGNG, who made a delight- fully humorous speech. He expressed surprise that the president or some other responsible person,was so indis- creet in departing from tho mum' Scotch caution as to allow him to take charge of a toast of that kind. He would, however, like to explain that he was not so completely a " Sassenach " as his name would iraply. He was not going to say that Scotsmen _could notrnherit the Kingdom of Heaven. When the time came for him to shuffle off this mortal coil and take passage to the great Nether regions, and he cared to look up some or his Scotch friends he thought he would save time by not beginning with heaven first. (Laughter). Touching upon the works of Burns (Mr. Young) said he honestly believed there was nothing in the English language to surpass the magnificent sentiment in the lines of Tam-o'-Shanter. It was in the lighter work of Burns that he had the wonderful knack and capacity, for coming down from his high pedestal to invest common circumstances with the pathos, glitter and charm of his manner. They knew more of Burns than they imagined. Day by day they used sentences and quota- tions,yet they were ignorant as to their origin. After participating in the hospitality of many Scotch functions he thought tie was right in concluding that haggis was a vital poison. It had been his business to try and secure on the list of statutory government poisons, the inclusion of haggis along with arsenic and prussic acid. (Laughter). In conclusion, Mr. Young expressed the hope that the Warrington Caledonian Association would always be a prosperous and flourishing organisation. He coupled with it the name of their esteemed Mayor, Dr. G. W. Joseph. (Applause). The MaYoR in response said with regard to his Scottish association s he was educated as a boy in Scotland and he claimed amongst his acquaintances an old Scotch gentleman who was an intimate friend of Burn's in the days of hie youth. Scotchmen through- out the world had made a tame, and this was duo in some way to the nature of their country. It was a well known fact that the people of countries with small natural advantages were as a rule quick witted. It might be that they emigrated but. be could not help thinking at a 11 events that a great eeal of their prosperity, energyand enterprise,was due to the fact that they were very quick witted especi ally where finance was concerned. The toast of " The Lasses "was proposed in felicitous phrases by Mr. G. 'LW. STF.EL who enumerated a few of the virtues of the gentler sex. He remarked it was impossible for human intelligence to appreciate what the lasses did for mankind.He hoped tbey would always cherish their women folk and give them their support and encouragement. The duty of responding to the toast fell to the lot of Mr. W. C. RCESELL who expressed the hope that an innovation might be made next year so as to include the ladies in the invitation.</p>
        <p>The toast of the President (Mr. J. Fulton Smith) was entrusted to Mr. A. C. ROBERTSON and the President suitably replied. The proceedings teraainated with the singing of a Auld Lang Syne."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"JOURNALISTS AND PUBLIC SPEAKERS."</p>
        <p>Let me at the outset explain that the in last week's comments under this beading beginning "I had myself inferred many things" should have read "I have myself suffered many things." Uncorrected, the compositor's little slip would appear to convict me of inconsistency; indeed, if I may paraphrase a certain Pauline deliverance, it would be to my professional brethren a stumblingblock, and to readers at large foolishness NO JELLY-FISH.</p>
        <p>Although many of his fellow-members of the Board of Guardians seem t 3 think the Rev. A. M. Mitchell a thorn in the flesh, I make bold to confess to a sneak- ing regard for that irrepressible gentleman. The fact is that on taking up an appointment in a strange town some 20 years ago, I found just such a doughty free lance—of the same name, too I—maintaining the like fearless attitude at the fortnightly meetings a a similar body. The whole of the 17 other guardians on that Board were bitterly opposed to this Mr. Mitchell, who, however (like the reverend one of that ilk) was not of the order of invertebrata. They had no patience to hear him speak ; they ridiculed, insulted and boycotted him ; but he continued his policy of uprooting scandals, attacking abuses, and exposing questionable proceed-. ings, and finally, to the admiration of the community and commendation of the Government Inspector for the distlict, triumphantly vindicated it at an official inquiry, and completely routed his 17 opponents. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell may, for aught I know, be often •. in the wrong, zeal outrunning discretion. That is a I point with which I am not at present concerned, as it may conceivably provoke difference; but surely unprejudiced outsiders throughout the Union will agree that a public representative with sufficient backbone to contend, in season and out of season, against well-nigh overwhelming odds, for what he believes to be right, is a valuable asset.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CORPORAL PUNISHMENT IN SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL PUNISHMENT IN SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's " Examiner " reported a dis- cussion on this subject by the Cheshire Education Committee, and on the very day of its publication an article of my own on the question " To flog or not to flog " appeared in a Yorkshire contemporary. Perhaps in the circumstances the following extract, therefore, may interest Fnme "The question is even now debatable—now when the voice of the extreme and sometimes mistaken humanitarian is heard in the land ; when only the headmaster of an elementary school may correct ' in the physical sense, and even he is hedged about with regulations and per forms it reluctantly with the fear of irate parents and a summons for assault before his eyes ; when the rank and file of the army of would-be rod-wielders have perforce ceased from troubling and the weary birch is apparently for ever at rest ; when, in a word, only the erring scions of our aristocracy attending the good old public schools may be whacked at discrelion and walloped with impunity. The time-honoured rule in question, there observed as of yore, but everywhere else discredited, and consequently fallen into disuetude, may or may not represent a vicious system that we do well to deprecate. Time was when I should have affirmed this and favoured its abolition with all my heart and soul. To-day lam not so sure that we have benefited by going to the opposite extreme. At all events, with all their harshness and what would nowadays be denounced as brutality, these old teachers—most of whom took the entire school unaided somehow succeeded in training some of the finest and foremost of the citizens whom we have from time to time delighted to honour. " " UNTO THIS LAST "—WHICH?</p>
        <p>Travelling on the L. and Y. Railway the other day, 1 fell in with a lively " commercial " who, along with various other remarkable experiences, solemnly assured me that at a certain station farther north he had read an official notice that "the last down train is cancelled from this date, but another has been sub- stituted for it" ! Assuming that my casual acquaint- ance was not drawing the long bow, or engaged in the modern equivalont for that once popular pastime known as pulling one's leg,—.Hibernia must look to her laurels. FISHING YARNS. The account of the general meet:ng of the Warring- ton Workingmen's Association (Northern Anglers' Centre), reminds me that the operation hinted at in the preceding paragraph is supposed to be in especial favour with anglers. I have some wonderful speci- men's mainly drawn from Mr. Carter Platt's inexhaustible stock through the medium of the "Yorkshire Weekly Post," to which paper he has for many years contributed a characteristic column. Ho has therein told how be once received by post this scurrillous libel from an irreverent scoffer; The fishing season is now on! Walk up, Ananias! Old fish stories re-decorated, fresh stuffing inserted, and turned out equal to new by an efficient angling editor kept on the premises ! A large selection of the latest and loudest patterns of pike yarns always in stock ! Special versions made to order in all the latest modes, with the best quality of trimmings, style and fit guaranteed. Apply to The Fish Shop, Weekly Post,' Leeds." Why should tall stories be supposed to be the peculiar prerogative of the angler? One of these days, when I have time and space at command, I intend to make it my business to show that the golf enthusiast is, in this respect, capable of</p>
      </div>
