      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>Some strange new' THE ENGLISH doctrines are being set MINER. forth with a great deal of fervour by Mr. HAROLD BEGBIE, the author of "Earthen- ware," a book which has shed a good deal of light on what GENERAL BOOTH has named "the t•ubmerged tenth." Mr. BEGBIE is in the disturbed colliery district of South Wales, and, so far as we can make out his meaning, he is of opinion that the miners are deserting the old leaders for the new because they are becoming less religious, and are inclined to follow those who "preach Socialism cf 8' rather wild and godless character." We are not inclined to dispute a point like that with the man on the spot, though it will probably be disputed with much heat by those who have been in Wales for a longer time than Mr. HAROLD BEGBIE, and who will decline to admit either that Wales is losing its religious faith or that " the present discontents," to use BURKE'S famous phrase, would have been made either less or more by a difference of attitude towards religious beliefs. What we are concerned about for the moment is Mr. BEGBIE'S view Of the moral influence exercised by Englishmen on the Welsh miner. Here it is the turn of the Englishman to protest. The discovery of steam coal, says Mr. BEGBIE, took all the world to Cardiff—which signifies, we presume, that a urge number of miners from other parts of the country were attracted to that place by the prospect of work. Up to that time the Welsh miner was " a primitive and gentle creature." He lived on pleasant terms with employers,who under- stood both his language and his nature. He worked long hours, "but gave himself to the religious life of the village." He was sensitive and emotional. On this idyllic state of things with the long hours and the low wages, the English coalminers came flooding in. And with what sad consequences! "Swiftly and violently that district of South Wales ceased to be Welsh and ceased to be happy." Every- one met by Mr. Begbie assures him that it is impossible to exaggerate the effect of the English immigration. The English collier who went to Wales was a rude barbarian. He had no love of music, of education, of home life, of religion. He took his dogs out for a walk instead of his children. He used "swear words "in great profusion. "He spent Sun- day not in hymn-singing, but in fighting and drinking." His "habit of filthy speech i 9 denounced by every single Welshman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>- ANTHRACITE STOVES.</p>
        <p>These Stoves burn all day Without attention and are r invaluable for Private Or Public</p>
        <p>Rooms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BEST AND MOST ARTISTIC STOVES, on Show at</head>
        <p>THE BEST AND MOST ARTISTIC STOVES, on Show at</p>
        <p>PENDLEBURYS FURHNOIUSSHEERS</p>
        <p>Near CENTRAL STATION. TEL 43.</p>
        <p>Write for descriptive Booklet. Anthracite washed Nuts kept in stock.</p>
        <p>have spoken to as one of the most serious causes of moral degeneration in the Welsh</p>
        <p>character," says Mr. BEGBIE. There is a lady who has assured him that Welsh Children learn "bad words" from the English miners. "They think. it manly and fine to swear like an Englishman," On the other hand he has met a Welshman who says, " There is a moment when nearly every man must swear or burst." Were there any of these moments, we wonder,before the English rill_hers went to Cardiff? If so, what did Welshmen of that time do under their Pressure? Here is one of those cases in which a great</p>
        <p>- - - deal too much is attempted. As BURKE said, YOU cannot bring an indictment against a Whole nation. And you cannot with justice and in common sense, bring a general-Con- demnatory charge against a whole class. It w • be worth Mr. HAROLD BEGBIE'S While to inquire where the English Miners who flooded Cardiff came from. When the demand arose for addi- tional men to work Welsh coal there was also a growing demand for miners in the English coal centres, and it has continued from that day to this. In Northumberland and Durham, for instance, the number of Miners has something like doubled in thirty Years. It is much the same in Lancashire -and the Midlands. What seems to be assumed nevertheless, is that the miners who flooded 'South Wales—" inundation" is the word , actually employed—came from the older coal-fields bringing their "swear words" along with them. This involves the further I *-a,ssumption that the general condition of the -r-,nglish miner is one of great moral depravity We take the liberty of giving this an instant denial. There is a good deal of swearing, no 'doubt, among English miners, as there is in all classes of English society; but if Mr BEGBIE will look up the evidences on the subject he will find that the general tendency</p>
        <p>aril°ng the mining community is towards careful and sincere religious observance. The //liners maintain their own little chapels ; they an to a large extent their own preachers, 'SundaY school teachers, class leaders. If there is an accident in a mine, and men are imprisoned until they starve to death, as at Hartley many years ago, the tokens left 'behind indicate that much of the time spent in the dread waiting for death has been iu singing and prayer. When the miners search among their own numbers for men to Place in honourable positions they almos always choose the religious. Most of the ruiners' leaders now in Parliament have been local preachers. These are some of the reasons why we protest against the attempt to glorify the Welsh miner as he was at the 'exPense of the English miner as he is.</p>
        <p>The people of War- THE MAXORESS. rington are vastly interested and pleased .V 7 t h the novelty of having their Mayoress arried in their midst. The circumstance ,18 ;unique in the history of the borough, °. would, alone, make the municipal year of 1912 a memorable one. But there is the rmore remarkable fact that His Worship e Mayor is also to be married this year T? that Dr. Joseph and his charming daughter all fair to establish a record which is not at I/. likely to be equalled at any future date. iss Joseph is to be escorted to the altar on Tuesday, and yesterday the Members of the Council and the r eials collaborated in the happy little 'Unction of making the Mayoress a handsome Present. The speeches were as felicitous as they usually are at wedding presentations and expressed the very warm feeling of egard in which the youthful Mayoress is Alderman Bolton struck a character- istically unusual note, and at the same time I:raised a hearty laugh, by warning Miss VsePh that her troubles were just beginning. hone and feel sure that the Alderman 'will Prove a false prophet.</p>
        <p>--- It is a hopeful sign of •rMAGISTRATES AND the times that magis- -LICENSING REFORM terial Benches in great centres of popula- tion are showing a disposition to press for such sweeping reforms in the licensing elrstern as were approved by the Warrington justices at their meeting last week. The rePort in our last issue was open to some luisconception, as the magistrates have no ,P,°Wer, under the law as it stands, to make the drastic alterations referred to. Before the -The recommendations as to early closing, regulation of clubs and the readjustment of licence duty can take effect, the Govern- Ylent will have to intervene with new legis- lation. Briefly stated the position is follows During the strike riots ..astrate summer the Liverpool magis- Put into force special emergency rwers and considerably curtailed the hours nring which the public houses in the city Were allowed to be open. The effect was salutary, the cases of drunkenness immedi- ately showing a remarkable decline while the small shopkeepers in the poorer districts VPerienced a spell of unusually good _Tlsiness. So convinced were the Liverpool JUstices of the beneficial results of their 9ktion, that they decided to use their 111fluence to bring about a permanent reduc- ,ir of hours throughout the country. There is a general expectation that the Government will make another effort— „this time unmolested by the House of -Lords—to tackle the licensing problem, and the Liverpool magistrates have sent forward a recommendation that the</p>
        <p>reforms which their unique experience suggests should be included in the measure. At the same time they have circularised the Benches in other places, with a view to obtaining support in their bold attitude. At their meeting last week the Warrington Bench considered the Liverpool recommenda- tions and it will be a source of satisfaction to local temperance reformers that six of the seven suggested changes were approved. The proposals to which the justices gave their assent were</p>
        <p>_ (1) Tllat the opening hour of licensed premises be altered from 6 a.m. to 8 30 a.m. (2) That no wonan, or person under 16, should be served with intoxicating liquor for consumption either " cn " or " off" the premises before 12 noon. (3) That the closing hour of licensed premises on week days he altered from 11 p.m. to 10 p.m. (5) That the hours of opening on Sundays be limited to three between noon and 10 p.m., but so that one of such hours be between 12 noon and 3 p.m. (6) That clubs be put on the same fooling as licensed premises as to the hours during which intoxicating liquor can be supplied, with similar power to the Licensing Authoi ity to grant special exemptions. (7) That the basis for the licence duty be the quantity of intoxicating liquor sold. The only recommendation which was not supported was (4) "That the Licensing Authority be empowered to make orders with regard to special premises and in given localities for closing either earlier or later than 10 p.m." The fact that bodies of magistrates are prepared to pledge them- selves thus far in the direction of licensing reform is welcome evidence of an advance of public opinion and should encourage the Government in its determination to persevere with legislation on thorough-going and comprehensive lines.</p>
        <p>A " whirlwind " cam- A WHIRLWIND paign " against Home CAMPAIGN. Rule took place on Monday and Tuesday, the most prominent feature being the speech- making of Sir Edward Carson and Mr. F. E. Smith at Liverpool. Other "Tory front- benchers" joined in the justification of the plot to prevent a Cabinet Minister from dis- cussing or explaining the Home Rule question in Belfast. It is perhaps significant that while Mr. Wyndham and Mr. Walter Long gave their sanction to this outrage upon one of the most vital elements of liberty—one of the most cherished, too, in Great Britain—the right of free speech, Mr. Austen Chamberlain had not a word to say about it. That such a discussion would be "insulting" or "provocative" is a suggestion so far-fetched as to be quite laughable. In the nature of things, Mr. Churchill and Mr. Redmond would desire to meet the appre- hensions, whether genuine or feigned, of Pro- testant Ulster, with an olive-branch rather than a blunderbuss, but the Orangeman's reply is that argument is an insult.</p>
        <p>The Tariff Reform,Tory THE DEAD PAST case, it must be remem- AND THE LIVING bered, is to a large FUTURE. ex t ent using the "Orange drum" only to drown the chorus of vociferation which it fears because it cannot give any direct reply to it. Sir Edward Grey, in his speech at North Sunderland on Saturday, dealt very trenchantly with some of those Tariff Reform and Tory fallacies which the Tory Party would like to cover by raising a dust over Home Rule. What of the prophecies about the much-abused Budget of 1909, the rejection of which pro- voked a constitutional crisis? That Budget has provided the money for increased expen- diture on Old Age Pensions and other 'reforms in a way that has made the pressure of taxation as little felt by the country as it is possible to be felt. But the Budget is not merely finding present revenue. Under this Budget and the Insurance Bill we have legis- lated not only for the present but for the future. Well, it is partly because the Tory Party feels that social reform has been, is, and will be a sphere in which the Liberal Party can oppose actual performance to deceptive promises that the Tories are trying to blind the eyes of the electorate by a dust kicked up from its prejudices and animosities</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Another capital programme has been submitted to the patrons of the Hippodrome during the week. A good bill is headed by "The Yellow Fang," presented by Miss Mary Mayfren's Company. The title suggests something Oriental, and in fact it is a true story of life in an opium den. Mr. George Pickett makes up well as Hung Kow, the keeper of the den, and ~..arries the part well. As his assistant, Sun Kee, Mr. Lawrence Osborne displays much talent. Miss Violet Campbell as Judith Beck, a slave to the dreamy opium, is a decided favourite, whilst Mr. Alfred Fisher, as a tool far Hung Kow's villainy, plays the part very convincingly. In the character of a drunken sport, Mr. Francis Annesley does himself great credit. The staging of the gruesome drama is perfect, and the acting fine. The story tells how the drunken sport is decoyed into the den and how, after a series of exciting incidents he is eventually rescued and the den raided by the police •, Mr. Walter Finney being a capital City constable. On the vaiiety side, Florence Esdails is a vocalist of no mean order, giving two songs in a highly pleasing style. As a comedian, S. W. Wyndham scores a success with an extremely funny song and a choice selection of jokes. Scharff and his assistant present an original idea "In the music store," in a clever and effective manner Sisters May are a success in their smart vocal and dancing turn. Jack Belton and Minnie Osborne add more variety with their Irish and Scotch tit-bits. The cinematograph pictures are excellent and the selec- tions by the band, under Mr. Widdop's skilful conductorship, afford great pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>There will be a good attraction at the Hippodrome next week when the ever popular pantomime "Babes in the Wood " - will be presented by Mr. Harry Elliston. A strong cast will icclude : Mabel Osborne, L ene May, Robb Wilton and August and September. There will also be specialities by Nance Grocutt, the singing Welbh girl ; the Four Minims, and Five Yorkshire Lasses. A full chorus and ballet I under the direction of Mr. Ernest Ramsey will also take part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>The Examiner Saturday 'January 27,1912;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>re"r‘O'NeW•ol.l"'l/4 . LNOT•ES AND NOTIONS., , "If the country were absolutely sober for twelve months, the police would have com- paratively little to do.—Mr. Justice Lush at the Law Courts. ,</p>
        <p>* * * The Corporation officials are greatly delighted at the forthcoming wedding of the Mayoress—they have been granted a whole day's holiday on Tuesday! *o* A Trades Disputes Bill, involving the principle of compulsory arbitration in industrial troubles, will be introduced at the meeting of the Debating Society on Tuesday evening by the Home Secretary (Mr. M. Howarth).</p>
        <p>t" r. 4 Free Traders have been as pleased as Tariff Reformers with the nature and contents of the Dumping Exhibition. The trashy character of the competing foreign goods, and the nervousness of the " stall-holders " at the advent of a determined questioner, are features which cannot be missed.</p>
        <p>* * If it has been nothing else, the exhibition has been a happy hunting ground for the ladies, but men with votes, and convictions to be shaken or cemented, have been very scarce. Not a few ardent spirits went in to be impressed and came away scoffing. The " dumper " is not such a fearsome person after all. * * Liberals noted with amusement the monopoly of the Union Jack by the promoters of the Dumping Exhibition. All the lady helpers wore aprons made out of the national flag, and even caps fashioned out of the same emblem. Whisper it quietly—but the aforesaid Union Jacks and aprons are under- stood to have been on tour with "the Dumperies." They certainly looked the worse for wear.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. C. E. Parkeri showed a praiseworthy interest in the exhibition by paying more than one visit. He was effusively welcomed on "the other side," and when the photo- grapher nearly " snapped " him on the platform in confabulation with Lord Wolmer the delight of the local patrons knew no bounds. Whether Mr. Parker was converted by all he saw and heard, an article from his pen nelct week will probably reveal. * * Another interesting personal visit was made by the Rev. W. Cooper and Mr. Harry New—two gentlemen who are not exactly persona grata with the local Tariff Reformers. Mr. New was honoured with an introduction to Lady Greenall and the resulting passage of arms was, it is said ,quite amiable though pointed. Unfortunately the official note-taker was not within hearing at the time. • * A telegram from Cannes says that in a foursome on the Cannes golf links, Mr. A. J. Balfour and Sir Edgar Vincent played a four- ball match against Sir Seymour Fortescue and Mr. A. H. Crosfield (captain of the Cannes Golf Club). When the fifteenth green was reached, Mr. Crosfield stood four up on his companions, and at this point it was found necessary to abandon the match, the last three greens being under water. *• * THE ANTI-HOME RULERS. FIRST SAGE: Yes I like Sir George he put it so fair—no stretching ! SECOND SAVANT: Aye SO do I. I don't care for them as is bigamists. Evidently the Anti-Home Rule meeting contained some very advanced moral philo- sophers. (This was a. conversation between two of the audience at Tuesday's lory meeting). • Mr. Lloyd George and Sir Rufus Isaacs returned to London on Saturday from their holiday in South France. Questioned as to his plans, the Chancellor replied, "Oh, I am now back for work, and shall be staying in London." He added that the weather in South France had been particularly bad, and the first sunshine he had seen since his absence was that which greeted him at Dover that day. * *</p>
        <p>In his will the late Mr Labouchere appointed three trustees, including Mr. Davis, who was present at his death, and Mr. McKenna, the Home Secretary. He has left a large yearly income to his daughter, the Marchesa di Rudini. Mr. Labouchere is said at Florence to have left a fortune of nearly £2,000,000, the residue of which, after provision has been made for the Marchesa di Rudini and certain other relatives, and legacies have been paid to several other persons, will be divided among his nephews, who will bear the name of Labouchere.</p>
        <p>* * * The report to be presented to the annual Conference of the Labour party at Birming- ham, in which gratification is expressed at the repeated manifestation of industrial unrest, states that the resolution of the Government to enrol special constables from people opposed to trade unions and classes prejudiced against workmen is an ominous sign for the future, for if in time of disputes the employers' supporters receive the badge of authority enabling them to create dis- turbances with immunity, and to attack workmen, the latter will be bound to retali- ate, and instead of riot and disorder being unusual and regrettable they will become a common and necessary feature of industrial life.</p>
        <p>0 At the " Old Boys" dinner at the Boteler Grammar School on Thursday, the Rev. Horace Gray added to our stock of school boy " howlers " by some choice gems brought to light in a recent "general knowledge" examination. One .question was " What do you understand by being below par ?" and one boy who must have had a very stern father answered " It means a taste of the stick." " R.5.V.P.," in the opinion of one young hopeful, indicated "Reply sharp, very pressing." "What do you mean by turning over 3. new leaf ?" called forth : To behave yourself better than you have been doing or as the third form has done during the term." The best specimen, however, was the reply to the query: "What would you do to a boy if he swallowed a match?" The first aid recommended by one genius took the follow- ing form: "I should give him some water (obviously with the intention of putting the fire out), I should then exray him. I should then cut him open, stitch him up and keep him quiet." * * 0 Any Protectionist will argue that it would be a good thing for all concerned if more and more of our food supplies were drawn from 'our Colonies., He will admit, further, that these food supplies have to be paid for by exports; and point ecstatically to the beau- tiful way in which Nature is prepared to keep the balance, encouraging on the one hand the food production of the outlying parts of the Empire, and making possible on the other an increase of manufactures in the Mother Country. But he will then go on to say that, while we must import food, there lis no need for us to import manufactured</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>"OLD BOYS."</head>
        <p>"OLD BOYS."</p>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL RE-UNION.</p>
        <p>"Old Boys" of the BotPler Grammar School met under convivial circumstances on Thursday evening to renew old associations, revive happy memories of school days and wish prosperity to the venerable institution which Las done so much for the educational life of the town. The occasion was the tenth annual dinner'promoted by the Old Boys' Uniqn and, like its predecessors, the function was characterised by a fine spirit of good fellowship and proved a gratifying success. Dinner was served in the large schoolroom, the Winmarleigh Cafe Company ca'ering in first class style. Councillor Henry Roberts presided and a numerous company included Aldermen J. Smethurst and A. Bennett, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), the Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster), Mr. C. S Burgess, Mr. W. E. Atkin, Mr. E. A. White, Dr. E: J. Fox, Mr. C. D. Parkinson, Mr. Segar Oven, Mr. J. Moore Murray (director of education), and Mr. J. H. Smethurst (bon secretary of the Old Boys' Union). Mr. Robert Pierpoint wrote from Cleveland-sconr e London :—I am very sorry that I shall not be able to be one of the "old boss" at the dinner on the 25th. I hope that all those who are present—my school fellows—young or old " old boys" may have a very pleasant evening, full of loyalty to the old school. Please remember me kindly to all who kindly remember me.</p>
        <p>Amongst others who sent letters of regret were the I Mayor, Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart. Revs E C E Carleton, Thomas Schonberg and J A Bart., Professor Conway, Mr A J Ashton, K.C.; Mr Roger C Parr, Mr Vere B Davies, Lieutenant-Colonel G Yates (Bolton), Mr J Fulton-Smith, Mr Rob( rt. Dobson, and Mc Charles Reynolds. 't he toast of "The King" was honoured on the proposition of the PRESIDENT. " THE ProSIS MEMORY OF SIR THOMAS BOTELER." Mr. CHARLES D. PARKINSON, in proposing this toast, gave some information about the Willcocks Memorial Fund. He said they had invested £226 3s. 4d., and there was an accumulation of income amounting to £22 13s. 3d. for which they had had no call. The Ispeaker paid a tribute to late Mr. John Geddes who, he I said, was as grand a specimen of a " grand old man," ' as they had in Warrington. The toast was honoured in silence. " PROSPERITY TO THE SCHOOL."</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT-::-1;0.1).-0;ed -"1-;;Z;p;iitTi -CO- the School." Referring to family associations with the school, he said he was proud to be able to bring his two sons there that night aud to have them classed with himself amongst the" old boys"of the Boteler Grammar School. He first became a scholar as far back as 1867 but, unfortunately, he only remained there for the short period of eighteen months. The school of to-day, he considered, was in every respect an enormous advance on the school of those old days. We were a commercial country ; we lived upon our trade. It was quite true that we had to have luxuries which were indulged in by professional men, but we should not do very much good with the professions if we had not the trade which kept the professions alive. There was a period in the history of this country, not many years ago, when our educational system was very largely at fault in regard to its commercial curriculum. At the time he referred to, he did not think the Grammar School was a source of considerable strength—as be believed it was to-day —to the commercial and industrial enterprises of the town and district. In that respect, at any rate, the school had, so far as physical conditions would permit, been brought up eery respect- ' ably indeed to the needs of the times. As the years passed they would find a far larger number of the boys who had been educated in tbe school taking responsible and important positions in respect of the industries around them arid kindred industries in other parts of the country. It would not be the fault of the governors or of the most excellent staff, if that side of the curriculum did not receive full and proper atteetion. Having alluded to the improvements which bad been made in the school buildings Councillor Roberts said they had not finished yet. They had been reminded by the Board of Education that the buildings, and more especially the site upon whieh they stood, were not such as a town of the importance and size of Warrington ought to have to satisfy its educational needs. They wanted the Grammar School—if be mighteay so without in any sense reflecting upon other schools—to be at the apex of the triangle of their educational system. Tbey wanted it not merely to supply the education necessary to enable their men to enter professions, but to train those who were to be the captains of industry in the years to come. To do that efficiently, it should he their ambition to give, as far as premises and eqoip- ment were concerned,facilities ebich were not excelled by any school within many miles of the town. War- rington was big enough, wealthy enough and important enough to see to that being carried out and, while it was by no means an easy task to find a position which would be suitable for the combined purpz ses of a school and recreation grounds, he hoped that before long they would be able to secure a sufficiently accessible and well-adapted site. The atmosphere of the school, the esprit de corps which was cultivated, ought to be more in evidence in the general life of the town than it had been. He would like to feel that he saw around him that night a large body of men, with youth on their side in most cases, who were capable and would have the opportunity of taking a responsible and creditable part in the public life of Warrington. " I do not care about your politics " said Councillor Roberts "You may be Tariff Reformers or Free Traders, Conservatives or Radicals. That does not matter ti me. Let every man act in these direc- tions according to his conscience and his common sense You may have your religious opinions also. In this land of freedom it is our proud boast that no man owes allegiance to the conscience of any other man, but only to himself and to his God. But I hope in regard to the common needs of this great community there are men amongst us and that this school will send out men in increasing numbers from year to year, who shall join with those who are taking their part and doing their level best for tbe institutions in which we have a common interest, which are free from political and religious influences of a controversial nature. The Grammar School boys will, I hope, be known by name and character as amongst the foremost who are serving their day and generation in this respect." (Applause.) THE REV HORACE GRAY, in responding, emphasised the fact that the commercial side was not the only aspect under which they knew the Grammer School. Many of them treasured the classical aspect (Elear, Hear.)They were,however. paying due attention to com- mercial requirements and Mr. let C. Herzog,—(applause) had, in obedience to the calls which the school had made during the last two years, been able to teach the boys that amount of commercial French and German which was necessary to enable them to go straight into the office or into other sections of the town's activities.As Councillor Roberts had said, they wanted the school to be at the apex of the triangle in that part of the world. Nothing could give him the (speaker) greater pleasure than that the school should be the envy of Lancashire—and it could be. (Applause) They had a solution, if they could only work it our, and he saw no reason why that happy result should not be attained. They wanted the school to be such that those who were responsible for the greater and more important industries of the town could come to it and say—as happily he had heard it, said a few days ago—" I want you to send me a boy who can do so and so." They knew that French and German were required and they must meet the demand which the trades presented to them. It would, how- ever, be the last thing that any educational expert would advise to teach their subjects merely from ti e commercial standpoint. They tried to teach the X Y Z of all the subjects which found a place in the school curriculum and that was how they could do their duty to the town. They had not made a call on the Willcocks trust because there had been no urgency, but he thought he could guarantee that by the end of July the banking account would show a very different figure. A very pleasing feature of the letters which he received from parents was that they invariably said their boys were happy at the school. (Applause.) With regard to the scholars taking their part in municipal life, the speaker pointed out that last year, in order to compete for the old boys' prize, the whole school studied a book which dealt very largely with the building up of the British Empire. He hoped some good fruit would result from that. (Hear, hear.) The headmaster con- rlueed by commenting upon the satisfactory nature of the examination results, pointing out that they were achieved without a system of cramming, and were only made possible by the assistance of a very satisfactory and helpful staff. (Applauie). "PROSPERITY TO THE COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON." Mr. OSWALD H. COOKE, in submitting this toast, referred to the debt which we owed to men like Sir Thomas B iteler who in the Middle Ages carried the banner of civilization and religion into outlying districts and did much to make England famous. Having referred to Warrington's progress, he said they could look forward with confidence to the time when the town would become better in the matter of housing, because of recent years great improvements had bean made. The Town Council would have to look to their guns now that the Insurance Act Committees were coming into force and they must see that the wide powers conferred upon them were wisely exercised. In conclusion:the speaker conjured up visions of a day when School-brow would be lined with trees and geraniums, when the Circus at Market Gate would have a fount of clear water flowing from it —on the lines of Paris or Trafalgar Square—and when the Ship Canal would carry on its bosom vessels like the " Mauretania," crowded with American passengers, viewing the beauties of Warrington. (Laughter and applause). Mr. H. GORDON ROBERTS, in responding, said a stranger walking into Warrington from any direction could not fail to be struck by the prosperity that was evident at all the works. Smoke was Issuing from all the chimneys—Alderman Bennett would probably rather not see it—(laughter)—but that smoke meant that work was being done and that Warrington was sending out not only into England but to all corners of the world the produce of the labour of her workpeople. With</p>
        <p>regard to the government of the town, there seemed to be a lack of method in dealing with the old portions of the town. We had only one thoroughfare worthy of the name and that was Bridge-street. Buttermarket- street, Horsemarket-street and the approaches at the far ends of the town were a disgrace to the county borough and it behoved the Council to begin to do something right quickly. One of the most striking features of the past year's work was the experiment which had been carriod out in the isolation hospital at Sankey for the cure of consumptives. That experiment, he was told, had been well worth the trial and he hoped it wouN be possible to dou'lle or treble the accommo- dation, if necessary. (Hear, hear). THE PRESIDENT." The health of "The President" was honoured on the proposition of Mr. C. S. Wooos. During the evening an admirable musical programme was submitted. Mr. J. Babbitt, as usual, scored signal successes in " The Trumpeter," "Dear Heart," and other songs which were given with all his wonted fervour and expression. Mr. T. Critchley was heard to advantage in "Gentlemen, the King." The humorous items were provided by Mr. Victor Roberts and Mr. Walter Moores, each of whom created great merriment. by well-chosen songs and sketches. Mr. Bert Smith was a capable and sympathetic accompanist.</p>
        <p>5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
