      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>League of Pity Dance.</head>
        <p>League of Pity Dance.</p>
        <p>• Miss Broadbent, secretary of the League of Pity, infurms us that this dance, at the Winmarleigh Cafe, has been postponed from Thursday, February Ist, to Friday, February 2nd. Tickets may be obtained from Miss Wainwright, 12, Froghalldane. ANTEIRACITE STOVES, Ecornonical, clean, clperful Price from 37/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge St. Warrington.—.Advt. Knutsford Liberals.</p>
        <p>Arranged by the Knutsford Division Liberal Association a public meeting will be held in the Council School, Appleton on Thursday next. Mr. P. M. Oliver (prospective Liberal candidate), Coun- cillor James Kendall, J P. and Captain H. G. Roberts (chairman) will give addresses. The Picturedrome. The chief picture on the bill to appear at the Picturedrome on Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday next will be " The Bootlegger," whilet on Thuisday, Friday, and Satuiday "A message from beyond ' %sill be the chief attraction. In addition .there will be many others of a humorous and pathetic nature. CUTLERY, Rodgers celebrated knives and forks, 4t3 —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at. I Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Salvation Army. The annual prize distribution and junior demonstra- tion took place en Monday night, and Adjutant Coutts presided over a gene' audience. Dialogues, quartettes, recitations, s-dos etc., were well rendered by the Band of Love, under the direction of Mrs. Fletcher. The prizes were presently' by Mr. W. Stevens, J S.M. assistediby Bandmaster J. Lee.</p>
        <p>Infirmary Centenary Million Penny Fund. The committee of the above fund announce that they have arranged a whist drive and dance to be held at the Enipi.e Hall Skating Rink on Monday. The affiir promises to be as great if not a greater success than last year's and patrons are asked to be there early as the committee desire the whist drive to commence at 730 p.m prompt.</p>
        <p>- - - FIRE GRkTES. If you want a new grate our larg and varied stook will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. _</p>
        <p>-- Liberal Women's Fancy Dress Ball and Carnival This promises to be a very popular and attractive function in the Parr Hall on Thursday next. An immense vatiety of costume is promised, and we understand that Mr. A. H. Crosfield will be among those in fancy dress. Prizes are offered for competi- tion in no has than nine different classes. A whist drive is to be provided for non-dancers. VACUUM CLEANERS free the home from dust. Price 2to 7 guineas. Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at, Warrington.—Advt. Ladies in a Trap Accident. - • -.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dodds of Penketh House, her sister Mrs. Stcekdale (Stockport), and Mrs. Irwin (Manchester), a cousin of the late Mr. Dodds, were the unfortunate victims of an accident on Knutsford-road, yesterday afternoon. The ladias were returning from Lyrnm in Mrs. Dodds' governess car,driven by a young coachman, when the pony bolted down the slope of Black Bear Bridge and galloped at full speed towards Warrington Bridge. When opposite York-street the animal swerved and crashed into a tramway standard with great force, and was killed instantaneou.ly. The ladies were thrown out of the vehicle, but fortunately escaped serious injury. Mrs. Dodds sustained nothing more than a severe shock but the other ladies and the coachman were both cut about the head and face. Dr. Joseph was soon on the scene and after attending to the immediate needs of the ladies in a neighbouring shop (Locker's) he sent them home in a taxi'. It is a matter for congratula- tion that the accident was not attended with more serious consequences for all concerned. " OSRAM AND "TANTALUM " LAMPS.—Agent. JS• GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at., Warricgton.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Musical Society's Concert. The second subscription c ncert of the season in connection with the Warrington Musical Society will he held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening next. The u. usually heavy bookings indicate the great interest which centres in the event, and this is no tit übt largely due to the fact that the solo vocalist will be Miss Marie Houghton, a former member of the society, who has been studying under the aegis of Madame Albani in London, and has achieved some notable successes on the concert platiorm. It will be remembered that Miss Houghton's last pubic appearance in Warrington was as Bessie Throckmorton in the Musical Society's opera " Merrie England." We understand that at Wednesday's concert Miss Houghton will, by the special wish of Madame Albani, sing Handel's " Rejoice greatly" with orchestral accompaniment. The audience will also have the opportunity of hearing a distinguished violinist in the person of Miss Helena Koutorovitch, who will play Mendelssohn's Violin Concerto.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Headmastership for Local Competition. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will be held on Monday evening when the business to be considered will include the following :</p>
        <p>THE EVELYN-STREET SCHOOL. NO OPEN COMPETITION FOR HEADMASTERSHIP. The School Management Committee has resolved ;—That the male certificated assistant teachers (head and assistant) now serving in the public elementary schools in the Borough, be given an opportunity of applying for the position of head master of the boys' department of the Evelyn- street Council school, the commenoing etliry for the position at the rate of £250 per annum upon the 31st March, 1913, and to be increased or reduced after that date, in accordance with the Committee's seale of salaries, upon the average attendance of the department during that period; that all applicants be warned that canvass- ing members of the Education Committee, either directly or through friends or third persons, will be considered a disqualification ; and that tl,e appointment be made by this Committee. In consequence of the closure of the Sankey Bridges (infants') Council School and the infants'department of the Trinity School which the opening of the new school will necessitate, the committee bes appointed the headmistresses—Miss Bertha Pugh and Miss Dorothy Bee—to be headmistresses of the infants' and girls' departments respectively of the Evelyn-street School at their present salaries—£l33 and £145 per annum respectively. £F.EDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee bad before them a letter from the Clerk to the Board of Guardians with reference to the cases referred to them by the Committee, of children whose psrents were in receipt of Poor Law relief, and who were reported by the head teachers as being, in their opinion, unsble, by reason of lack of food, to fully benefit from the instruc- tion provided. It was noted from the reports of the relieving officers and the medical officers, that the children in question had been found to be sufficiently fed, and that the Guardians were satisfied tbat the relief granted to the parents in these cases bad been properly applied. After the subject had been fully discussed, it was resolved that in future, when children are reported to the committee, either by the head teachers or other persons, BS being apparently unable, by reason of lack of food, to take full advantage of the education pro- vided for them, the procedure shall be as follows, viz.: —(a) the children shall be examined by the School Medical offic9r as soon as possible with a view to his furnishing the committee with his opinion as to whether or not the. children are insufficiently fed ; (b) the parents of all children reported by the School Medical Officer to he insuffi- ciently fed shall be immediately informed thereof ; and the children at once admitted to the children's dining rooms ; and (c) inquiries shall then be made into the financial circumstances of the parents of these children with a view to the committse determining as to whether the meals shall be provided free or whether a charge shall be made upon the parents in respect of the cost of the food supplied, sod, lif so, the amount thereof, each case being dealt with on its merits. SCHOOL FOR DEFECTIVE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>AN NSUITABLE SITE. At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee communications from the Board ot Education—stating that the position of the suggested site in Parr-street for the erection thereon of a special school for physically defective children was not considered suit- able for the purpose, and that they were not prepared to approve the Committee's propos 11 to use a part of that land as a site for such a special school—were sub- mitted and noted, and further consideration of the subject was deferred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LETTER FROM MR. F. STOTT. THE NEW ZE/ILli'i-iD ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM MR. F. STOTT. THE NEW ZE/ILli'i-iD ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>In all probability you will have received cable news of the result of the Triennial Elections of the Dominion. The results in thirty constituencies was left for the second ballot to decide.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the last elections were concluded and we are now in a position to form an idea as to the position of the parties, although we cannot definitely state v hat tie actual numbers are for each of them_ Time Maori else ions were decided yesterday, and two of the successful candidates arc being claimed as supporters of the Opposition by the Conservatives and as Liberals by the Government. .</p>
        <p>If the two members give their allegiance to the Government, which is probab'e, the position will be that Sir Joseph Ward and Mr. Massey will each have 37 supporters. The full compliment (SO) is made up by two Independents and four Labour members. It will therefore be seen that the Labour adherents will hold the balance of power in the new parliament. Sir Joseph Ward has promised to make a statement to-morrow as to what action he intends to take.</p>
        <p>It is difficult to see on what grounds he can con- tinue in office, after the recent expression of public - opinion, for in the late Parliament the Government had a majority of 17. SECOND BALLOT. This is the second electioa which has taken place under the second ballot system, and in all probability it will be the last. Both parties have expressed their opposition to it. It lends itself.to all kinds of political intrigue and dishonesty, and does not permit of the accurate political expression cf the electors. TORIES SUPPORT SOCIALISTS.</p>
        <p>In a number of constituencies we had the novel spectacle of the Tories giving their full support to revolutionary Socialists, not because they agreed with a single tenet of the Socialist programme, but because of their desire to defeat the Government.</p>
        <p>Three of the Labour men elected, owe their success to the support they recaived from the Tories in the second ballot.</p>
        <p>LABOUR AND THE ELECTIONS. In the late Parliament there was only one Labour mernbn., and unfortunately he was defeated at the recent election by a Tory in the second ballot. Tie Labour Party had 22 candidates in the field, and there were 10 Socialists. The large increase in the votes cast for the Labour and Socialist candidates indicates that the workers are now moving on the lines of their colleagues in Australia. If these forces were represented in Parliament in propoition to the votes registered for their candidates, the Labour Party would be entitled to ten and the Socialists three members. There is every probability that the Tories tail' take the reins of g)vernment. Their candidates only received 35 per cent, of the votes cast at the first ballot.</p>
        <p>CAUSES AND EFFECTS. The result of the election has been a surprise even to the Tories. It was fully expected that the Govern- ment would be again elected, but with a reduced majority. The re-action is not as much against Liberalism as against " Wardism." Since the death of the late Mr. Seddon the party has been losing in public favour. The action of Sir Joseph Ward in connection with the gift of the Dreadnought has been resented by thousands of the electors, and has been one of the main causes in bringing about his defeat This change of front, from an opponent of corn pulsory military service to one of the strongest supporters of the principle has also cost him a considerable numbers of votes. One Cabinet Ifinister Sir John Findley (who along with Sir Joseph Ward represented New Zealand at the Coronation) was defeated, along with three ex-Ministers.</p>
        <p>I hops to send a further contribution by the nest mail with comments on the recent licensing poll and compulsory military service.—Yours . FRED STOTT. Roseneath, Wellingtcn, New Zealand. December 21st., 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>FOUND IN THE MERSEY.</head>
        <p>FOUND IN THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Woman's Fear of an Operatic n.</p>
        <p>The deputy corner Mr. F. A. Jonel conducted an inquiry yesterday afternoon into the circumstances surrounding the death of Mary Ann Chorley aged 39 years, of 17, Booth-street, Warrington, whose body was recovered from the river Mersey at Atherton's Quai, on Wednesday. Matthew Chorley, husband of deceased said she had complained of pains on the right side of her body and had been to the Infirmary. There, she was informed that an operation would be necessary and she appeared t) be afraid of tbis. She had never threatened to take her life. Mrs. Griffiths, 10, Hesketb-street, said in December she had met deceased in Dutton's entry. She said she had been told by two doctors that she would have to undergo an operation. She afterwards began to cry. Witness had not seen her since that day. Frederick Dutton, 35, liesketh-street, sub-nptain in the 19arrington Grappling Corps, said that on the after- noon of Wednesday he saw the body floating in the Mersey. He along with others got the body out. Sergeant Stewart said there vt ere no signs of violence on the body and there were no papers in deceased's possession. The jury returned a verdict of Found drownld."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Belfast.</head>
        <p>Belfast.</p>
        <p>[TO TILE EDITOR OF TITE " EXAMINER. "J Sir,—Whatever the crocodile tears which Sir E. Carson so readily sheds upon the v4rious dusty plat- forms of our great public halls may mean, there is one thing very certain to every thinking person. And that cause, which this sorrowful orator is championing, been as strong as he would hare us believe, an attitude of absolute indifference to the visit of the'First Lord of the Admiralty, would have been the most powerful argument which the Anti-Home Rulers could have brought against the Government. But one suspects that the motives behind this attempt to kill free speech are deeper than appears on the surface of things. The object of these faction-mongers is simply this : Having a poor case, they wish by their yetpings, to cause a violent demonstration of the Nationalists in order to throw the charge of impassioned tyranny against them, But it illustrates to we Englishmen, the fine restraint which the United Irish League is capable of exercising upon its nynnbers, when by a word it can command them to stay at home and not meet these maniacs with the weapons of lunacy. It is to be earnestly hoped that the Nationalists as a body will ignore the idiotic snarlings of these loyal and broadminded tolerationists. I feel confident that Mr. Churchill will not only go to Belfast and hold his meeting, but that he will go through the main streets of the city and enter the ball openly, as becomes his position as the head of the Navy. When that great paper "Punch" is moved to condemn this policy of Carson, it is plainly evident that a grave and disastrous overstepping of English fairplay has been committed and Home Rulers should hail this condemnation of Ulster with gratitude. Never can the argument of tyranny and oppression be brought against the Nationalist Party twain. ONLOOKETrij</p>
        <p>461=</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>THE FOOD THAT KEEPS YOU FIT.</p>
        <p>APPRECIATED BY THOUSANDS.</p>
        <p>When you feel "knocked up " when your spirits are low—when health and energy forsake you, and weakness and lassitude are the order of the day, then it is that you begin to think about your food. Much of the unfitness in the land is simply due to the use of improper food. You recognise this and begin to change you' diet; but you will fail in your best endeavours if you neglect to use in liberal quantities that famous Food-Beverage known as Dr. Tibbles' NI-Cocoa. It works wonders when a man is "knocked up.' Its gentle tonic property quickly exerts a beneficial influence upon his constitution and soon he is fit again And having made him " fit " it keeps him "fit." Compounded of those four famous articles of diet— Cocoa, Kola, Malt and Hops—it contains just the qualities necessary to make a perfect food. Use Vi-Cocoa and your health is guaranteed. Give Vi-Cocoa a trial. Never mind what you've been drinking at breakfast or supper in the past, start drinking Vi-con to-day—you'll feel the benefit of it at once. Do not ask your grocer for " Cocoa "—ask for</p>
