      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"His Indian Wife."</p>
        <p>There are dramas and dramas, but a drama to appeal to the general public must have a good share of sensationalism and a nearly equal proportion of humour. In" Big Indian Wife " which has been staged through the week at the Royal Court Theatre there are the two essentials of a good drama, and what more suitable place could have been chosen for the incidents of the play than the old romantic West? The plot is good and many exciting and humorous situa- tions are to be witnessed and enjoyed. But, however well written the story may be, almost everything depends on the actors and actresses who portray the various characters and in this respect "His Indian Wife " company reaches a high standard of merit. Miss Marguerite Estivelle displays much talent in the difficult part of Wild Rose. As her lover, Hubert Ainsworth, Mr. Leonard Williams is the typical hero. The part of Virolfskang, a treacherous Indian, is in the able hands of Mr. Graham Winsforcl whilst the juvenile actor, Master Max Gumpert soon gains the appreciation of the audience. The story if how the lovers are separated and eventually united leads one from the rolling prairie to the civilised and unromantic East.An ' abundance of witty humour is kept going by Billy Twiggles and his companion Black Duck. There is also the unfaithful wife in Lady Grace Kingsley and in this part Miss May Grace achieves distinction. As her husband Mr. G. Moreton Williams gives a capital study, whilst Mr. Gerald Byrne as a just Indian Chief, leads one away from the old school theory that all Indians are to be regarded with suspicion. Mr. Harry Sydney makes a good mad medicine man while the faithful Lonely Star is in the capable hands of Mr. George Portions. The other characters though minor are equally as good and the staging is perfect. One nearly forgets the discomforts of English fogs when the free and open life of the West is most realistically depicted. The lovers of a good thrilling drama have been amply satiefied with this week's performance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week Will be an exceptionally interesting time for patrons rif the theatre. Mr. J. C. Lodge- Percy's company will present a capital selection from their repertoire of pla3a on five evenings, while Thursday will be set apart for a complimentary benefit to Mr. A. M. Loader, the popular general manager. On Monday and Wednesday the great society drama Moths "—adapted from Ouida's great novel—will be staged. It is described as the greatest moral play ever written. Miss Henrietta Sehrier will appear as Fuschia Leach and Mr. J. C. Lodge-Percy as Lord Jura. On Tuesday and Friday the attraction will be the celebrated play "A Fool's paradise" by Sidney Grundy, author of "A bunch of violets," &amp;c.—a work in which Sir John Hare achieved much distinction. On Saturday evening the famous romantic play "Mr. Barnes of New York " will be given, with Miss Henrietta Sobrier as Marina Paoli and Mr. J. C. Lodge Percy as Mr. Barnes. MR. LOADER'S BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Loader will take his second annual benefit on Thursday evening anda'there is every indication that it will be a "bumper." tinder Mr. Loader's manage- ment the entertainments at the theatre have been brought to a high standard of interest and merit, and playgoers will doubtless show their appreciation, as they did last year, by crowding the building for the benefit performance. The programme is one of excep- tional attractiveness. One of the principal features will be a dramatic episode "The broken st! " written by Mr. Loader. The characters will be taken by •Nliss Gladys Adams, Messrs. Charles D. Law and A. M. 'Loader. Miss Henrietta Schrier and Mr. J. C. Lodge-Percy will apear in the amusing American playlet " Speechless.' A duologue entitled " The Clown" (by Mr. B. Townroe) will be given by Mr. and Mrs. B. S. Townroe. The following local artistes will also take part :—Miss Dorothy Browne at the piano ; iis Sara Silcock, soprano ; Mr. H. M. Ashton, humorist ; Mr. W. A. Warburton, bass; Mr. J. H. Draper, tenor ; Miss Nellie Mullins, soprano ; Miss Dorothy Turner, mezzo-soprano ; Mr. Willie Farrington, comedian ; Mr. Walter Moores and Mr. W. W. Whittle, humorists. An orchestra, under the direction of Mr. Tom Boothroyd, will play selections. Artistes from the Palace and the Royal Theatte of Varieties will appear by kind pei mission of the resi.ective managements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>The heavy snowfall during the week deterred all but the enthusiasts on Saturday last when Ryldnds W.R.C. Harriers only mustered five. However, Harding, Brooks, Woods, Burton and J Thorniley made the best of the occasion and enjoyed a five miles run without racing. Warrington A.C. were fortunate to muster twenty for a" go as you please " training spin, at Wilderspool in which the most noticable were II Holbrook, W Bolton, J Parsonage, A Siddall, A Ciregson, T Turton, Shadwell and C Vase. A number of the juniors shaped in a manner that suggests a keen contest for places on the teams for the West Lancashire and Nortlaern Championships. At this juncture it would have been detrimental to the championship aspirations of the club had the weather compelled the runners to forego a training spin and thanks are due to. the indulgence of their friends together with the efforts of Messrs C Berry and R Bennett who designed, constructed, and operated the latest typo of" Snow Plough " which has since been facetiously described as 2 11. P.—anyhow it was Hard Pulling for 2. To-days fixture for the senior section only is from headquarters at 3.15 p.m. Members of Warrington A.C. desirous of being selected for the Junior Championship are required to travel by the 2.20 p.m. train from the Central Station Ao Widnes, and by the Transporter to Runcorn, where the trial run takes place in conjunction with the return inter club fixture with Runcorn Tally Ho, whose head- quarters are" Plantation Pleasure Grounds," Runcorn Hill.</p>
        <p>\VEST LANCASHIRE C.C.A. The scrutiny meeting, held on Thursday evening at Liverpool, passed 16 junior and 6 senior trams for the championships which, by the kind permission of Lord Derby takes plena at Knowley Park, Prescot, on Satur- day next, a cheap outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WINO'S</head>
        <p>WINO'S</p>
        <p>.....htnin. COUGH _CURE</p>
        <p>ALBERT Slaryr:Esq.:BLS.B.Sc.; the celebrated London analyst, says:—VENo's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is composed Of pure Ingredients. and Is a most excellent reraedy for bronchitis. asthma. and all lung affections."</p>
        <p>The purest and surest remedy obtainable for COUGHS and COLDS INFLUENZA COLDS ryILDHEIS COUGHS BAD BREATHING BRONCHITIS BLOOD SPITTING eIIRONIC ASTHMA WEAK LLINGC Price Did., 1/1f and 2/0, of all Chem!</p>
        <p>WIN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
