      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>REV. HON. C. F. CROz,S.</head>
        <p>REV. HON. C. F. CROz,S.</p>
        <p>Photo</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Chester has appointed the Rev. Hon. C. F. Cross, Vicar r‘f Stretton to be rural dean of Frodsham, in succession to the late Rev. Canon Blogg, The appointment has given great satisfaction to Mr. Cross's parishioners, by whom he is held in affectionate regard, and to church people in all parts cf the rural deanery. The new rural dean is the fourth but second surviving son of the first Viscount Cross,and nephew to Mr. T. H. Lyon, and was born on' September 22nd, 1860, in the house which is now the Vicarage, Northwich. Mr. Cross's early years were spent at Hill Cliffe, Warrington. Be was first educated privately under thB late Rev. Canon Gray, Kirkby Vicarage, Liverpool, and then proceeded to the great Public School of Rugby. His University course was undertaken at Trinity College, Cambridge, where he obtained the degree of M.A. Having studied at Leeds clergy school, Mr. Cross was ordained and entered upon the curacy of St. John the Baptist, Chester, which he held from 1884 to 1887. He was curate at New Brighton from 1887 to 1891, in which year he was appointed to the living of Shutwick. Mr. Cross came to Stretton in 1897, and under his guidance the church has made steady progress in all departments of its work. He takes a very keen interest in the Church Army and is adviser of the van work in the diocese of Chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>St. Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>Sermons were preached at this church on Sunday in aid of the Church of England Society for providing for Waifs and strays. The preacher, morning, afternoon and evening was the Rev. Herman F. S. Collier, of Blundellsands, Clerical Organizing Secretary. It was pointed out that the society has the care of 210 children in five homes, one of [which is at Warr:ngton, namely, St. George's, which owes its origin to the energy and enthusiasm of the Rev. G. B. Thurston, rector of Lymm, and who was at one time in charge of St. George's Mission Church. The home affords accommodation for 17 boys. There are 3,900 children in all the 104 homes. In the afternoon what is known as a "pound service" was held, members of the congre- gation bringing with them pounds of tea, coffee, sugar, flour, &amp;0., which were deposited in the chancel, and have been sent to the Warrington horse for use,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>St. Peter's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>At St. Peter's Church the annual sermons in connec- tion with the Church Pastoral Aid Society were preached on Sunday. The pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Rev. B. Percival (vicar) and the preacher in the evening was the Rev. H. P. Hiscoke, vicar of St. Ambrose, NVidnes. The offertories during the day were devoted to the funds of the society. On Monday evening the annual meeting was held in the schoolroom, .10bn-street. Councillor Steel presided, and he was supported by the Rev. E C. Dzwick, Dean of St. Aidan's College, Birkenhead, the Rev. B. Percival (vicar), the Rev. J. H. Garnett (curate), and Messrs. T. H. Wratislaw, J McQuillin, T. Reddi,h, W. Pinson, E. A. Padmore and the Church Army Captain.—The Chair- man said that in a large working class palish like that of St. Peter's they appreciated the support given to them year by year by the Church Pastoral Aid Society who contributed £l3O a year towards the stipends of the curate and the Church Army officer. It had been said that a clergyman in order to do his duty to his parishioners ought not to have a parish of more than 2,000 people, but in St. Peter's parish they had 11,000 and only the services of two clergy. had it not been for the support of the Church Pastoral Aid Society they would have been unable to have the services of the curate and consequently the whole work would have fallen upon the shoulders of their Vicar. Although a poor parish they contributed IN each year towards the funds of the society, which was the fourth largest amount contributed in the whole diocese of Liverpool. —The Rev. E. C. Dewick gave an interesting address on the work of the society.—On the proposition of Mr. Wratislaw, seconded by Mr. McQuillian, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Rev. E. C. Dewick and the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Warrington Sunday School Teachers' Association.</head>
        <p>Warrington Sunday School Teachers' Association.</p>
        <p>Science and the Bible.</p>
        <p>-------- ---- - There was a good attendance at a gathering of the Sunday School Teachers' Association, in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on Monday evening. Canon Willis presided and an interesting address was given by the Rev. W. Handy Cock, vice-principal of the Warrington Training College. The rev. gentleman took for his subj.ct " Science and the Bible ' and in the course of his remarks said that as far as he could see for his study of science there was nothing in the facts of science contrary to the true teachings of the Bible. At some length be reasoned out the existence of an infinite eternal God, w ho, in the finite past created the present universe asla training grind for mortal creatures in preparation for an immortal state. He went on to explain that the loving God who had the interests of His immortal creatures in view could not have pre- vented the existence of evil in the world. Continuing, he described the fulfilment of the predictions cf the Old Testament, showing that the course of salvation opened up, was quite as 000 would expect, and he went on to explain and showed, the t though the doctrine of the Trinity was beyond reason it was not contrary to reason. He concluded a most interesting address by saying that as teachers they must be sure of what they taught. The discipline in some of the Sunday schools was disgraceful and unless they had an exceptional disciplinarian at the head of the Sunday school it would not be successful. They must insist on order and quiet- ness and if they would do that, they would have to give the children something worth being quiet for. The teachers must know the subject on which they taught, thoroughly. If they would study and understand the subject the Sunday school would be a success. (Applause). A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the rev. gentleman, and at the close refreshments were served.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a good attendance at the weekly meeting of this society on Sunday, Mr. Donald INlcEachern presiding. Mr. T. Dale, of Bank Quay, gave the address upon " Neherniates desire before the King for the good of his own people the Jews." Miss Eva Tanner sang most sweetly, "God who madest earth and heaven "and Beyond." Mr. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. H. Osborne and Mr. A. Healey took part in the meeting. Übe Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) expressed the hope that there would be a large attendance at the open meeting to be held on Sunday. Legh-street Prlmitiva Methodist School. The quarterly tea and concert in connection with the above church was held on Thursday in the schoolroom and passed off very successfully. rhis eßort was organized by the married men, assisted by several ladies of the church. A substantial tea was provided, to which a goodly number sat down, after which the com- pany settled down to enjoy the programme of the con- cert which was to follow. Mr N Cook, jun., presided, and gave a brief address. Miss Elsie Hewitt, who is a young vocalist of great promise, scored a distinct success in her renditions of " A Woodland Madrigal " and "I he valley of laughter." Mr E Hannon, who is ever at home on the concert platform, was very successful in his songs "The land across the sea " and "By the fountain," the latter being encored and he responded with" Good company." Mr G Gough who was in good voice gave fine interpretations of "The Trumpeter" and " Out on the de ,p," the audience showing their appreciation by encoring the latter song, the response being" To-morrow will he Friday." Mr S Rowlands, who has recently returned from U.S.A. after a seven years' stay and who was connected with Legh-street before going out there, gave two violin solos which were well executed showing fine teaching skill, the pieces being" Sarabandi " and " American Ragtime," thc lively rythem of the latter so stirring up the audience that an encore was demanded and con- ceded. Mr C 4 Whitfield, who is an old favourite OD</p>
        <p>'e -SOl</p>
        <p>the platform caused mach merriment with his humorous recitations' The Census" and" Uncle Dick's advice to single women," the latter being encorei. The concluding item was an exceedinely well given humorous dialogue by friends from Hill Cliffs Baptist Church, entitled " Mixeres Matrimonial Mart," and was most amusing throughout, causing roars of laughter Miss A Atkinson accompanied the vocal items with good taste and judgment. Mr N Cook accompanied the violin solos successfully. During an interval a vote of thanks was proposed by Mr E Griffiths, seconded by Mr R Bridge and supported by Rev W Cooper. The singing of the National Anthem brought a pleasant evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>The *Examiner, Saturday, January. 27 1912</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Bold-street Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>To a very interested and crowded gathering of the Bold-street Wesley Guild, Mr. Percy Silcock gave a practical proof of Longfellow's famous statement, " And things are not what they seem. With the aid of several splendid drawings Mr. Silcock showed how the sensations of sight are often wrongly interpreted by the brain, producing effects which are frequently quite the opposite from what might be expected. He pointed out how even the Greek architects were Conscious of optical illusions, and designed their huge columns and flights of steps in such a manner as to avoid the pro- duction of these curious effects. Explanations of several other remarkable phenomena were also given, some of the drawings causing much amusement. "Seeing is believing" will be a very doubtful adage in future to all those who were present. A very hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Silcock for his splendid paper on the propositon of Mr it J Peake. seconded by Mr Stanley C Jose, and supported by Mr W Miss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. Church Tea and Concert.</head>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. Church Tea and Concert.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea and concert was held on Monday, about 160 sitting down to a substantial tea, prepared by some of the lady members. The concert was presided over by Mrs. Crosfield (of Colwyn Bay)and an excellent programme was gone through. Miss Eva Tanner (who possesses a sweet soprano voice) rendered " Orange Blossom " and " Dear little Jemmy Face " in a pleasing manner, as did also Madame L Fairhurst the songs Flight of ages" and " Good-bye." Mr. Edward Pownall's powerful rendering of the bass songs "The Skipper" and "The Young Royalist " received very hearty applause, and Mr J Willett pleased the audience with " Dear Heart " and Roses." Dr Law received an encore for his exceedingly realistic recital " Kissing. Cup," his powers as an elocutionist being displayed to great advantage. Master Frank Dowling gave a recita- tion entitled The Charge of the Night Brigade," the experiences of P.C. 600 highly amusing the audience. Master Donald Pearce and party contributed a coon song and dance "Lily of Laguna," end Mr Sam Aukland rendered some humorous items in his own inimitable style. During the evening the Secretary, A Tilling, presented the annual report of the Chapel. The report stated that "In looking back over 1911, so far as the work of our Chapel is concerned, we find there is nothing of a particularly striking nature to report upon, but rather the year has been one of steady, consistent effort. The lack of appreciation of religious services, which we regretfully hear of in connection with so many churches and chapels of our land, is not, we are thankful to say, applicable to the people of this Mission, for the Chapel is regularly comfort- ably (and, on occasions uncomfortably) filled with perple desirous of worshipping God, and hearing the faithful preaching of the Word. We have no paid ministry, and most of the preachers who fill our pulpit are working men, with a message for working men and women, brt we sincerely believe that the canopied and lofty roofs of our gret.t cathedrals and magnificent churches do not resound to more heartfelt songs of praise and thanksgiving, and do not witness, in any greater measure, the outpouring of God's Holy Spirit, than is manifested in the inspiring services that we hold in our little Bethel here. As a Church, we of the Workingmen's Mission pray for guidance that our influence in this district may only be for the enobling and uplifting of the people within our immediate sphere,. We believe that the Gospel of Jesus Christ which is preached here week by week is the only permanent cure of all social evils, and in endeavouring to spread the principles of the Master. The reports of the various branches of the Mission's work were included in the Sunday School Secretary's report a few weeks ago, and I don't propose to go over the same ground again. Suffi.:e it to say that, all departments are in a healthy condition. I have already mentioned that the chapel is well tilled every Sunday evening, and I don't think it is out of place here to mention that the Sunday school has hem more crowdea during this last few Sundays that on any other previous occasion. The question of the extension of the Cha`pel is of importance ; but the extension of the Sunday school premises is of far greater and more pressing urgency, and we are hoping that before many months are passed we may have additional premises which will make it possible to carry on the work with far greater effectiveness, and under something like comfortable conditions. For some months past the ladies of the various sections of the Mission have been working, pre- paring for a bazaar for the Extension Fund, to be held in November of this year. We are looking forward to a successful bazaar, and appeal for tha support of all our members and friends. The list of officers for the coming year is as follows :—President, Mr N Hough ; vice-presidefits, Mr George Dale ; treasurer, Mr F Taylor; communion secretary, Mr R Jones; general secretary, Mr A Tilling; Committee, Messrs T Dale, D Hill, W Worrell, F Atkinson, II Edwards. J Hassell, T Edmund; Woolley, P Gleave, A Gleave, G Head, S Millington and Callam. The Women's Suffrage question does not trouble us sr; far as our Church is concerned. Our lady members get the vote precisely on the same terms as the men, but the lady members have always voted almost unani- mously against the inclusion of lady members on the committee. Of course, there is nothing to prevent this decision being altered at any future time if the lady members are agreeable, as I am sure the " mere men ' will not stand in the way. Financially the chapel accounts stood at the end of the year as follows : Brought forward from 1910, £6 Be. 101 d. ; income dur- ing year, £lOl 16s. lid. ; expenditure during year, £96 7s. 21 ; balance in hand on the 31st December, 1911, £ll ISs. 7id. I may say, however, that since the accounts were made up we have had various items to meet which have reduced our balance sheet about £4. The Society Class is still under the able leadership of our good friend Mr. T. Dale, and is in a healthy con- dition. To Mrs. Crosfield again onr thanks are due for the generous financial help she has rendered to us during this, as in previous years, the cost of lighting, fuel, water, etc., being kindly defrayed by her. We hope that the knowledge of the good accomplished by the mission may be more than sufficient recompense to our kind and generous patron for all she has done for it in the past The total number of Church members on the Church roll is 125, the average attendance at the monthly Communion Service being 63. Mrs. CROSFIELD, in commenting on the report, con- gratulated the workers on the satisfactory condition of the Mission, and expressed her intention of seeing that an extension was made to the Sundsy school premises before many months were passed, which state- ment (needless to say) was received with rounds of applause. A vote of thanks to Mrs. Erostlsl4 and the artistes was proposed by Mr. T. Dale, and the singing of the Doxology brought a very pleasant evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>National Union of Journalists.</head>
        <p>National Union of Journalists.</p>
        <p>The it'll d annual dinner in connection with the Warrington, Widnes and Runcorn branch of the National Union of Journalists, was held at .the Winmarleigh Cafe, Warrington, on Saturday evening. Over twenty members and friends were present, and the gathering proved an unqualified success. Mr. Frank Fennelly (chairman of the branch) presided, and the chief guest was Mr. W. N. Watts, of Man- chester (general secretary of the Union). The cater- ing of the Cafe Company gave the utmost satisfaction. After dinner the loyal toasts were submitted from the chair and heartily honoured. Mr. G. H. Warburton Steel proposed "The N.O J.," and com- mended its work as being based upon the true prin- ciples of brotherhood. Mr. Watts responded and dealt in an interesting way with some of the most pressing questions which are at present occupying the attention of the Union. He looked forward to the time when there would be an international federation of working journalists. Mr. Watts spoke highly of the loyalty and solidarity o.f the Warrington branch, and paid a special tribute to those who had been the pioneers of the Union 'in the district. The toast of " The Artistes" was submitted by Mr W Mason, and "The Chairman" was honoured on the proposition of Mr II Corrin. A high class musical programme was gone through. Mr J ilabbitt sang a very choice selection of songs in his usual finished and expressive style, and roused great enthusiasm by his stirring and dramatic rendering of " The Trumpeter." Mr Walter Moores was eminently successful in his clever humorous songs in character. His most popular effort was " Captain Ginger," the perfect" make-up " being a striking feature. Items were also contributed in very creditable style by the following members of the branch: Messrs II Corrin, G Archer, H Walker and B Baker. The duties of accompanists were efficiently carried out by Messrs T F Wallington and Bert Smith. An artistic souvenir programme had been designed by Mr. C. A. Pennington. The hon. secretary of the branch is Mr E Iddon and the hon. treasurer Mr C E Hawthorn.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, on Tuesday, Walter Williams, described as a moulder, of heat Bromwich, was charged with having on two occasions obtained food and lodgings by false pretences. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner called at the house of Esther Twist, 20 Parker-street, on Thurs- day, August 11th, 1910, and stated that he wanted lodgings. She questioned him as to his occupation and so forth and he said be was a manager in the employ of the Singer Sewing Machine Co., Smkey-street. Believing this story to be true, she took him in on the agreement that he should pay her 14s. per week. The following day she asked him where his luggage was and he told her that he expected it to arrive sometime that afternoon and asked her to take it in during bis absence. lie then said he would not be home for dinner and she made it up for him to take with him. Ile said he would be home for tea about six o'clock. As no luggage arrived and the prisoner did not put in an appearance she went to the Singer Sewing Machine Co., and inquired about the prisoner. They replied that no such person was employed by them. It appeared the prisoner afterwards went to the house of Maria B. Racey, 41, Parr-street, and obtained six days lodgings valued at 10s, by similar false pretences. Prisoner, who had been arrested in Birmingham the previous day, admitted the offences and he was sent to prison for one month with hard tabour.</p>
        <p>VANNIAN'S INGENIOUS FRAUD. WARRINGTON MAN HEAVILY FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, John Hatton, vanman, 5, back of 31, William-street, Birmingham was charged with embezzling the sum of .£1 13s. 3d. on June 25th. 1911 from Alfred Itadfirth and on June 17th with embezzling ss. 7d., and on June 19th the sum of 7s. 11d. Mr. T. S. Steel who appeared for the complainant said that defendant was in the employ of the Latchford Stesm Laundry, recently conducted by Mr. Radfirth and it was his duty to collect aod deliver clothes and receive the amount payable in respect of work done. When he left the clothes it was his duty to make out a receipt for the money, a duplicate of which remained in the receipt book. Each customer was identified by a number and in making out the receipt the defendant put the date on and the letter and number of the person paying the money, and the amount received, together with his Initials. In the two last charges, it appeared he had collected the sum of ss. 7d. from Mrs. Ada Whitfield, 11, Thyune-street, and instead of accounting for the ss. 7d. he had received he gave a receipt for that amount and altered the counterfoil- to appear that be bad collected 2a. from another person. In the other case he called on Mrs. Sarah Smith, 6, Pletcher-street, on the 19th June, and received 7s. 11, Be altered the counterfoil in a similar manner and only made it appear that he had collected 10d. He made the counterfoils so that they appeared to have come from other customers,and the 2s. and the 10d.were amounts owed by customers. In the first charge the</p>
        <p>circumstances were rather different, for in that case the defendant had been sent on the 231. h May to collect money from Mr. Henry Llosd, a carter, who brought manure from the works. The amount of the account was £1 ss. 3d., and a further load brought the amount to £1 133. 3d. Defendant gave a receipt for the amount, and on the 27th June he did not appear at work. The matter had been investigated, and a warrant was issued. The defendant bad been arrested on Friday last. It appeared he went to Birmingham, and from there to London and he then went back to Birmingham where he was arrested. When charged with the first offence he said" Yes, that's right" and when be was coming in the train he told Sergeant Stewart that he had never been easy in his mind since the matter occurred. When he was charged with tha other two offences he had said he did not remember them. Mr. Radfirth was away on account of ill-health. Not only were there these charges but on investigation it was alleged that the total amounted to .£4O. _</p>
        <p>Corroborative evidence was given by Henry Lloyd, Bridge Farm, Thelwall, Bessie Clark, 135, Kiaderton- Birkenhead, Mrs. Whitfield, Mrs. Simla Smith and Sergeant Stewart. Mr. Arthur Browne who appsared for the defendant said he was going to submit that there had been a cartain amount of laxity. The defendant was receiving at the time 275. per week and had said that Mr. Radfirth never gave him enough to keep the horses. He had a great love for horses and had spent money in looking after them and had won various prizes. He did not dispute the charges. Mr. Browne then made a pathetic appeal on behalf of the defendant. Tae magistrates retired and on their return defendant was ordered to pay £l2 10s. inclusive of costs or two months' imprisonment. LADS CHARGED WITH SHOP-BREAKING.</p>
        <p>Albert Antrobus, 70, Oldham-street, was charged with attempting to hurglarionsly beak aud enter the dwelling-house of Elijah Hunt, 260 Knutsford-road, with the intent of stsaling on the morning of the 23rd inst.</p>
        <p>- Percy Antrobus, of 70, Oldham-strest, was charged with feloniously and burglariously entering the shop, it, Grove-street, on the 15th December, and stealing a quantity of cigarettes and sweets valued at 2s. Gd., the property of Henry William Allender. Arthur Tomlinson, 4, Old-road, was charged with receiving stolen property. The Chief C instable stated that for some weeks past the police had received complaints to the effect that shops had been broken into in the neighbourhood of Knutsford-road. Special observation had been kept by the police. In the early hours of Tuesday morning Detective Metcalfe was concealed and saw the prisoner Albert Antrobus go into a shop in Koutsford- road and mush the back window. The officer immediately effected an arrest. It appeared the youths had to start work at six o'clock every morning, and their plan was to get up two or three hours earlier and</p>
        <p>iitin the 0 1 g , ' , • , .... .. - ,- -,--„, DEFRAUDING LANDLADY. -. IV- •</p>
      </div>
