      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CARDINAL BOURNE.</head>
        <p>CARDINAL BOURNE.</p>
        <p>ea On Sunday last, with stately ceremonial and in the presence of a grcat congregation, rdinal Bourne, the Roman Catholic Archbishop of Westminster, ma.-.'e his first appearance in Westminster Cathedral since his return from Rome. Dr. Bource is only the fourth Archbishop to be made Cardinal since the Reformation. We reproduce above a Portrait of the Cardinal, wearing the scarlet rob: and black cassock, F. cated on his throne at Westminster House.</p>
        <p>'AiiiiiiAßlAi CHAPTERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CHRISTENING LORE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTENING LORE.</p>
        <p>SOME MORE CURIOSITIES FROM</p>
        <p>CHURCH REGISTERS</p>
        <p>The description of children as "half- baptized," invented by the clergy to describe those who had been sprinkled by other hands than theirs, reminds us that special provision was long maintained for the administration of Baptism to young children likely to die. By the medimval theory unsprinkled children could never see paradise. What, then,of the little new-comers who did not seem likely to •be stayers ?It was surely incumbent upon the Church to do something speedily for them. Both before and after the Reformation the Canon Law arranged that baptism could be administered• by any person in case of danger. To midwives in particular this privilege was given aad they were licensed by the Bishop as baptizers. The old oath is so quaint and interesting as to deserve quotation. The woman was to be "faithful, diligent, and ready to help every woman, as well the poor as the rich, and not to forsake the poor woman to go to the rich," and took pledge "in no way to exercise any manner of witchcraft, charms, or invocation.' The clergy had the duty of teaching the woman "the very wordes and fourme of Baptisme to thententes that they may use them perfectly and none other." Such baptisms may be found duly recorded in parish registers and some of them are decidedly novel. 1582, Sept. 12. Cislye, daughter of Roger Tasker, 'Nimes by Roose Preest, mydwief, that it was so weke that it could not tarry untelle Stmdaye. The name of the children used to be given on such occasions, but there was constant liability to error and many curious entries in registers show that sometimes mistakes were made. Here is an entry made at Hanwell : Daughter. Thomas son of Thomas Messenger was born and baptized October 24 by the mid- wife, called a boy and named by the godfather Thomas, but proved a girl. The inconvenience of having a girl named Thomas would be frequently felt. Possibly it was from such errors that in the sixteenth century the custom crept in of giving the name Creature. Here is an entry from Staplehurst : The xxvii. of Apryle there was borne ii childre of Alexander Beerge the one christned at home snd so deceased called Creature the • other christned at church called John. This name was probably suggested by Tyndale's Bible: "Yf any man be in Christ he is a newe creature." Since baptism, by the old theory, was a transforming rite they would feel able to apply the text and call the little ones new creatures. Here are some entries in illustration: 1561, June 30. The chylde of God filius Ric. Stacy. (Oxford). 1595. Ther was buryed the Bonne of John . . . who dyed imediately as he was borne being named Creature. 1573, April 24. h God's creature, the sonne cf John and Jane Flazzard. (Kidderminster). But sometimes the children lived on and carried the name through life. One at least carried it to the altar. 1579, July 19. Marryed John Hoffynder and Creature Cheseman yong folke. FONT AND BASIN.</p>
        <p>For such extemporised ceremonies as these no special conditions or ritual were enjoined. It was different in the orderly baptism of the Church. Children could only be sprinkled from the font, and the Church Law which dates from Elizabeth's reighn strictly forbids the use of a basin. The Puritans belittled the ceremony and used a common basin, and when in Commonwealth days they gained the ascendency, fonts were in many cases removed altogether. After the Restoration, fonts were again used, but not, in some places without a struggle. There is evidence of a tug-of-war in the entry made in a village in Middlesex where the vicar gets even with the clerk by writing in the register; Elizabeth, the daugter of Wm. Pratt, Feb. 25, 1671-2. The first that in 11 years was baptized with water in the font, the custom being in this place to baptise out of a bason, after the Presbyterian manner, only set in the font, which I could never get reformed, till I bad gotten a new clerk, John Brown, who presently did what I appointed to be done. There is a touch of historical interest in the entry made by the clerk of Peterborough Cathedral at the opening of the new font after the Restoration. 1660. Hellin Austin, was baptized the 7th of March in the Cathedral Church, being the first that was christened in the font there after the setting it up, the said font being pulled down and the lead taken out of it by Cromwell's soldiers. AD. 1643. Those who may be able to inspect old registers should give a careful scrutiny to the entries of the Civil War and Common- wealth period. We make but one quotation, though almost every register can show some- thing unusual. It is from St. Botolph. Aldgate William Clark, a soldier, and Thomasine his wife, who herself went for a soldier, and was billeted at the Three Hammers in East Smithfield, about seven months, and was afterwards delivered of this Child the 16th day of this July, and was baptized the 17th in her lodging, being one Mr. Hublers house. She had been a souldier, by her own confession, about five years, and was some- time Drummer to the company. SPONSORS AND GODPARENTS. Another vista of interest opens up when we begin to discuss the curiosities of sponsor- ship. Few people are aware of the great and almost superstitious importance that used to attach to the offices of godfather and god- mother. In the medieval Church when a man and woman had joined to be godparents to a child they were regarded as "spiritual parents" and, as such, were related not only to the child but to one another. Being related spiritually they could not marry. This led to a great abuse. The habit sprung up for married persons to ask for divorce on the ground that at some time they had been godfather and godmother together at a baptism. Since the Church made the law which declared it wrong for "spiritual parents" to marry, the Church could not refuse a divorce to those who could prove that they had been such. But as there were no records in those days the truth of the statements could not be tested. Once this easy way to divorce was discovered it was shamefully abused, and in 1497 Cardinal Ximenes began his agitation for the compulsory registration not only of baptism but of the names of the sponsors. The manifest advantages of regis- tration in Spain appealed strongly to Thomas Cromwell, and in 1535 registers were made compulsory in England. Registration with all its value and interest arose out of the Church's desire to fight a great social pest, and so long as the controlling influence of the Church in England remained Catholic the rule as to the registration of godparents was</p>
        <p>rigidly applied. But in Elizabeth's day joint sponsorship was no longer a barrier to marriage, and godparents names ceased to appear in the books. FOUNDLINGS.</p>
        <p>- The registration of foundlings is often peculiarly interesting. Such children fell to the care of the parish and usually received the parish name as their own. They took their Christian name from the nearest Saints Day. As a record in the parish of St. Denis, London, says.: 1567 December 14. A cbylde that was Lund at the strangers dore was founde on Saynt Petter day. Wherefore they named the chylde by the day that he was founde, and surname by the parish, so the chylds name was Fetter Dennis. A child found in the church porch of St. Dunstan was called Mary Porch. But the , gem of all such christenings is in the register of St. Helen's Bishopspate. It is self-explain- ing. Job Rakt-out-of-the-Assbes found the last of August in the lane going to Sir John Spencers' back gate, and there laide in a heape of old cole apt:dies, was baptized the first daye of September following. The name Job was well selected, and may have been inspired by the feeling that some measure of patience would be required to carry such a name through life. But little Job was spared the ordeal. A note is added that "he died the next day after."</p>
        <p>FOUNDLING HOSPITAL SPONSORS. In 1741 the famous Foundling Hospital was opened in London and awakened much interest. Infants found in any part of the Metropolis were taken there and cared for until they were able to earn a livelihood. The christening of these neglected sprigs of humanity took place on Sunday mornings, , and it appealed to-many of the gentry as a happy thing to go and stand sponsor for them. The children were called after their sponsors, and received such high-sounding names as Bedford, Bentick, Abercorn and Russell. In later years the sentimental gentry found themselves followed by the grown children to whom they had lightly given their names, who felt entitled to beg or borrow on the ground of supposed relation- ship. This effectually put an end to the desire for sponsorship ; but the 'habit has I made the Baptismal Register very interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>-THE REGISTER TAX.</head>
        <p>-THE REGISTER TAX.</p>
        <p>Some interesting years to scan in registers are those at the close of the 17th century.</p>
        <p>Under William 111. a tax or duty on register entries was imposed, which in some districts appears to have aroused resentment. In some parishes baptisms were discon- tinued, or at any rate the parson has ceased to enter them to save the tax. In other districts there are entries which show that he has been alive to his duties; and regretted that his parishioners should be disloyal to the law. Such jottings are: 1697. John Fry bad a child born in April but not baptised by me. _ 1697. Feb. ye 16. Edward Bayley a dissenter had a child born yet was not made Christian according as our Church requireth. ILLEGITIMATE CHILDREN._</p>
        <p>Entries of the baptisms of illegitimate children are often accompanied by pungent comments, and did the subject lend itself to treatment here we might quote many quaint paragraphs. The clergy in the 17th century often insisted on penance and only received the children" by tollerance." Here is a typical note from the Rochdale Register: 1583, Dec. 15. Jacobus flues Jacobi Earmihaw et Alice Hallowes, boothe of and the sayde Alice did lye in the house of ux' of James Caine in Butt'wortb, the woman bath done her penance. The term " base-born " is usually applied, but the register of Lambeth indulges in the titles "A merry begot" and "A bye-blow." Many follow the more seemly plan of using the Latin " Filius Vulgi " " Filius Scorti " or "Filius terre " ; others rush to the other extreme of making comments which are supposed to voice public opinion and lash the offenders. Here we practice reticence, our readers will know the reason why. Those at any rate will know, who remember how effective our ancestors could be when they took to plain speaking. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ..-'\"...r.,,-..,—,•,-w-s.e.-,‘"</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ..-'\"...r.,,-..,—,•,-w-s.e.-,‘"</p>
        <p>UNCLE CALEB TALKS SOLEMNLY ON BURGLARS. The law says that burglars are people who break in where they have no right, in order to steal. Many children are in great fear of them, but do not realize that they themselves may be little burglars, to be dreaded by others. While two persons are talking, a boy rushes in and breaks right into their con- versation with an interruption to ask some question or to tell some news. He wishes to steal their attention—one kind of a little burglar Occasionally, late at night, a company of boys make a great noise, and break in on people's sleep and steal their rest. Some- times, in a church service, a group of girls begin whispering and giggling, breaking in on the solemnity of the service and stealing away others' enjoyment of it—the little burglars When a company of children are having a merry time, some one breaks in on the happiness with a disagreeable remark and robs them of their smiles—a mean little burglar But there is one class of burglars that come breaking into the mind and steal the atten- tion. When you are in school or at church, and thoughts of play come into your mind, say to yourself, The burglars dre coming." When some one is talking to you, and some- thing happens to draw your attention away, you know that a burglar has come. Frighten him away. Come, boys and girls, let us cease being burglars,stealing from others their attention, their quiet, their happiness, and let us take care that burglars don't molest us. THE NUT CONVENTION CHARLES ELXER JENNY. The Nuts once gathered from every clime, To settle some questions for all time. From every part of the world they came— Large, small, proud, and unknown to fame; From the Hickory up on the highest round To the humble Peanut from underground. From far and near to the meet they strode And some on a bay Hersechestnut rode, While those from over the ocean blue In a shell-barque sailed to the rendezvous. To a long-haired Cocoanut with three eyes • Was first awarded the ptlm for size. Next, after consideration sage, They granted the Chestnut's claim of age. The smallest of all was the Chinquapin; The Beechnut seemed to them all most thin. The Butternut won as the hardest shell, Though the big Black Walnut worried him well. And the Peanut and Paper-shell Almond fought For the softest place, which the latter got. The roughest, they voted by word of mouth, Was the brown Brazil-nut from the South. The smoothest was named as the slick Pecan (A. hazel-hued Filbert also ran). But the crown of all and the final test They never decided—just which was best— For as they pondered the question grave</p>
        <p>By Milo Snowden, I.P. THE POINT AT ISSUE IN THE COTTON DISPUTE. In the terms of the temporary settlement of the lockout in the cotton trade it is said that both sides are fighting for a great prin- ciple—the workpeople for the right to say with whom they will work and the masters for the right to say whom they will employ. It is perfectly obvious that there is no possible reconciliation of these two positions. Either one side or the other will have to give way eventually, or the question in dis- pute will have to be settled by a compromise. Logic and consistency are on the side of the operatives, who say that they will not work with non-unionists. The employers themselves have made the attitude of the operatives a logical and consistent one. If the employers did not recognise trade unionism, and if the wages and conditions were not regulated by conferences between the masters and the operatives, then the attitude of the employers in insisting upon perfect freedom to employ whoever they wished would be quite reasonable and consistent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>AN APPEAL TO FACTS.</head>
        <p>AN APPEAL TO FACTS.</p>
        <p>But in considering the point in dispute in this cotton trade trouble it must be borne in mind that trade unionism among the operatives and federation among the employers are recognised as the respective organisations for the regulation and fixing of wages. The federated employer does not arrange with his own workpeople what wages he will pay them. When he joined the Federation of employers he gave away his individual right to treat with his work- people in the matter of wages. In like manner the individual operative who is a member of the trade union has given up his right to bargain with his employer in the matter of wages. Both sides have done these things because they are convinced that on the whole it is to their individual advantage that collective bargaining should take the place of individual bargaining. The operatives were driven into combination because experience had shown them that if they stood alone, the individual workman was powerless against the employer. Trade unionism among the operatives made them more powerful than the individual employer. Hence the employers were driven to federate in order to be able to bargain with the trade union leaders. Apart from that, the federa- tion of employers became a necessity. By federation the employers are able to arrange many matters of advantage to the trade, such as the regulation of output. A MUTUAL EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>- - - -- And so it came to pass in the cotton trade that the great majority of the operatives were members of the trade unions, and the masters were nearly all members of the Employers' Federation. These combinations are not only inevitable and necessary. but experience has shown that they are good for both parties, for the trade as a whole, and for the community directly dependent on the industry. The operatives secure higher and more constant wages by their trade unionism, and they are given a personal. freedom which they could not enjoy if they had not the knowledge that there is a powerful organisation behind them which will protect them against unfair treatment. The federated employers gain too in greater freedom from petty strikes, and by knowing that the rate of wages they have to pay ls the same that must be paid by all their com- petitors who are in the federation. This equalises competition, and ensures an employer against sudden demands for wages advances which may not be made to his competitors in the trade. The non-federated employer is just as much an enemy of his own class as the non- unionist workman is. The latter takes the benefit of the higher wages which trade unionism has secured, without helping to maintain the organisation; and the former carries on an unfair competition against the federated employers, using non-union labour at lower wages to undersell the others. It seems, therefore, to follow as a logical and reasonable conclusion from that accepted situation (that is from the recognition of trade unionism by the federated employers, and of the necessity of federation by the employers), that the employers should welcome the efforts on the part of the opera- tives to make membership of a trade union a condition of employment. If the employers really do wish the trade unions to exist And to be strengthened they ought to put no obstacle in the way of the unions insisting that all persons working at the trade shall be members of the union. Non-unionism among the operatives encourages non-federa- tion among the employers, and that is just as disastrous for the masters as the other is for the workpeople. THE NON-UNIONIST POSITION. There is really no question involved in this cotton trade dispute of the masters being deprived of the right to employ whom they think fit. In insisting that they will not work with operatives who are non-unionists the trade unionists are simply saying: "We have come to an amicable understanding with the employers as to the rate of wages which shall be paid in this trade. We have been able to make this arrangement because both workpeople and masters have combined to bring about this common understanding. You, the non-unionist, have stood outside and refused to be a party to this arrange- ment. Therefore you have no right to come in and share in the advantage of this joint and collective settlement and we refuse to work with you." It is no more an interference with the employers' freedom to put into the conditions regulating working arrangements that only trade unionists shall be employed in a federated factory than it is to make it an obligation upon the employer that he shall pay a fixed rate of wages. There are 'few industries in the country where the work- people are better organised than in the cotton trade, and yet it is a by no means uncommon thing for employers in other trades to agree that they will employ nothing but union labour. By an agreement between the employers and the trade union, the Bradford Dyers' Combine must get their labour only from the trade union office. In the printing trade the" closed shop" is a very common</p>
        <p>thing, and in the engineering and boiler shops on the Clyde and the Tyne and the Wear, a non-unionist workman would never dream of expecting to be allowed to work alongside union men. LEGAL COMPULSION. So that it is no new demand that the Lancashire unions are putting forward. It would be no more an interference with the employers' freedom if they agreed to employ only non-unionists than it has been where the employers in other trades have agreed to do so. If the employers did agree to this, I think it would naturally follow that unionists should not take employment in non-union factories. A leader of the cotton operatives made the statement the other day that the Government ought to compel employers to engage only union labour. If if that were done it would involve much more Government interference than on that one point only. It is done in Australia, but that is only one of the details of the system of compulsory arbitration which they have in force there. That arbitration system is based on the idea of collective bargaining. The individual workman has no rights at law under this system. It is the unions only which are recognised, and it is part of the award that the employer must give prefer- ence in employment to a trade unionist. This is precisely the logical position which the Lancashire federated employers ought to adopt, and it is the only thing they can do consistently with their recognition of trade unions as the joint agency for the regulatim of wages and labour conditions. The six months interval should help to clear away some misconceptions.</p>
        <p>GOODALL S STOCKTAKING SALE NOW PROCEEDING AT • KINCT STREET • MANCHESTEV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LANCASHIRE POLICE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE POLICE.</p>
        <p>Fifty More Mounted Men.</p>
        <p>ADDITIONAL COST OF 130,000.</p>
        <p>The Home Secretary has approved a scheme sob- rented by the Lancashire Standing Joint Committee to increase the present force by fifty well-horsed police, and to recruit at Once 150 extra police to give effect to the police (Weekly Rest Day) Act. It is also proposed to prepare a list of suitable men willing to serve as constables. It. is estimated that the yearly cost of the mounted police will be about £6,500 to £7,000, and of the foot officers £15,000. The Standing Joint Committee are proposing to carry out at once, with regard to the foot officers, what will be compulsory within a period of two years. With the recent increare of pay granted to the various grades of the force it is estimated that the additional sum required for police purposes will range between £30,000 and £40,000. In formulating their scheme the Joint Committee kept in view the experience gained in the recent labour dispute, which confirmed the opinion that a small number of cavalry or mounted police are far more useful to disperse a crowd than a much larger number of foot police. Mounted men, with well-tra ned horses, can be quickly transferred to any point of disturbance and it is on these lines the Standing Joint Committee now propose to proceed. At present there are about twenty-four men trained as mounted police is the county force.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>"THE MUNICIPAL THEATRE."</head>
        <p>"THE MUNICIPAL THEATRE."</p>
        <p>Mr. Basil Dean, at his lecture on "The Municipal Theatre : Some Beginnings," at Manchester University on Wednesday afternoon, suggested that the present repertory theatre!' should not endeavour to transform themselves into municipal theatres, for which the public were both unprepared and unwilling, but should rather pave the way for this later development of theatrical enterprise. He suggested that the endow- ment of plays of known educational value would encourage municipalisation, whilst leaving the burden of financial success still upon the shoulders of theatre managers, and thereby setting up an artificial standard both of taste and production. He BRid "Is the theatre a good national investment ? If you can honestly find an affirmative to that question, then why not abide by your beliefs ? Go to your stockbroker, which is the city corporation, or the National Government, maybe, and insist that they invest at least a small portion ot your money in gilt-edged security. Do not look for your interest in pounds, shillings and pence, but, like the holder of Consols, gain comfort from the fact that the nation's humble investment'in Drama Six per Cent. Deferred is some surety for the nation's well-being.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DOCTORS REFUSED TO PASS HIM.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS REFUSED TO PASS HIM.</p>
        <p>Said Nothing But Operation Could Cure</p>
        <p>Ex-Soldier's Awful Piles.</p>
        <p>ZAM-BUK SAVES HIM FROM THE KNIFE.</p>
        <p>Before he found Zam-Buk, Mr. John Newby, though not 30, suffered terrible agony and bet a lot of money through itching and bleeding piles. Mr. Newby, who lives at 78, Evelyn-street, Deptford, London, S E., is ex-Private 6130, 2nd Essex Mounted lufantry, and served in the Boer War.</p>
        <p>" I think I contracted the piles through sitting so much in a wet saddle when in South Africa,' Mr Newby told a reporter. "On returning to England I got work in a London warehouse, where the terrible. itching made my life miserable. I gave up the ware- house work and went to sea as steward, sailing to Australia and New Zealand. During the voyages the piles bled freely, and I got into ,such a weak state that twice doctors refused to allow me to re-join my ship. They said I ought to stay on shore or undergo a surgical operation, the only possible cure for a chronic case like mine.</p>
        <p>"r lost a lot of money through my awful complaint besides never having any pleasure in life. I was at the Greenwich Seamen's Hospital for a time, and afterwards attended as out-patient at St. Mark's Hospital, City Road. I was also under different doctors, and tried in addition practically every oint- ment and salve I heard of. But the piles defied them all, and at lest there seemed nothing for it but the dreaded operation. "A ft iend, however, persuaded me to try Zam-Buk before going into hospital. I was in such great agony that my friend bought me a box of the balm straight away. The relief Zam-Buk brought me was really marvellous, and I wondered what magic remedy I had at last got hold of. A few days after beginning with Zam-Buk the bleeding had stopped, the itching wasn't half so bad, and the painful group of piles had got much less. "Steadily I persevered with Zam-Buk until all trace of the complaint that bad tortured me for seven years had gone. For many months now I have enjoyed splendid health. I feel it my duty to let other sufferers know of my wonderful Zam-Buk cure."</p>
        <p>RECOGNITION OF RAILWAY WORKERS. L. AND N.-W. MEN AND UNION OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The election of the new boards for railvvnymen under the Conciliation Act shows that on the London and North-Wcstern Railway, after the agitation for trade union recognition, oLly three sections availed them- selves of the right to appoint an outside trade union official as secretary. The other boards have appointed one of, their own nembers, and three of the boards elected Mr. W. G. Lorraine, of the A.S.R.S , Manchester district.</p>
