        <p>STATE INSURANCE AND TEMPERANCE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Chancellor Interviewed.</head>
        <p>Chancellor Interviewed.</p>
        <p>HOW THE ACT WILL MAKE FOR SOBRIETY.</p>
        <p>In the forthcoming number of the "Pioneer," the Monthly organ of the temperance department of the Wesleyan Methodist Church, is reported an interview by Mr. E. H. Lamb, M.P., with Mr. Lloyd George on he relation of the new Insurance Act to temperance reform. There is a provision in the Act by which friendly societies may make a rule that benefits may be suspended if the sickness has been induced by the misconduct of the claimant. Asked " Will drunken- ness be included within that category ? " " Why, certainly," was Mr. Lloyd George's immediate reply. "If a man were ill with delirium tremens, for instance, he could not go to his society under the Act and claim his 10s, per week." The moral is obvious— drunken- ness is penalised. Asked whether over-indulgence in alcohol would be treated as an " appointed " disease, so that sanatoria for inebriates could be erected under the provisions of the Act, Mr. Lloyd George ssid " That question ought to be addressed to the Local Govern- ment Board. It is for them to decide what are the diseases for the treatment cf which sanatoria may be Provided. They will probably make up their list from time to time, and they have full power under the Act to include any disease." The Insurance Commissioners are to issue a " compulsory form" relating to patients' habits during the period of disease or disablement. Every friendly society will have to make use of this form. " Do you think that it it is likely that any provision will be inserted in this form as to abstinence from alcohol during the period of sickness ?" " Not only likely," Mr. Lloyd George replied, " but quite inevitably in many eases. You may rest assured that under the Act the doctor will be perfectly competent to give definite instructions to his patient to refrain altogether from the use of alcohol during the period of his sickness." The amount of an employee's contributions is not directly handled by the man himself, but is deducted from his wages by his employer. Mr. Lloyd George said: "I have always looked upon that its a valuable safeguard in the case of a man addicted to drink. I think you will find that as his contribution will be deducted by his employer the balance he retains for drinking purposes will be less by that amount. You see what such amass usually does is this. If he is a heavy drinker, he probably divides up his wages and gives his wife a round sum in silver, and keeps back the rest, and the coppers —well, coppers you know, are very useful when a min is going for a drink. So the result, I think, will be that in future the insur- ance money in such cases will really come out of the amount previously spent in drink." "Will not the Act make property owners extremely careful about letting their premises to persons who by spending too much of their earnings on chink allow the properties to become insanitary? " "That is one of the effects to which I am eagerly looking forward." Mr. Lloyd George also said: "The publication of any details about social conditions cannot fail to have a very beneficial effect in fashioning public opinion respecting drunkenness, as well as other urgent problems that concern the life cf the nation." With reference to the controversy in the medical world whether alcohol can at any time or under any circumstances do any good to the human body, Mr. Lloyd George was asked, " Would the administration of the Insurance Act help to solve this problem?" He replied: "I should not think there was any limit at all to the problems that will be subjected to investigation, and this seems to be a very practical question for investigation to be presented to these eminent medical gentlemen.''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>" GENTLEMANLY " PARTY AND FREE SPEECH.</head>
        <p>THE " GENTLEMANLY " PARTY AND FREE SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Sir Harold Eiverston,M.P., and Mr. J. F. L. Brunner, M.P., were the principal speakers at a Liberal demon- stration at Northwich on Tuesday night. Sir Harold said that Northwich was the bright spot of Cheshire Liberalism. It. was curious that all Industrial Cheshire was represented by Liberals, tut Toryism, like the germs of disease, seemed to flourish In dark corners. (Applause.) The present Government (he continued) was more in line with twentieth century ideas and was the most democratic in history. The extension of the rights of the very poorest had been the guiding principles,and the extension of Ireland's liberties would come next. (Applause). Ulster Orangemen might shriek and English Unionists swear, but Liberals would not be deterred by Tory tlctics or abuse from giving popular local representative government. The "gentle- manly " party which howled down the Prime Minister were to-day trying to deny free speech to Irish Nationalists. But the substitution of violence for argument would not be a sign of advancement in our national career, and steps, be did not care how serious, must be taken to enable the Belfast meeting to be held. (Cheers.) Thirty years ago Parnell was imprisoned for much less than Sir Edward Carson had done. (Cheers). lkir.Brunner, dealing with anti-German feeling,said that It was perfectly deplorable that England and Germany, who had no cause of quarrel, should have a state of irritation between them. Every individual ought to try to find int the means of convincing Germany of our friendliness. One always suspected that at the bottom of it were the peoplei who made money out of arma- ments, building ronclads, making guns and gunpowder. Mr. Brunner also said that the reform of the Poor Law would follow the Insurance Act, and then we should get an absolute grip of poverty. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MR. HOBHOUSE ON THE THREATS OF VIOLENCE</head>
        <p>MR. HOBHOUSE ON THE THREATS OF VIOLENCE</p>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. liobhouse, Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, speaking at Bristol on Tuesday night, asked how they could expect peace abroad if there was a chance of a party coming into power who threatened civil war at home.—(A voice: " Bloff! ") Well, they never knew how fierce a fire would blaze started by a single match, and there were signs that reckless and violent people would be only too glad to set the heath on fire in more directions than one. The speeches of Conservative leaders in the Lancashire campaign con- tained language which did not permit of the Govern- ment looking on threats of violence and outbreaks with anything but a serious eye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>for</head>
        <p>for</p>
        <p>&amp;umtree</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Irmic&amp;C</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January, 27, 1912.</p>
        <p>[To be concluded].</p>
        <p>No, - Bulin-er: Don't' 'go to lciin in this mood," and while she was urging him a tele- gram was delivered for Bulmer. He opened it and gave a cry of surprise and satisfaction. "Give Bostock in custody as Edward Prit- chard, of Denver. Charge of safe robbery, Cable Denver police. It is the only repara- tion I can make you. Am leaving the Chase at once.—Caulfield."</p>
        <p>He showed it to Helen and then went in with it to Mr. Stevenson. "We'll have the police here at once," said the latter. Bostock glanced at them very uneasily; but they said nothing to him of the purport of the message, and in silence waited for tho police. "I've had enough of this," said Bostock at length, and started to leave. "You will stay here," said Bulmer, getting between him and the door; and not liking the look accompanying the words, Bostock drugged his shoulders and remained. _</p>
        <p>Jar before the police arrived a bulky letter came for Mr. Stevenson. It was from Sir Greg-I.ry, with an enclosure for Bulmer He began to read, and his look at first, anxious and pent, began to change; exclama- tions of surprise followed quickly from hi 3 lips. He looked up at Helen, and she saw that his eyes were shining brightly and joy- ously. "What is it, Bulmer?" she asked. _ _</p>
        <p>, "The best. Or almost the best. Thank God we have been wrong, Helen. Oh, my dear one," he cried in a voice half choked with emotion and with eyes glowing and faco radiant with happiness, he stretched out his hands to her. In amazement and confusion she hesitated, drawing back abashed before tho love she read in his eyes. "The police are here, sir," announced the clerk at that moment; and as Huliner seized Helen's hand and pressed it, they entered the room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>STORY.</head>
        <p>STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESBEVID.] (Copyrighted in the United States of America by Arthur W. Marchmont.)</p>
        <p>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR W. MARCHMONT, Author of " The Man Who Was Dead," " By, Right</p>
        <p>ot sword,— etc.</p>
        <p>• CHAPTER XXV. 3AXE- STEVZNS. • Deere Bulmer had recovered from the first overwhelming shock of Helen's revela- tion, some strangers came clambering on to the little plateau where they stood. Both welcomed the interruption. "Let us go," said Helen, and led the way U p to the parade, where he sat down on one of the seats and buried his face in his hands. Like a man in a dream—dazed, unsteady, utterly wretched and uncertain what to do or say—he made no reply. He was incapable for the time of coherent thought. "You will be better alone for a time, Bul- mer. Come to me to-morrow morning. I know how you are suffering, for I have been through it.' He sat as though he had not heard. He did not see her go, and when she turned pre- sently and looked back, he was still sitting in the same attitude of abject despair. The hours that followed were the blackest of his life. He remained on the sea front, oblivi- ous of everything about him ; dead to every- thing except the crushing weight of his grief, the stunning sense of infinite loss, and the sickening horror which the truth had caused. When the dark fell he returned to Man- chester and went to a hotel, where he passed a sleepless night, tortured by the ugly thoughts which came crowding into his mind when the first effects of the shock had passed. Next to the loss of the woman he loved, the part which Sir Gregory had taken dis- tressed him most. As Helen was Sir Gre- gory's daughter, the Chase would be hers. He must have known it from the' first; and yet he had concealed the fact, and had al- lowed her to live a life of poverty and drud- gery. That his father, a man whom he al- ways regarded as the very type of honour and chivalry, could be guilty of such an act, filled him with wonderment and shame. To the fact that he had lost his inheritance Bulmer did not give so much as a thought. In comparison with his grief and all the mis- erable mess of wrong that had been done, it was as nothing. The first thing to be done was to put Helen in her rightful place; and the thought of the steps to be taken gave wel- come occupation to his mind. It must be done quietly and with the avoidance of all possible scandal, for his mother's sake. Helen would be quick to see that. She had often spoken of his mother in terms of deep affection; and he knew that his mother loved her. llelen would be a great comfort to his mother in the days to come, when he himself had gone abroad. For he must go away, of course. That was inevit- able.</p>
        <p>The explanation had better take place in Manchester, and he would telegraph to his father in the morning to come there. They could then return together to the Chase, and he would make his hurried arrangements to go abroad. He was almost himself in the morning, and felt able to meet Helen for the full explana- tion of everything. He sent off the telegram early to his father, and by ten o'clock was at Helen's lodgings. "I thought it best to leave you to your- self yesterday, I3ulmer. I did right?" "Quite right. I have been able to think things out. I have come to tell you the re- sult."</p>
        <p>"I suppose it is necessary; but I—l wish you had not." __</p>
        <p>"Yes, it is absolutely necessary. I have wired to my father to come here. That also is necessary," he said, as he saw she was about to protest. "I will tell you in a sen- tence why. You are the heiress of the Chase, and you will have to go and live there." "Tim heiress of the Chase? I? Bulmerl" she cried, in profound amazement. "It is yours by right.," and he explained the provision of the settlements of the estate which gar° the property to the eldest child, whether male or female.</p>
        <p>"But I would not take it from you, But- mer," she replied instantlyl very earnestly. "I could not.' "My mother will need you, Helen." "But you?" "My mother will need you," he repeated. "And now I am going to ask something from you. A great wrong has been done you by my father—how he could do it passes my un- derstanding; but it will be sot right at once. I will not ask how you feel toward Sir Gregory; but for my mother's sake, I do ask you to meet him with as little bitterness as possible, and to let all this terrible mischief he set right quietly. I can see now how he has been punished for the wrong he has done," and he described the change in his father. "I am not seeking to excuse his con- duct. There ie unfortunately no excuse. But for the honour of our name, for--" "There is no need for another word. You have only to say what you wish, Bulmer, and I will do it." _</p>
        <p>_ "Thank you. I was sure of it," he replied with a sigh of relief. "But I would rather not meet Sir Gregory, at any rate for the present, if it can be avoided. You shake your head; but—there, I had better tell you everything and you will understand." With that she told him fully what had occurred at the Chase in her inter- view with Sir Gregory; and all her troubles in London ; the loss of her work, the engage- ment at Mr. Garwood's, the charge of theft, the visit of Sir Gregory, her flight to Man- chester, the recognition by Garwood, and the recommencement of the persecution. The story distressed him intensely, and he saw that she attributed everything to his father. "You are wrong in setting it all against Sir Gregory, Helen. You have had another enemy. I suspect it is Mrs. Caul- field's doing through a man named Bostock, an American. Do you know the name?" "No, I never heard of it. The only man in the affair besides Mr. Garwood was a Scot- land Yard detective, named Simpson." "But Scotland Yard knew nothing about it. I went there when I heard what had occurred. Can you describe this Simpson?" She described Bostock, and he recognised the description at once . . . .</p>
        <p>_ "That is Jasper Bostock .to the life. Now I am sure," he exclaimed confidently, and his look darkened. "He was acting for Mrs. Caulfield, and this fellow Garwood was in league with him." "Not Mr. Garwood, I am sure," said Helen. "Mr. Stevenson made the same mis- take when I told him." _ _</p>
        <p>"I had forgotten Mr. Stevenson," cried Bulmer. "Does he know everything? About Sir Gregory and the rest of it?" "Yes, I had to tell him," she admitted, regretting the act as she saw the expression on his face. "But he will not breathe a word to a soul," she added, with a smile. "He is the kindest friend I have ever had." "v„,, .nt Tint :in von know what he</p>
        <p>wrote to Sir Gregoryr "Wrote to Sir Gregory, Mr. Stevenson? Are you sure?" "I chanced to see part of his letter. It was that told me to look for you in Manchester." "I did not know. He prondsed to keep my secret," replied Helen, disconcerted by the news. "I cannot understand it."</p>
        <p>"Thank heaven he broke the promise, or I should have remained under the belief that you had married in America. I have not told you that;" and he described how the news of her marriage to n Californian mil- lionaire had been reported in the Pocatello paper sent to Sir Gregory. It was this Dos- tock's work, of course, at Mrs. Caulfield's instigation, to make me feel free to marry her; and, but for Mr. Stevenson's letter, the plan would have succeeded. We were to have been married in a few days' time. Thank God, I escaped that." At that moment a letter was brought to Helen. It was from Mr. Stevenson, and con- tained an urgent request for her to go to his office at once. "If Mr. Dulmer Trevellock is with you, please ask him to come lie— Very urgent," was the conclusion of the letter.</p>
        <p>In a few minutes they were at the office. Hearing their arrival, Mr. Stevenson came out of his private room, leaving the door ajar. "I have got the man who has been hounding you, Miss Powell," he said, his voice deep-toned With suppressed feeling. "We shall get to the bottom of it all now. Is this Mr. Bulmer Trevellockl" he asked, with a sharp, unfriendly glance. "Yes," said Helen.</p>
        <p>"I have had a telegram from Sir Gregory asking for news of you. It is better that you should be here to know what infernally un- derhand work has been going on." "I am afraid I know it," replied Bulmer, noticing the curt tone. Mr. Stevenson turned to Helen. "You re- member what I told you about a loafing gut- tersnipe coming here the other day, with a tale about having been sent by a stranger? It turns out to be true. The man himself came here this morning with a tale that he was a detective named Simpson, and had been sent down to see me on some unpleasant business connected with you, and he had scarcely spoken a word before the gutter tramp ar- rived, to tell me that he bad seen this Simp- son in the street, and had followed him to the office. You had better come in."</p>
        <p>They entered the room together, to find Bostock leaning against the mantelpiece, while the other stood on guard at the door which opened out to the staircase. Bostock smiled nonchalantly as he saw Bulmer and Helen. "Good morning, Mr. Trevelloa," he said jauntily. "You know this man, then?" asked Mr. Stevenson. "The name I know him by is Jasper 'nos- tock ; and for a good many reasons, which he knows, I am glad that it is here we meet," replied Bulmer significantly. 'I shall be at your service directly, but for the moment my business is with that young lady—and very unpleasant business it is. I guess she knows it, too." Before any more was said Mr. Stevenson took the other man out of the room, and, giving him some money, sent him away. As he re-entered, Bostock was explaining that he intended to charge Helen with the theft of the jewels from Mr. Garwood. It had been to take this last chance of getting rid of Helen that he had come to Man- chester.</p>
        <p>"Who sent you?" demanded Bulmer. "The person whose property was stolen." "Can you get through on the telephone to Mr. Garwood, Cathedral Chambers, St. Paul's-churchyard?" Bulmer asked Mr. Ste- venson. "We'll bottom this at once. I know this fellow is a scoundrel, but we'll have the proof of it. Wait one moment, please. There is a telegram I should like to send first. May I write it in the other office, and send your clerk with it?"</p>
        <p>It was to Mrs. Caulfield. "I know all, or nearly all. Miss Helen Paxton and I have met. Your accomplice, Bostock, has come here to prefer a charge against her of theft. You know the reason, and that now it is use- less. What do you mean to do?" "Now, Mr. Stevenson, if we telephone to this Mr. Garwood, we shall soon have the thing clear," he said, returning to the others. "If you have any regard for your father's good name, you'll call a halt, Mr. Trevel- lock," declared Bostock, with an unmistak- able threat in his tone.</p>
        <p>"This charge is Mr. Garwood's matter. We will see if he authorises it. I have a pretty shrewd idea what is at the bottom of it. And now, silence, please, until we hear from him."</p>
        <p>"But it pleases me better to talk, sir. You have often wondered why I was made so wel- come at the Chase. Your father could have told you—if he had dared." "If you dare to say a word against my father, I won't answer for the consequences to you," said Bulmer, between his teeth. "I guess I'll take the risk of that. And I guess, too, that you'd better take quietly what's coming; for it's got to come, do what you will. You must be made to understand what it is you're up against. I have your father's good name between my finger and thumb, and I have only to say a word to brink on him the vengeance of someone to whom he did about the foulest trick one man could serve another. You may scowl and look ugly, but it's the truth all the same." "You had better be careful," growled Bulmer. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Bostock shrugged his shoulders indiffer- ently. "He sent his partner, Jake Stevens, to gaol on a false charge in order that he might keep to himself a gold claim they had struck together. The plan succeeded. Jake went to gaol, and while there his wife ane child died of starvation." .</p>
        <p>"You lie !" cried Bulmer, fiercely. "I'm telling the truth. And when the man came out of prison he swore to have his old partner's life in exchange for theirs. I told Sir Gregory Trevellock what I knew, and that a word from him would bring Jake Stevens on his trail, and that little useful fact made him think it best to remain friendly with me. I can still do it, and, by God, if you try to jump me now, I will do it," he cried, with a sudden change to fierce- ness.</p>
        <p>"So you know Jake Stevens, eh?" Mr. Stevenson put the question. lie had come in while Bostock had been speaking. "And can find him, eh?" "You may gamble on that," replied Boa- tock with a sneer. "I rather gamble on your being a dirty, blackmailing liar. I am Jake Stevens." All three started in astonishment; and Bostock changed colour. "And I know you now. You are Ned Prit- chard, horse thief, crook, camp sponge, and blackguard." Before Bostock had recovered from the ad- ditional surprise of this recognition' the telephone bell rang and Bulmer and Helen went to it. A few sentences interchanged with Mr. Garwood showed that he was no party to the charge against Helen; and he declared that, if necessary, he would come at once to Manchester.</p>
        <p>"I was sure of it, Bulmer," el,c. said.</p>
        <p>"Then I'll sgtu;ro acco.unta with that blackguard. You'd better not come in with Inc.' He was very pale, and the light of rat:pa 1.:. higa mnaniTl7.</p>
      </div>
