        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THE LAW ON STOLEN GOODS. SUGGESTED IMPORTANT CHANGE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society had under consideration on Monday evening a non-party proposal affecting the law as to stolen goods. Last week the sparse attendance on the Government benches was a subject of general comment, but on Monday the positions were reversed, the Government being well represented, whilst very few Conservatives put, in an appearance. Mr. J. McDermid (deputy-Speaker) was in the chair. At question time The PRIME MINISTER (Mr.E. Wynne Jones), referring to a question put by Mr. R. Done (C., Eddisbury) on the " re-shuffling "of Cabinet appointments, said that at the last meeting the member for Eddisbury referred to the standard he (the Prime Minister) set up alit questions should always be answered when they sere asked. That standard still remained. Questions should be answered in that way, but if people asked silly and frivolous questions they must not be surprised if arrangements were not made to answer them at all. (Laughter). Mr. E. th,BottNE (1., Newcastle-on-Tyne) introduced the resolution :</p>
        <p>" That in the opinion of this House it is desirable that the law should be amended and extended so as to provide (1) that any person brought before a court of summary jurisdiction and charged with having in his possession or on his premises with Ws knowledge any goods or things which may be reasonably suspected of having been stolen or unlawfully obtained and who shall not satisfac- torily account to the Court for his possession of the same shall he liable on summary conviction to a penalty not exceeding £5, or in the discretion of the Court to be ia.prisoned with or without ' hard labour for an period not exceeding two rucnths' and (2) such goods shall be retained in the bands of the police and be conflscated,unless within a period of six years they are claimed by the true owner." He stated that according to the present law, it was no offence to have in one's possession goods of any value or description, unless there was the evidence of an actual owner to prove that the said goods had been stolen. The change asked for by the Association of Chambers of Commerce of the United Kingdom at their autumnal meeting in August last was that it should be an offence to have possession of goods, reasonably suspected to have been stolen, the possession of which could not be satisfactorily accounted for, and that such goods should be retained in the bands of the police and be confiscated unless within a period of six years they were claimed by the true owner. the great seed that existed for such a change had long been fully realized by all who had given the subject any attention, and those who should know best—the Chambers of Commerce, representing the commercial communities and the owners of property, and the police who were responsible for the protection of property, the prevention of crime, and the pursuit of offenders— were of one opinion as to the necessity for such a change. It certainly could not be said to be flattering to our boasted progress and civilization that the law of England in the twentieth century was such that If the greatest criminal in the world succeeded In stealing thousands of pounds worth of gold or silver and managed to melt it down beyond recognition, he could defy the law for all time, and it would be useless to touch him. This was doubt- less due to the fact that, at Westminster, commercial and business men had so very little to do with the actual making of our laws. It was, of course, very different in the Warrington Parliament. (Laughter and applause.) Local Acts of Parliament had been passed, embodying the provisions asked for, and were now in force in Liverpool, Manchester, Glasgow, Iluddersfield, Salford, Oldham, Birkenhead and Bootle. During the last twenty years, however, such powers had not been allowed by Parliament in any local Acts. It was exceed- ingly difficult to believe that anyone who had any regard for common honesty and the protection of property could, by any process of reasoning, bring him- self to object to such an amendment of the law as "wee suggested. A great many well-meaning people made a fetish of the alleged principle of law that ' A man is innocent until he is proved guilty," just as they did of the other saying " An Englishman's house is his castle." For all practical purposes these were to a large extent merely empty phrases. Those who felt concerned lest our English police system should degenerate to the level of that of !thesis or the Continent might dismiss all anxiety from their minds, as the new provisions would not give the police any authority which they did not already possess, but con- ferred extended powers only on the justices. To explain the practical value of the suggested legisla- tion to a town like Warrington, it was only necessary to instance the large manufactories which made exten- sive use of block tin, speller, brass, lead, &amp;c. The first two metals were very expensive and by reason of being easily melted down, identification was impossible. The metal, though heavy, was readily portable. It was therefore comparatively easy for thieves to enter works in the night-time and commit thefts. Cases of that kind had arisen and the police had afterwards found in the possession of a convicted receiver of stolen rretal, what they bad every reason to believe was the actual metal re-melted. Identification was absolutely impossible and the police were powerless. We heard ouch nowadays about the rights of property and the attacks upon capital, and yet we allowed the common thief to exact his heavy toll with practical impunity, whilst his victims were rated and taxed almost to breaking point. Certain traders might fear being hit by the amendment of the law, They could, however, rest assured that only through gross negligence or wilful carelessness in the conduct of their business could they be affected. Mr. Osborne contended that the interference, so called, with the liberty of the subject would, in this case, be for the public good. The present state of the law protected an army of professional thieves and enabled them under certain conditions to become re-possessed of stolen property. The resolution was not a party measure but a simple, businesslike proposi- tion, and it was difficult to hnderstand upon what definite principle of social rights it could be denied. Mr. E. A. SANDERS (L., iSt. Helens) seconded the resolution._</p>
        <p>Mr. J. JARMAN (L., Burton-on-Trent), in moving that the resolution be deferred for six months, asked why, if the proposed amendment would confer no new powers on the police, there should be any need for it ? However, it had been pointed out that under the law as it stood now, the police might arrest but the justices would not convict. Practically speaking, it was the business of the police to obtain convictions and not simply to arrest people. At present the obligation rested upon the police to prove unlawful possession, and he contended it was only reasonable that they should do so before a conviction was made. " Reasonable suspicion " was open to a very broad construction, and nobody would be safe if arrests could be effected on such lines. Would the new law apply to land-grabbing ? (Laughter.) In seemed wide enough to cover .even that and he bad been wondering whether, in the case of wrongful ' enclosure of land, the rightful owner could, if be put in en appearance within six years, be able to get it back again and obtain a conviction. (Laughter.) He did not know that convictions were a good thing without &amp;finite proof, and at present proof involved identification of the goods. After all, if a man accused another of having stolen his watch, the least he could do was to prove that the watch in the suspected thief's possession belonged to the prosecutor —not merely that he had a" reasonable suspicion " that it was his. With regard to the suggested con- fiscation of goods, what i hullabaloo there would have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>"I Said DOAN'S</head>
        <p>"I Said DOAN'S</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. Howard, who lives at 3, O'Leary Street, Orford Lane, two doors from the Orford Barracks, and opposite the Beamont Schools, Warrington, says :—" It was a cold that brought on my kidney trouble. It settled in the kidneys, end began to suffer with cruel pains in the small of my back. After Etooping it was a great struggle for me to get upright again. In bed I could hardly turn over. Often I cried out with the pain.</p>
        <p>"I hoird that Doan's backache kidney pills were a good kidney medicine, co I obtained some and started to use them. It was not lontr before my back felt easier, and a course of the pills soon put me right again. " I am quite free from the pains now, and have lost that drowsy feeling I used tl have all the time. I shall always speak well of Dose's pills. (Signed) (Hrs.) E. Howard."</p>
        <p>Over 5 years later, Mrs. Howard said :—" derived lasting benefit from Doan's pills. I have often told my friends how much good they did me. I have occasionally used the pills since I gave my testimonial, when I have had a touch of backache; and they have always given me relief."</p>
        <p>been if Mr. Lloyd George had dared to propose such a thing ! (Laughter.) Undue importance should not be attached to the fact that a long list of Chambers of Commerce bad approved the suggested change. Mr. WATSON (Soc., E. Birmingham) characterized the proposal as the most reactionary one ever brought before the House. It was one of the beauties of the English law that a man was innocent until be was proved guilty, but the now law absolutely reversed the position and made him guilty until he could prove his innocence. " Many of us," he continued, " may have some property which the police may think we are not entitled to, and unless we could prove we had got it by honest methods, the best of us would be in danger of being locked up and having our goods confiscated." (Laughter.) The whole of our laws emanated from our landed aristocrats and the Universities. They were for the defence of property rather than for the protection ef human rights. A man could half kill someone and escape with a fine of 7s. 6d, but if be stole an apple he might get three years. Was it proposed to give compensation to a person who was wrongfully detained? If spoor man broke a window to call attention to his being out of work he got twelve months but if a suffragette did the same she was dismissed with a caution. He supposed that was political action. (Laughter). Mr. M. HOWARTH (Home Secretary) said he came to the meeting opposed to the resolution, but after hear- ing the two preceding speeches he was in favour of it. (Laughter.) The member for Burton-on-Trent seemed to think it was a frightful thing to call upon a man to prove how he o flle into possession of goods suspected of having been stolen. It was not, however, asking too much, as it should be a simple matter for a man to show that he had obtained the property by honest methods. The present system had many weak- nesses. In 1909, 712,000 persons were brought before courts of summary jurisdiction on the evidence of the police, and 85,000 or 12 per cent, were dismissed because their cases could not be proved. It was a serious thing for any individual to be brought before a court of law. Even if he were innocent, 4 stigma seemed to remain. There was a danger of an officious or inexperienced policeman, whose desire for promotion was greater than his sense of judgment, arresting people who were perfectly innocent. He did not, however, think that such cases were likely to happen very often. Mr.C. E. PARKER (L., Dundee), said the Associated Chambers of Commerce passed a resolution some time ago in favour of tariff reform, and ever since then he had lost entire confidence and interest in them. (Laughter.) The present resolution was not very clear in its meaning. Was the" reasonable suspicion "supposed to be exercised by the buyer of materials, the police, or the justices, before action could be taken ? It some- times happened that a man who was constantly purchas- ing certain classes of goods might come into possession of property believed to have been stolen, and yet be able to show that he had acquired it in a legitimate way. Would he, under those circumstances, have to forfeit the goods? The resolution seemed to involve a departure from recognized principles of our law and it should receive very careful consideration.</p>
        <p>The PRIME MINISTER (Mr. E. Wynne Jones) said he was sorry the bulk of the debate was devolving upon the Ministerial side of the House. The Opposition were strong in their complaints the previous week when the Chancellor of the Exchequer was the only Minister present, but that night there was not a single representative on the Opposition front Bench. The Warrington Parliament led the Imperial House of Commons in many ways and if the police desired new legislation their best course was to get it approved by the Debating Society and it would no doubt find its way to the statute book in due course. (Laughter.) He would like some proof to be given that the suggested change bad had a deterrent effect in places where it had already been enforced. Why had not Parliament granted similar powers to local authorities since 18e2? He supposed every member of that House was well-known to the police— (hear, hear)—so there would not be much danger of any of them being taken into custody. (" question " and laughter.) There should be no fear about granting extra powers to the police. At present a nan might admit he had stolen a particular thing and snap his fingers at the police. If they were unable to trace the owner- ship of goods, the prisoner was discharged and took the property with him. Incidentally, the Premier said he was glad the Warrington magistrates had risen to the occasion for once, and the way they proposed to deal with the licensing question did them credit. They could count upon the support of that aide of the House in the action they had taken. Mr. E. A. SANDERS (L., St. Helens) and Mr. H. E. IlawnionNE (C., Belfast) supported the resolution. The CHIEF CONSTABLE (Mr. M. Nicholls) charaterized much of what had been said in opposition to the proposal as" piftle. " The police already had power to arrest a suspicious character. If they bungled matters and did something without reasonable and just cause, the aggrieved party could lake civil action against them. All they asked was twit when no owner of stolen property could be traced, the magistrates should have poa er to punish a man for being in unlawful posession unless he could give a satiafactory acc)unt of it. For twenty-six years he the (speaker) had been associated with the police and ho believed very few people experienced undue hardships at the hands of the force. If they did, they generally managed to make money out of it (laughter). The non-granting of powers on the lines suggested to local authorities of recent years was largely due to the fact that when Corporations promoted Bills their London agents generally advised them to omit such a contentious clause, for fear of jeopardising. other parts of the</p>
        <p>its a Story.</p>
