        <p>measure. Parliament also hesitated to grant locj powers on questions that should be dealt with in national Act. If either party had been likely to capital out of the question, an Act would have be,, passed long ago. M to the "reasonable auspiolon.,o the police would invariably have to take the initiall°,( unless some other person of stability drew thof attention to a case. The justices would, _lf, course, have to decide whether there wri cause to convict. The member for Burton lite absolutely wrong in his supposition that it was t'• duty of the police to obtain convictions. It Nolo, matter of indifference to them and rested entirely IV Is the magistrates. The police, of course, natnrally that. freq us ntly persons who should be punished did ov, get all that they deserved. The fact that a person t discharged did not always mean that he was not There were 581 cases of drunkenness in WarrinS, during the past twelve months, and about 55 vr_,e'r dismissed. Of those, be did not sappose that 5 r cent were cases that had not been proved. Mr. llowannt If a man pleads guilty, is not tba sufficient evidence on which to convict ? ~„ et• The CHIEF CONSTABLE : Not so far se this oP- ment is concerned. Yu must establish ownership. „e Mr. OSBORNE, in replying to the debate, said object of the resolution was to prevent dishonest' far as possible. Ile hoped that the House would linc'tO it was composed of business men who were prepared ; do what they thought WM right towards a ionsitlem community. Mr. Osborne tlnslly expressed, indebtedness to the Chief Corstable and 4-1:i Inspector Williamson for the readiness with which LD" bad Aff,rded him information. The resolution was passed without a division.</p>
