        <p>Hatton. ~,EDT)ING CELEBRATION.—We regret that the corn- g_Mentary paragraph under this heading last week c "Id have caused any annoyance to the parties oncerned by being premature.</p>
        <p>Thelwall. r at ' IRE GUARD.-Mr. T. Ridgway held an inquest ,„Thelwall, on Monday,touching the death of Percy ftlward Miller. aged 18 months, the son of Herbert ;Tiller, of Wash-lane' Thal wall. The evidence abowed !pat at noon on Friday the mother was cleaning the 4replace, and in her absence at the coalhouse the child fot to the fire and was badly burned, dying on Saturday fir.cqu the injuries received. There was no guard to the `e• A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>Burtonwood. fire occurred on Monday awning at a barn premises of Mr. Alexander Broomfield, green- general [dealer, of Brook Vale Cottage, virtlrtTrivood. A message was despatched for the se-rnwgton Fire Brigade which, under the command of 'pageant Cook, was quickly on the scene. The burning eZ was situated between two cottages. The brigade dued the flames in an hour, but one half of the Ucture and the whole of the contents were destroyed. 44s damage was not covered by insurance.</p>
        <p>Padgate. JOFLC-NERAL—The funeral of the late Mr. William h.,"rton took place on Saturday last. The service was hewe"4o in the Wesleyan Church where the deceased had tf choirmaster for a number of years. Rev. E. iii„iprgan was the officiating minister, and at the conclu- of the service Miss Bennett played the Dead March. rL c°l-tege then proceeded to the Warrington Cemetery heere the interment took place. Representatives of aWk 0 Church and choir were present, and but for the wardness of the hour and a misunderstanding as to Co , there would certainly have been a much larger V: The deceased gentleman had been 3Ployed by Messrs. Garnett, of Warrington, and their ditl_dlY treatment of him during his protracted illness 4,,e5 credit alike to employer and employe. Among 6."6,r8, Wreaths were sent by Mr. R. E. Garnett and the the Wesleyan Church,Pedgate. i ChotinM°RlAL SERVICE.—The tragic coincidence of the of aster and organist dying withinwithinweek - a th easekil°ther, lent to the memorial seivice helot in ri'leBefeSan Church on Sunday evening a double Oak e. The Rev.,E. Morgan CMS the preacher, and /alley text " Yea thoagh I walk through the r.... 0 the shadow of death I will fear no evil " The e'evatgleentleman spoke with feeling about the deceased Short h' and with respect to Mr. W. Johnston, gave in rt history of his life, which went to show that n Zaal parts of the country the late choirmaster had ci:/ed himself a loyal and devoted Methodist. Mr liYaral7ol7,, Presided at the organ and played the " Dead WELET Guruo.—The Rev. E. Morgan proved a ,T„alar lecturer with a podular lecture to the members Guild assembled on Monday evening. The sub- .i.e?t was the Methodist celebrity " Billy Bray." As 'Light be expected the lecture was full of humour and ' aPeakers beside Mr. Morgan were able to draw on sother "a almost inexhaustible mine for humorous and telling reeudotes. Mr. Roscollor presided and Messrs. John „ nnett, Wright and Duckworth were responsible for vote of thanks which was enthusiastically endorsed. , Loris, r, tfol - BREATII•LONG OPPOSITION TO up. 114 RrLt.—Addressing a Tory meeting at Padgate, Church School on Thursday night, Lord Weimer, Said he was proud to be a member of the Unionist .1.7t,Y, and as long as he had breath in his body be would isist the proposal to force Home Rule on Ireland and el:Wand without a mandate from the people of the h°4lstry, The question of Home Rule had been , 0 ore the people in 1886 and 1898, and on liith th ose occasions the people condemned h- ang , opinion it was nothing but fraud th Lrieker to pass that measure behind the backs of Rl:t:e°Pl.. e eithont a mandate or the opportunity of a .withrendurn. Be said he deplored any interference rese,Athe right of free speech ; but with regard to the Ulster, he thought their position was a Gex,""aule one. The people of Ulster said that if the tur ernmett were going to hand them over to the epee,Y of their hereditary enemies and foist home Rule clod' them it was an electioneering trick and a political tleie/ and they would rather fight than submit. In oh attitude Ulster had the authority of the greatest Ileentile.ti,°as of political liberty.. (Applause.) Alderman Occupied the chair, and votes of thanks were LOndel. 00 the proposition of Messrs. Mather and „TEN -1 q AND BOWLING CM:IL—BOWLING SECTIos ing 1 he above club held tilt ir annual social even. lijiltfe "21 Thursday evening when the members were rd hY the Bowls Committee to partake of a knife tea. The members were also invited to bring extriaale friend. From this social ladies are rigorously hasiVed, the members of a sub-committee lag, -8 all the arrangements in band, select- Visad Purchasing, and cutting up the numerous and 0.8. The tables were very tastefully displayed gath—„,..ll:3ad (elite a flutter of excitement amongst a large ' there:Te 01 ladies who had previously been regaling thenallven in another part of the building, trimly of diem, °°hin" longingly at the dainties