      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Penketn.</head>
        <p>Penketn.</p>
        <p>- L ORD WOLNIER'S VISIT.—The Newton Divison Con. sore ative and Unionist Association held a public meeting in the Wesleyan School, Penketh, on Tuesday evening, when Viscount Wolmer addressed this meeting. speaking on this Insurance Act he said that the Bill should never have been made a party question but he could quite understand the opposition to the Bill because it had been sprung upon the electors. He contended that the manner in which this Government guillotined the Bill was nothing but a scandal and Mr. Lloyd George bad no one to blara3 but himself,for the Bill ra,)t being popular. He also dealt at Same length on Home Rule and the Veto Bill.—Mr. J. Fulton Smith was chairman. The Corservative party agent, for the Newton Division Mr.W. A. Mawdesley was also on the platform. ST. MARY'S MISSION, WONIEN'S GUILD—The Women's Guild held their annual social on Thursday evening of last week at the Infant's School, Penketh, which had been very nicely decorated by the com- mittee. The social took the form of a tea and enter- tainment and was certainly very much enjoyed by the 90 members or more of the Guild who put in appear- ance. Afor partaking of tea, the party were provided with an excellent entertainment. The Rev J R Jones (president of the Guild) presiding The first item on the programme was a pianoforte solo by Miss Murray. Songs were very creditably rendered by the worthy Vicar, Miss Davies, Miss Jones, and Miss Murray, and Mr. W. H. Ireland, a reading was listened to with interest, given by Mrs. T. Owen, also a recitation by Miss M. Murray. Humorous songs were given by Mr. W. Moores assisted by Mr. B. Smith, of Walton. Mrs. Fieldbloor also gave her usual interest- ing performance of Scotch dance and Irish jig. Young girls from the Council school assisted in the programme which was much enjoyed by the company. The ladies who presided over the tables were as follows :—Misses Tinsley, M, Tinsley, Jeans, and Mrs. Owen.</p>
        <p>Lymm. URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The monthly meeting of the Lymm District Council was held at the Council Offices, on Thursday evening. There were present :—Mr 0 0 Smith in the chair, Messrs W G Groves,J Holt, F Lockyer, T H Warburton, R H Gotobed,S Davtes,WMullard (clerk and collector), W L Donaldson, the Rev G B Thurston, Dr Edward Meyer de Jong (medical officer of health), and D Mort (surveyor). -----</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lockyer stated that the balance in the bank on the general account was £683 Gs 71 cheques that night amounted to £570 IBs 6d, leaving a balance of £ll2 8s Id. On the gas account they had a balance in the bank of £622 18s, cheques that night amounting to £386 Os Bd, leaving a balance of £236 17s 4d.</p>
        <p>THE SiWERACE SCFIEME. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on January 9th a letter was read from the Local Govern- ment Board approving of the proposed sewerage scheme in general, but asking fur certain alterations and amendments.</p>
        <p>The Council were recommended to approve of the engineer's report and further estimated that in order to carry out the alterations and requirements the clerk he instructed to apply to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow the sum of £9,465 instead of 0,000 as at first stated in September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LAND P U RC-II A SES.</head>
        <p>LAND P U RC-II A SES.</p>
        <p>It was also recommended that the Council purchase from Sir H. W. Lever the piece of land containg about 38 perches in the Meadows for £4O and also to purchase from Mr 3 W Fox a piece of land in the same meadows containing acre for £7s,snd from the executors of Mr. George Dickenson a piece of land in the same ground containing two thirds of an acres for £75 and further that the Clerk take the necessary steps to transfer the land to the Council ,including that of Mr. Ross, for which provisional agreement for purchase had already been entered into. Further that Mr T Ridgway,solicitor of Warrington he instructed to act and carry out the purchase for the Council and also to act in connection with the agreement between the Railway Company and the Canal Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NEW STABLES.</head>
        <p>NEW STABLES.</p>
        <p>It was decided to advertise for tenders for the build- of stables in the yard adjoining the Council Offices in accordance with the plans and specifications prepared by the Surveyor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NEW GAS MAIN</head>
        <p>NEW GAS MAIN</p>
        <p>It was resolved to prepare estimates for a six inch main for the Dog Bridge, Oughtrington to Lymm lane crossing via Sandy-lane. CANAL BOATS Mr. Mort read his report of inspection of canal boats which was approved. CONSERVATIVE CIA'S LECTIIRR.-Mr. W. Gl. Lecomber gave a lecture on The Cathedrals of England " illustrated by lantern slides at the Conserva- tive Club on Tuesday last. There was a good audience and the lecture was much enjoyed.</p>
        <p>DE kTII OF MRs. R. GILLENDER.—We regret to have to announce the sudden death of Mrs. Gillender, wife of the Rev. Robert Gillender, formerly of Lymni, but latterly of Milfleld, Northumberland, which occurred on Friday, the 12th inst. The interment took place on the following Tuesday at Whiekham-on-Tyne.</p>
        <p>LYNIM LAD'S CLI:B.—The welcome visit of the Mionehaha Atuatcur Minstrels on Wednesdly last attracted a large audience, the drill Hall being well filled. The programme was one of exceptional merit, many of the items evoking enthusiastic applause. The concert was in aid of the above club which must have benefited substantially. rnE PRIMITIVE METHODISTS.—The Sunday evening Lantern Services in the Primitive Methodist School- room, continue to attract good audiences, the room being quite filled on the 21st inst. "Bobs, of Oxford " was the story of the evening, and its recital was closely followed by the audience. Miss Edwards gave an artistic rendering of " The Children's Home," to the accompaniment of Mr. H. Holt. Mr. W. T. Moss read the descriptive matter, and Mr. C. Moss operated the lantern.</p>
        <p>DENTIT Or Mn. A. SWANWICN.—The news of the death of Mr. Alfred Swanicick came as a great shock to his host of friends in Lymm. The sad event occurred Et his present residence, Linnet-lane, Liverpool, on Monday. The deceased gentleman retired to rest apparently in the best of health, and after having spent a particularly pleasant evening with his family. Within an boor he was found unconscious in his dressing room and shortly afterwards passed away. The funeral took place on Thursday. For many years Mr. Swanwick was associated will' Lymtn and though he took a prominent part as a Liberal made many friends on both sides.</p>
        <p>THE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.— The Congrega- tionalists held their annual social on Monday evening. An address was given by Rev. Mr. Ayton, sofigs were rendered 43y Mrs. Lewis and Miss Evans. Other musical items were given and refreshments partaken of. Business matters also receiving attention. An elder scholars tea was held on Tuesday, after which the Sunday School prizes were distributed by Mrs Thomas; followed l)y musical and other items by the scholars, after which games were played. Tei and games were provided on Wednesday evening for the younger scholars. The centre of attraction was the Christmas Tree.</p>
        <p>-•--- SERIES OF aAT HOMES."—The ladies of the Primitive Methodist Church were not wanting in coura,v.e when they undertook to give an "At home " extending over three consecutive evenings but the result amply justified their confidence, and the unqualified success of the function is now a matter of history. How a prosaic schoolroom can be transformed into a sumptious drawingroom, and its adjoining class rooms msianite s nab o retreats,cousy leo t grapple ; and where all the curtains, carpets, pictures, lounges, Ste., essential to the illusionment came from, supplies a further insoluble problem, but all these difficulties vanished before the ' energy of the ladies and et seven o'clock on Wednesday evening the transformation was complete. The guests were prodigal in their exprcsaions of approval, and the many kind remarks made each evening could not but have been , pleasant hear ng to those who had worked so hard for succes4. The Wednesday evenings programme included sprigs by Miss Dudley, contralto, of Bolton, which were very favourably received," The silver ring " being deservedly encored ; Mr. Pownall, bass, of Warrington. also received an encore for his solo" The Skipper." Miss Moston gave several recitations in her inimitable style. Mr. Burrell rendered several violin selections. Miss Mabel and Mr. F. Champ also played duets, the latter being the accompanist for the evening. A side show of living wax works under the direction of Mesdames Priestner and Cross were well pitronised and highly appreciated A feature of the evening was a gipsy dance, song, and drill, by sixteen amateur -zingaris. Much time had evidently been Spent in the preparation of this item which was performed in finished style, the picturesque drestes and rhythmic gyrations being greatly admired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Exaramer. Saturday, January, 27, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Exaramer. Saturday, January, 27, 1912.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ANTI-HOME RULERS. STORMY MEEIING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>An Anti-Home Rule meeting at Earlestown Town Hall on Monday night brought together a very mixed audience._ . . _</p>
        <p>The advent of the speakers and principals was the signal for a great demonstration, in which cheers were mixed with jeers and cat-calls. Mr. George Cave, K.C., M.P., said that no matter what the intentions of the Government might be, they could do absolutely nothing without submitting the matter once more to the judgment of the electors.</p>
        <p>. - There was much interruption, the speaker being submitted to a running fire of questions, while audible comments on parts of the speech were continually pre- sented in a high-pitched female voice. The introduction by the speaker of Mr. Devlin's name led to an outburst , of applause, which was kept up for a considerable time, and when it subsided the Chairman reminded the audience that Mr. Devlin was given a patient hearing on his recant visit to Earlestown. (Applause). Such cries as" What about South Africa?" "What's Kitchener ? " and many other noises not quite so di s - tinct made it impossible for Mr. Cave to proceed for some time. Eventually he was able to refer to the action of the Ulster Unionists, who, he said, were in deadly earnest—they wanted to be subjects of one United Kingdom, and they wanted the benefit of the British Government. Supposing these men stuck to their word, and he had no doubt they would, did they think they would get British troops to fire on them for being loyal to their c mutry ? He (the speaker) had been told by friends who attended the recent meeting of Ulster Unionists that they there saw a sight which they would never forget. Interruptions were continuous, and the speaker could only proceed at disjointed intervals, lie concluded by moving a resolution against Home Rule. Lord Wolmer, M.P. for the Division, was similarly received, but lies restored order for a few moments. During his speech, however, he was interrupted and heckled just as furiously. He said their complaint was that Home Rule was being rushed through without the people of the country being consulted about it. They talked about majority rule—he asked for the rule of the majority in the United Kingdom. The resolution was received with a mixture of cheers and jeers, and it was not clear whether it was carried. LABOUR WOMEN CONFERENCE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire branches of the Women's Labour League held their delegate conference in Earlestown on Saturday afternoon. There were about fourteen branches represented, Mrs. Simm (National Executive) presiding. Resolutions were carried protesting against secret diplomacy, welcoming the growing discontent amongst the workers and congratu- lating the women on having stood by the men, protest- ing against the use of the military in industrial dis- putes, protesting against unnecessxry industrial dirt in the streets and homes of the people, and calling upon the Government to provide legislation requiring employers to provide suitable cleansing facilities for the workers. Other resolutions, dealing with ths care of children and school clinics, were discussed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MR. J. A. SEDDON AND THE ULSTERMEN.</head>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDON AND THE ULSTERMEN.</p>
        <p>Speaking at a demonstration held on Saturday night, M. J. A. Seddon (ex-M.P. and prospective Lab)u r candidate for the Newton division), referred to the question of Home Rule and the present Bslfast dispute. If the people of Belfast were going to make free speech impossible in Belfast, then, according to the rules of justice, no anti-Home Rule meeting could be held either in England, Scotland, or Wales. Home Rule had no more to do with religion than potatoes with poetry. These Ulstermen were going to prove their religion by breaking heads and threatening violence and bloodshed. Wriat crimes were wrought in the name of religion Home Rule was not a religious question, but a question of the ascendancy of a minority, and it was an economic question. Belfast stood disgraced by the action of Lord Londonderry and the Unionist Committee. When once the national aspiration of Irishmen had been con- ceded industrial democracy would stand closer together throughout the British Isles. LORD NEWTON'S CROAKINGS.</p>
        <p>ceded</p>
        <p>The Newton Division Conservative Association made a departure on Saturday when for the first time the quarterly meeting of the executive was held at St. Helens. Lord Newton presided at the meeting, at the George-street Rooms, being supported by Lord ‘Volmer, M.P., Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, W. Collingwood, A. Smith, W. N. Pilkington, Guy Pilkington, and repre- sentatives of all the polling districts in the division. Lord Newton, after the business had been disposed of, addressed the meeting, and said the political outlook appeared to him to be of a gloomy, despondent nature, and almost the only thing they could feel any satisfac- tion about at all was the fact that the unpopularity of the present Government was undoubtedly increasing. With regard to foreign affairs we had now been drawn into the European system. This great change meant that we should no longer be able to muddle along with our antiquated military system, but for national defence every able-bodied man in the country would in future have to be trained, as was the practice in every country in Europe. In regard to matters at home, the outlook in Ireland was not much more cheerful. They were apparently not far removed from something approaching civil war in Irel,nd. It might appear to some people as indefensible that the Unionist party had seen fit to announce that they would not allow Mr. Winston Churchill and his friends to hold a Home Rule demonstration in the Ulster Hall, but it was not only the question of the demonstration but of the two principal speakers, Mr. Churchill and Lord Pirrie, two of the most prominent turncoats in politics at the present moment, and it was really asking people a great deal when they asked them to extend their generosity and toleration to political turncoats of that description. At home the outlook was equally gloomy, because they were threatened by the Socialists to what thqy were pleased to call civil war. He did not think anyone had pointed out that what was called social unrest, but to which they might fairly apply a much stronger term, had followed upon the heels of those two great measures which were to crown the edifice of Liberal legislation—the People's Budget and the Parliament Bill. But it was obvious that the Govern- ment recognised that they were going downhill. Among the steps they ct ere taking to make their fall as light as possible was to promise to give a vote to everyone who attained the age of twenty-one. That brought them to the question of female suffrage. For his party he felt twit the women had every reason to be dissatisfied not only with the Government, but with the House of Commons generally. Lord Newton contrasted the differing views of Ministers on the ouestion of woman suffrage, and prophesied further trouble for the Government. Lord Weimer, M.P., said that it was obvious that the Liberal Government was on tha eve of breaking up. It was only the crack of the party whip which kept them together, and it would be interesting to students of the Br tish Constitution to see how far party dis- cipline would be effective in preventing the breakii3g of the rotten walls of the present governmental edifice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION (NORTHERN ANGLERS CENTRE.)I Owing to the heavy fall of snow we had last week anglers were w 11 advised to spend their week end at home as angling in the Gowey turned ont a failure. However it is now rf aching the season when the speci- men that heads the catch list is registered and the anglers who brave the winter weather will undoubtedly be found amongst the winners at the end of the season. The date for the official match is August 18th, this date falling between the opening match and the annual match and brought forward about a month earlier tlym last years match, it being thought that at the last meeting that the small attendance of 1911 was through the event being left too late in the season. Societies of the Northern Anglers have not received their travelling cards up to time of writing. Anglers wishing to travel must use their old cards until the new ones are distributed which will be as early as possible. Members must also be aware that the rates of travelling have been raised, but to what amount, until the cards arrive, we are unable to say. The !next club Light, February 12th, will be altered to a general meeting as the business is of a most important nature and it will be to the advantage of members to attend.</p>
        <p>Warrington Sunday School Union. . A most successful gathering of Sunday School Teachers was held last Saturday in Wycliffe Memorial Hall. when demonstrations of Primary Methods were given by members of the Liverpool Primary Associa- tion. The Rev. J. Yonge presided over a well- attended meeting in the afternoon. In his address he pointed out how greatly reform was needed in Sunday school methods if the schools wete to be made attractive to elementary school children who were now being taught on such highly instructive and attractive lines. Miss Lang of Seaforth, with children from the Primary Department of Silver-street Wesleyan Sunday School who bad been trained by Mr. C. Sinclair, very ably demonstrated the use of the Cradle Roll, the Birthday Card the Archibald Bank, the Art of Story- telling, and the expression lesson. For an hour she held the rapt attention of the children and audience. At the close of the demonstration, Miss Crosbie of Liverpool answered questions arising out of the methods used. After tea the teachers again assembled The President, Rev. W. Reid was in the chair. Miss Crosbie delivered an enthusiastic address on "The Advantages of Primary Methods:' With a group of young Sunday school teachers Miss Tate, Liverpool, gave a demonstration of a Primary Teachers Training Class which was followed by a well-sustained discussion on Primary Methods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CRUSHED TO DE COALMINER'S PERILOUS WORK.</p>
        <p>The inquest on William Wareing, aged fifty-four, of 21, Silkstone.street, St. Helens, was held on Tuesday. Wareing had been employed for two weeks at the Ravenbead mine of Messrs. Pilkington's collieries. He was an experienced collier. During the night of Thursday last he was working with Charles Barnes, his drawer, and at 3.50 on Friday morning both men were suddenly struck down by a large piece of stammel, which fell from the roof of the coal and pinned Wareing down. Barnes was knocked flying, and so saved. The stone had to be broken before it could be taken off the man. -</p>
        <p>A fireman named Follen said he was in the place five or six minutes before the accident. There was a sprag under this stammel. VVitnees heard the fall and went to the place. The roof was wet at this place, and from the noise he thought there had beea a sudden " gulch " or slip, which brought the stone down. Witness had passed under this place and sounded the itone with his hammer just before. Wareing himself set the sprag, which he thought was sufficient to keep the place safe. A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>i bell</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>A handicap competition took place last Friday evening, on the miniature range at the Drill Hall. The conditions were, ten shots and three sighting shots, at a decimal target, at twenty-five yards; unlimited entries; gross totals of 100 to shoot off. At the con- clusion it was found that Messrs C H Page and J L Shepherd had to shoot off for the first and second places. Five rounds eaoh were shot at a decimal target, with the following result : JL. Shepherd, 47; C H Page, 25- Below are the individual scores:— Score. Wcap. Total. J L Shepherd (Ist prize) 93 12 ... 100 CII Page (2nd prize) E Howard (3rd prize) A Dovin S James C Benniou J W Plinston J Towers E Pollard 95 ... so ... 95</p>
        <p>A Shepherd 95 —. so —. 93 C Libbiter</p>
        <p>NATIONAL RIFLE ASSOCIATION* MONTHLY COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The third of a series of five monthly competitions, organized by the National RilleAssociation, took place, by permission of the officials of the Whitecross Club, on their range on Tuesday evening last. The con- ditions were :—A term of six men ; ten shots at a decimal target, at twenty-five yards ; possible score per man, 100; the centre of the shot-hole to determine the value of the shot ; no sighting shot a Bowed. Mr S James, the hon. secretary of the Whitecross Club, acted as range official. Below are the individual and total scores:—</p>
        <p>A Shepherd 93 F C Bennion A Down __ __ _. .</p>
        <p>E Pollard 92 C P Day J Bramhall 552</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WHEN DOCTORS ARE DEAF.</head>
        <p>WHEN DOCTORS ARE DEAF.</p>
        <p>WHEN A DOCTOR IS AILING he has the opportunity of visiting his pro- fessional brethren quite free of any fee. It follows, therefore, that a Doctor suffering from any disease or affliction would only go to the recognised authorities on the particular form of trouble he has. The affliction of deafness to anyone in the medical profession is terrible to contemplate. It means a life's study and labour lost. No one wants a deaf Doctor, as he is at such a great disadvantage. This also applies to all other professional men, business men and women, and general workers,no matter what their calling may be. The race for life's success and the keen competition calls for the highest efficiency of every faculty. Dr. M. L. H L.R.C., L.M., suffered from catarrhal deafness, accompanied with head noises, and had visited every well- known specialist all over the world, and was in despair of any benefit and with the dread of being obliged to give up his practice. His own profession having failed to give him relief, he turned his attention to electricity and scientific instruments, and ultimately investigated the merits of Mr. Philip V. Summer's Akou-Vibra Invention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DOCTOR INVESTIGATES.</head>
        <p>THE DOCTOR INVESTIGATES.</p>
        <p>The Doctor wrote for particulars and sent for a machine on trial and hire. His own words written out of a spirit of gratitude best express his verdict:—</p>
        <p>THE DOCTOR'S OPINION.</p>
        <p>"My ear trouble is of 11 or 12 years' duration, and has gradually become worse. My left ear is practically useless, and the hearing in my right ear is only about half as acute as that in a normal ear. My case is a pure one of middle ear catarrh with gradually increasing deafness, accompanied by constant noises (tinnitus aurium). For such cases your Akou-Vibra instrument seems to have been specially designed, and as far as my judgment goes, I think from both an anatomical and physiological point of view the treatment is scientific, rational and safe—scientifical and rational because the electrical massage and vibrations are designed to act upon the drum and through it on ossicles (or bones) of the middle ear, all of which structures are ' affected in this form of deafness; and safe because of an ingenious device, there is no danger of electrical shock in using it. "In conclusion, I am glad to be able to say that the noises in both ears are less trouble- some, and my hearing is already more improved by it than any other treatment I have ever had previously." THE INVENTOR IN LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Further comment is superfluous. His et ter can be seen by anyone, together with hundreds of others (many by doctors), and from the general public. The Inventor has received a great many letters from residents in Liverpool and dis- tricts asking him to give them an oppor- tunity of seeing and trying the Akou-Vibra. He has already visited the principal towns in Scotland and the North of England, and has received remarkable proofs of successful cases. Mr. Summer has local testimony proving that the Akou-Vibra will alleviate and cure the most obstinate cases of deafness and head noises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>FREE TESTS DAILY.</head>
        <p>FREE TESTS DAILY.</p>
        <p>Demonstrations are now being given for a short time at 36, THE ARCADE, LORD ST., LIVERPOOL. Hours 10 to 12.30, 2.30 to 6. Saturdays to 2.</p>
