        <p>Padgate Funeral Incident. Sir,—Should be greatly favoured if you would allow me a little space to correct a statement made in your paper last week, concerning the funeral which took place at Pttdgate Church last Sunday week. The writer appears to be in ignorance cf the true facts' when he speaks of the deceased as " a prominent Wesleyan worker." We are all aware he was the organist at the Wesleyan Chapel, but he was also the organist for the Church of England Ccmmunicants' Guild and was one of the most regular attendants at the Early Celebration at Woolston C.E. Mission. Only s recently as two days prior ta his death, he received the Holy Sacrament with the Vicar. The true fact concerning the funeral of one whom we all respected so highly is that the Wesleyan minister on arriving at the Church declined the offer to assist at the service inside, because he could not assist also at the interment, which was taken by the Vicar, as requested. Thanking you for allowing an opportunity of con ect- ing the error.—Yours &amp;c,</p>
        <p>A PARISIIONEE.</p>
        <p>Does Efficiency Benefit the Workers? Sir,—ln Labor Omnia Vincit's last letter he states " Mr. Money's analysis tells us that there are about thirty-eight million poor people in the British Isles." When this statement is examined it is found this is the number of those who evcape it come tax. Thus the man who receives £3 a wrek is a poor man ! L.O.V. is much nearer the ordinary meaning of tl e term poor when he adopts Mr. Rowntree's poverty line of below 21s. Bd. per week for a family income. L.O.V. says " according to Mr. Parker's own state- ment the only increase the workers have received is a matter of about ninepence per week per head." I never made a statement conveying so erroneous a conclusion. L.O.V. arrives at this figure by as-uming that the income of every working class family is below 21s. Sd., sod also that the 22 per cent. increased purchasing power of wages is the only benefit the workers have received since 1871. Thus when LOX. wants to prove how many poor peopls there are he adopts the £l6O per annum limit. When he wants to show how little the poor have received through efficiency he adc pts the 21s. Sd. per week limit. Is this fair? The question between LOX. and myself is as to what extent the benefits of efficiency have been diffused, and how much has been retained by monopoly. L.O.V. wastes his strength in contending that the working- man should dread efficiency, and impede its develop- ment, because the whole benefit has been monopolised. He will forgive me if I place his argument in a tenable position, and what. lam inclined to infer from his last letter, is really his standpoint. Thus the fruits of efficiency have been largely rnonopo'i*ed, and what las been diffused is proportionately small, and much 'Fro than is just. Now in this lam in general agreement, although the portion diffused is twin more than L.O.V. thinks. Let me briefly summarise the benefits the workers have received during the last forty years,which period formed the basis in our last letter. We have, first, a rise in earnings from 10 to 171- per cent.; second, an increased purchasing power of money wages, 22 per cent. ; third, a higher proportion of workers receiving higher class ray through an absorption of the labourers in the industries paying higher wages; fourth, the shortening of hours ; fifth, the public advantages coming to the workers through better sanitation, free schooling, accident and invalidity insurance, old age pensions; all these have been made possible through the products of efficiency—if not,then by what? Confirmation of these benefits could be given from the writings of more careful staticians than Mr. Chiozza Money, but since L.O.V. appears to have a preference for this writer I quote some of the items' given by Mr. Money indicating the material progress of the workers between the period 1867 and 1003. 1867 ' 1003</p>
        <p>145</p>
        <p>731,525</p>
        <p>3 D 8,675,480</p>
        <p>sd. to 57,d.</p>
        <p>100 , (1811). , Tha -first item calls for comment. An advance of £l5 in the earnings of all classes of workers in thirty- six years appears large, but it is probably right when the actual rise in wages is combined with the change from the lower to the higher paid trades. All the other figures are taken frcm Government statistics and doubtless these are also, although I cannot trace their origin. But when the benefits suggested have all been put together it must be acknowledged that, taking ant, account the greatly increased wealth of the country° the workers hive only received this minor portion. Are we not agteed in this? Our real point of difference is that L.O.V. looks upon capital as the chief mooopolis'ng agency, while I contend that capital cannot of itself be a monopoly, and can only function as a monopoly by sheltering behind a true monopo'y For example suppose a capitalist parts with some of his capital for the absolute possession of all the brine bearing land within a profitable working area. Presuming he is an alkali manufacturer, he is in a poeition to monopolise all the benefits which arise through the develpment of efficiency in the getting of salt, and the manufacture of alkali, because he has cornered the raw material, and therefore cannot be controlled by the competition of other manufacturers. L.O.V. would contend that the reason why the workers do not receive a full share of the fruits of efficiency is the result of a monopoly of capi al, whereas the chief culprit is the land monopoly. Capital unaided cannot exercise the functions of monopoly, because it is capable of being created indefinitely, and therefore to speak of capital as a monopoly is a contradiction of terms. Any combination of capital which from the nature of the industry employing it, cannot monopolise natural products is ever subject to the challenge of competing capital. It only holds its position and avoids conflict (or as we call it com- petitirn) by allowing f r the diffusion of the benefits of efficiency by reducing the price of the articles it produces, or the cost of the service it renders. I have not space to take up all the points in L.O.V.'s last letter, but some of his statements call for replies, however brief. Be says "Competition is practically dead in all cur great industries whether it be iron, wire, steel, leather, cotton, thread, mining, railways, and canals." This is inaccurate because a gross exaggeration. "Does not competition force down the wage of the workeis, to the lowest possible level of subsistance?" How can this be when wages are, and have been rising? "Must not the wage of the worker go lower as the prices of commodities decrease ? " No, the opposite is the fact. In the industries where the articles pro- duced have become lower in price through efficiency and competition, the wages have gone most up, while in the industries least influenced by efficiency, wages have remained almost stationary. "With the best machinery cue workman can pro- duce boots and shoes for 1,000 persons." The average number of boots and shoes that the individual worker produces taking the average of workers engaged in factories and the boots at sa. per pair is 284 per</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>Have we not beaten this subject thin ? I think this ought to be my last. CHARLES. E. PARKER. Penketb, January 18th, 1012.</p>
