      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>" DUMPERIES " ON TOUR.</head>
        <p>THE " DUMPERIES " ON TOUR.</p>
        <p>A WEIRD EXHIBITION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The famous travelling circus of the Tariff Reform League is now visiting Warrington. As this is the only exhibi- tion of its kind now touring the provinces, every Warringtonian should make a special effort to call at the Empire Skating Rink. Walk up! Walk up! and see the Great Dump Exhibition. Come and see how the foreigner does the work you ought to do. Why let anybody do your work for you, when you are so fond of work yourself? Why sit on a foreign chair when the Britisher just round the corner will make you one for twice the money? Will you shield your devoted head from good, wet, British rain, with a Japanese umbrella? No! A thousand times no! Let us boycott these aliens, refuse their cheap goods, and, as loyal citizens of Empire, insist on paying twice the price we need ! Remember, ladies and gentlemen, this is the last Great Exhibition. Come in your thousands ! Seize the opportunity while it is yours. Come and hear the band—or you may have tea—afternoon tea (as per advertisement)—if you prefer it. And don't forget the Cinematograph. We do all in our power to please you. All free after four o'clock. Should you wish to hear and see the opening per- formance, a sixpenny ticket will enable you to enjoy this inestimable privilege. Come and hear the British Lions roar at three o'clock this afternoon! You will never forget it. Statistical abstracts and other documents containing reliable figures relating to home and foreign trade and industry must be left in the cloakroom, as the sight of these is apt to create panic amongst the local attend- ants! Visitors are further requested not to examine the exhibits too closely, otherwise "their attention may be diverted from the points which it is the purpose of the exhibition to drive home." (See "Monthly Notes•on Tariff Reform" for January, 1912).</p>
        <p>The above is a fitting comment from "one who has visited the circus during the earlier part of the tour." Fortunately, or unfortu- nately, we had never seen the "Dumperies" until Lady Greenall and her attendant satellites of :the sixpenny league arranged for the really educative exhibition now being held at Warrington. Our gratitude is real and unassumed. The Dump Exhibition is certainly interesting—from many points of view. Primarily it is supposed to illustrate and bring home to an unthinking and indolent public the beauties of WORK per Sc. Now everybody knows a bit about work eitber from hearsay or experi- ence, but the man who loves work for its own sake is something of a rarity. There is a large majority of people who look askance at work, and dodge it, as they would the plague, when they see it coming. Perhaps theirs is the normal view. Work is an excellent and necessary thing as a means to an end, or various ends but by itself it is nothing to enthuse Aout. Yet the great promise of the promoters of these Dump Exhibitions, is WORK—more WORK, and still more WORK. Who is going to vote for work? There are people who can be persuaded (or bought) to walk round the town uttering the pathetic cry "The foreigner's got my job," but if, in the fulness of your heart, you offered one of these bereft gentlemen a job of his own whch would provide him with work and plenty of it, he would probably turn a cold shoulder upon you and wax eloquently impolite. Yet these exhibitions are being " trapesed " about the country in a vain endeavour to show that if we put up a tariff wall against the foreigner we should have more work. Ladies and gentlemen of the Tariff Reform League, what we all want is less work and more pay. "Here's to you,"—if you can provide it! "Dumping," when things are called by their right names,is the exposure for sale in this country of a foreign article at a price less than what it has cost to make in the country of its origin. This is what the " Dumperies " set out to exhibit, but failed. Consequently the definition of the word has been enlarged for convenience, and we are now treated to a miscellaneous assortment of imports which we are to assume would have been made in England if the wicked foreigner had not made them for us and sold them to us at a price quite ridiculously cheap, and within reach of the slenderest pocket. Why anyone should want to make these various goods—from tomb- stones to tapestry—when they can be bought so cheaply, is a mystery only to be explained by the new doctrine that WORK is the thing! All this outcry against the foreigner is essentially unjust and unwise, and we are sure the fact will be patent to all who stroll through the "Dumperies " with the intention of thinking for them- selves. The foreigner might take offence at the suggestion that we English people ought to tax him. And what then? Well, he might stop dumping on our shores and choose others more hospitable. "Just what we want," says the Tariff Reformer, "then there would be more work for all." Alas, only WORK! What the man in the street needs to consider, after due regard to work, is,' where are all these cheap, useful articles (not the gravestones) the foreigner sends us, going - to come from.? _" Oh,_ make</p>
        <p>them in England," whispers the T.R. man. But most of us are busy making other things, and at any rate could not make articles to correspond with those the foreigner sends, for the same price. That is obvious, or the dumped goods would have rotted on the wharves for want of buyers. And if we could not make these imported articles so cheaply, we should individually have to pay more for them—as much more as the manufacturer thought he could charge and still prevent the foreigner from climbing " over the wall." The proper way to talk about the foreigner and dumping of this kind is to say "Let him dump." Faster than he can dump here, we are ourselves dumping on his shores and underselling him in his own markets with the goods it pays us to make. Trade returns— that bogey of the Tariff Reformer—prove this conclusively. We print herewith one or two simple but expressive diagrams illustrating the fact that at dumping we can beat the foreigner at his own gam-,and have no more need to quarrel with him about it than he has to quarrel with us. Even the "Warrington Guardian " says that "England is the market of the world," and curiously enough it comes to such a sane conclusion solely after visiting the " Dumperies." The exhibition strengthened our own convictions in this particular and will doubtless have the same effect on the minds of other visitors who, we hope,will go in their thousands. Our contemporary is</p>
        <p>A very tawdry looking coffin (which no self-respecting person would ever think of choosing as his last sepulchre) was among the exhibits which were supposed to compete unfairly with British manufactures. Upon it was the label " Free Trade died here. Dec. 3, 1910." Evidently there are deader things than Free Trade even in the Tariff Reformer's view, or the " Dumneries " would not be still on tour. Personally, we should say that Tariff Reform was as dead as Queen Anne, whose demise is properly authenticated. Even Mr. Rylands must be finding the corpse too long above ground to ba wholesome, or he would not have confessed to the congregated anti-dumpers that "As a manufacturer he was disposed to view the advent of a tariff with a certain amount of apprehension. In all probability his own trade would not be advanced," but it would be a grand thing for "the thousands of workmen now idle through foreign competition." Evidently, Mr. Rylands is coming to the conclusion that it would be a sad misfortune if, through Tariff Reform, the thousands of tons of blooms and billets "dumped"by the foreigner at Bewsey and Dalton Bank did not as "raw material"escape the taxation so generally advisable. We heartily concur. One of the most interesting features of the exhibition is adisplay of Messrs Rylands Bros.' specialities, presumably made from the "raw material" so conveniently " dumped "by the foreigner. A cessation of ' the "dumping" might deprive the men at Dalton Bank of that WORK which the Tariff Reformer chooses to dangle before our eyes as the "be all and end all of our existence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Photo Burrell. A STRIKING VIEW OF THE CROMWELL STATUE AND THE OLD ACADEMY DURING THE SNOW STORM.</head>
        <p>Photo Burrell. A STRIKING VIEW OF THE CROMWELL STATUE AND THE OLD ACADEMY DURING THE SNOW STORM.</p>
        <p>singularly candid, when, in reviewing the Exhibition in Wednesday's issue it says : " whatever one's political opinions may be, the fact is impressed upon the mind that _England is the market of the</p>
        <p>world. " " Bravo ! The " market " of the world and the " workshop "of the world! What more can we, as a modest nation, expect? Business cannot be slack in the market of the world, and the deduction of our contempo- rary, though based on such unusual evidence,is fully borne out by the " booming " trade returns which have been creating new records in British industry quarter after quarter. Even if the " Guardian " had not led the way, we should certainly have given expression to the view that the Dumping Exhibition provides some very strong arguments against Tariff Reform. The bulk of the goods exhibited as "dumped" compare very unfavourably with English manufactures; in fact the only " exhibits " in the building which reflect any credit on their makers are those of local mani.facture, which in addition to getting an excellent cheap advertisement, emphasise the "nakedness of the (foreigner's) land" in a very suggestive and significant way. We are still the biggest and best manufacturing nation, we still hold "the market of the world" as a result of our Free Trade policy, and we have nothing to fear from the " dumping " of the foreigner.</p>
        <p>must import things which were not going to turn the people of this country out of work. We must see that our imports were either those articles which could not be produced in England or raw material which could be worked up by the workers and the induetries of this country, Free Trade was altogether out of spirit with the times. Leissez faire and Cobdenism as a theory was as dead as the dodo. The policy of state interfer- ence and supervision which now obtained in so many departments of life was in diametrical contradiction to the policy of Free Trade. We talked abut the preven- tion of sweating, the establishment of Wages Boards and minimum wages, and the inspection of factories. How many Wages Boards had sat upon those cheaply imported goods which they saw arcued them ? How many factory inspectors had inspected the goods which were being made in Poland, Japan and different parts of the world where they produced more cheaply than we could ? flow many of those countries had got a minimum wage and regulations which hampered indus- tries in England? The policy of State interference and regulation which the evolution of our civilization and industrial progress had to a large extent made necessary, rendered it impossible for us to allow the unregulated importation of foreign goods to continue. We were told that tariffs would by increasing the cost of production ruin the indutries of England. The now Insurance Act, however, constituted a heavy tax upon our industrie.. In future English goods would have to contribute something like .£17,000,0-.10 a year towards the insurance of the workers, vitilst the foreign goods against which they were comp-tiog would come in scot tree. Toe argument for tariff reform had been greatly strengthened by the passing of that Act. Mr. Philip Snowden, ALP., recently stated that a minimum wage of 50s. per week ,would break down every cotton mill in Lanc shire because foreigners could import their goods free into this country athi e they were not paying their workmen that wage. If Socialists believed thet,l how could they possibly continue to support Free Trade for a single raiment linger ? Mr. W. Furness, in seconding a vote of thanks to Lord Wolmer. sad that Alderman Smethurst and he were surprised and pleased, when they saw the President of the Liberal Association at the exhibition. They naturally thought they had got a good convert, and they congratulated him. Ile (tha speaker) wanted to hand Mr. Parker over to Lady Greene'', but he said "Oh no, wait a bit." (Laughter.) Th 3 present Govern- ment, continued Mr. FIIIIIO9F, appeared to have no concern f.. - the details of our trade, but occupied it3elf in devising means of adding burdens on our progress and increating taxation. In America, if any branch of industry began to fall away, the mania- , factnrers went to the p awers that be ant ageed them to put another 211 or 5 per cent, on the tariff. The result was that trade was kept up and a m nituum wage was guaranteed to the workers.</p>
      </div>
