      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM IN A NUTSHELL.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM IN A NUTSHELL.</p>
        <p>I "You cannot feed the hungry on Board of Trade returns " said Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., in his speech at the opening of the exhibition on Monday. If they analysed the figures, he contended, they provided testi- mony to the inadequacy of the policy which we had pursued all too long. The returns took cognisance of values and not of quantities. Only a few years ago we were the leading nation in the manufacture of iron and steel goods, but now we had dropped to third place, which apparent'y seemed to please "Free Traders." Even last year we exported less in quantity though more in value of steel goods than in the preceding year. The last returns published dis- cl,-,sed the astonishing fact that we imported £15,G00,000 worth less of raw material than last year, while we imported more of the finished manufactured articles. If we were such a great and prosperous manu- facturing country how was it that we were importing a decreasing amount of raw material, on which our manufacturers depended and importing an increasing amount of manufactured articles, which came into direct competition with the goods which our own work people made ? Looking at the other side of the picture we found that we were exporting more and more every year of saw material or semi-raw material, and sending out to our foreign competitors less and less of finished manufactured goods. On the authority of Mr. Philip Snowden, who could hardly be accused of being a bigoted Tariff Reformer, we had it that no fewer than two million families with five mouths to feed were in receipt of a weekly income of less than El a week. If that statement were true, and he bad no reason to doubt it, it was a standing disgrace to any civilised nation, let alone the greateit of nations in the world. We could not end that state of affairs by Board of Trade returns. We could only end it by finding work for those men to do, and we would never find it while , we pursued our present policy. The] another champion of Free Trade, Mr. Chiozza Money, told them that real wages during the last decade had declined, and in the EMS breath that German wages were rising more quickly. In the House of Commons a few months ego Mr. Tennant, the Under-Secretary to the Board of Trade, said that any considerable, or sudden, increase in ' the rate of wages must increase the oust of production, and that increased cost of pro4uctron would invite foreign competition. That was the whole case for Tariff Reform in a nutshell; in fact, he was content for the case for Tariff Reform to be judged not by their own speeches and arguments but by the utterances and evidence provided on that question by their opponents and by the knowledge wh.ch WAS at the disposal of every citizen. In his evidence before a Tariff Commission Mr. Goods 11, the managerf or Messrs. Lever in South Africa, stated that his firm Lad built up a vast business in Englaad, without the aid of Protection, and they had done it by cheap material and cheap labour. There was nothing in the proposals of tariff reform, continued Mr. Smith, bleb would reduce by one pound the amount of raw material that came into our shores. Under the exist- ing conditions it was only by buying their labour cheap, as Mr. Tennapt had said, that they could over- come the enormous competition they bad to meet with. The workingclasses were being dragged down and kept down by our present policy. Lady Greenall, who presided, was presented with a bouquet by Mrs. Rylands, on behalf of the committee of the Women's Tariff Reform League. Councillor Belliamin proposing a vote of tl anks to her ladyship, said a Liberal alderman once told him 'Harold Smith is a very promising young fellow, but you can hare Harold Smith, you cm n have his brother, and you can have Sir Gilbert, but if you will give us Lady Greenall we will whack you every time" (laughter and applause). Lady Greenall assured the audience that there was no danger of her going over to the other side. If she did go over to them they would not, perhaps, be very glad to receive her, because she did not think she would make herself very pleasant to them, and, further, no doubt all the Leaguers would follow her and there would not he a Radical left.in the town. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. MIL RYLANDS'S APYREITENSIOIS</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Pete; ltylands, who presided on Tuesday, said that if the Radical party had been the promoters of tariff reform it would probably have gore round the country like wild fire. As a manufacturer he was dis- posed to view the advent of a tariff with a certain amount of apprehention. He could See perfectly well that if a duty were placed upon any commodity which his firm manufactured there would be introdaced into this country a number of competing firms who would produce the tens of thousands of articles no .v imported from abrcad, and he would feel that competi- tion as seriously as he did the effect of today's dump- ing. In ell probability his own trade would not be advanced and pwsibly it might go the other way, but the great fact would remain that hundreds and thous- ands of workmen now idle through foreign competition would be in full employment. WHY BE FRIGHTENED ay PROTECTION? Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., in re-opening the exhibition, said he saw no reason why we should be frightened by the w)r.l Protection, ft had a nice, soothing sound, and there could not be anything wrong in either its meaning or application, He contended that if the is. tax on corn had beEn maintained to the present time the quirtern loaf would not have been any dearer, whilst the impost would have been raising sub- stantial revenue, and the colonies would have been induced to enter into reciprocal arrangements. WEDNESDAY. Mg. c. g. PARKER'S vlsrr. Alderman Bennett presided on Wednesday and exprested satisfaction that the exhibition had been visited by prominent local Free Traders. He was glad to see Mr. C. E. Parker there the other day. He did not know whether there was any hope of converting him. He should be glad if there was, for Mr. Parker was a man for whom he had a great respect. If Free Traders wsuld clear their minds of cant and come to the exhibition with unbiased minds and an honest de-ire to know the facts Tariff Reform would make converts wholesale.</p>
        <p>THE FOREIGNER'S INDUSZRIAL CONDITIONS. Lord Wolmer, NIP., said it was not the amount of our imports but their nature which mattered. We</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>TIIIIRSDA l'.</head>
        <p>TIIIIRSDA l'.</p>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM AS THE PANACEA FOR IRELAND'S WOES. . _ _</p>
        <p>„ I Considerable interest was taken on Thursday in the visit of Mr. George Wyndham, M.P., formerly Chief Secretary tor Ireland. Mr. E. J. Hall, presided. "lhe exhibition ” declared Mr. Wyndham "vindicates the truth of our own belief and it exasperates our opponents. To such a point is that exasperation carried that in some towns the dumping shop has actually been raided by the enemy aud its interesting exhibits damaged or destroyed. They can easily be replaced, but the false idol, the Dsgon of Cobrlenism, bits been smashed into smithereens for ever and can never be restored to its pedestal of intellectual arrogance." Mr. Wyndham said the exhibition gave some evidence of what the Empire could produce. If they looked at those products and asked ia what measure they were forthcoming, they would find that the " dear loaf" was not only a bogey but a lie. If they took all the wheat which was used in tbis country tact year, more than half of that came fr,orn this country and the Empire. Considerably less than half of it came from foreign countries. If that were true, it was an untruth to say that ty accepting what Canada proferred we could raise the price of living in this country by a single farthing. Referring to the cinematograph pictures of life in Germany, Mr. Wyndham said his hearers would probably have teen told that they would tee that mishappy German in his sordid misery, eating black bread, sniffling doubtfully at horse sausage, dressed in rags,—in fact the tatterde- malion of Pro'ection. (Laughter. What they would actually see, however, was a happy, cheerful, well-dressed man, drinking his beer in the beer garden, listening to the land, hurrying in crowds out of the factory with a smile on his face, because the statesmen of that country, from Bismarck downwards bad thought it was the duty of statesmanship to see that the worker bad an opportunity of winning a wage. (Applause.) It was little wonder that such an 'exhibition should attract people in tholisanth, even in spite of distr.mtions of Home Rule—indeed tariff reform was the only solution of the Iri:h rhffliolty. Home Rule would aggravate it. Tariff reform wonld Ival the wound of centuries. By tiriff reform alone coul I we grant two great boons to Ireland—we could give her our market free and thus give her the largest preference given throughout the Empire, and by tariff reform only we could mainta'n the credit of this country and so continue to lend Ireland money in order that the farmers of Ireland might own their farms. (Applause.) That measure under which, with the pre- ceding Acts, some 233,000 men had become owners of the soil had been allowed to drop because their opponents preferred the plison of Home Rule to the ' cure of tariff reform. In tariff retain was to be found the only safeguard for the security of the j3rit6h Empire. Free Traders I Made much of the fact that last year the total value of ,1 our exports and imports was £1,200,000,000. In reply to that, tariff reformers said "We want work and wages. Profits are not enough unless they are trans- lated into wages which reach the pockets of the people." One would have supposed that during a year when our trade was worth so much, the labour exchanges which have had to shut their doors. On the contrary they were thronged with applicants and in the nine months from January to September of last year the number of applicants for a job who got no job was 873,513. If that state of affairs continued during the remaining three months, it meant that in that year of specious but false prosperity more thn 1,000,000 men came to the State for the lowest form or employment at pitiable wages and could not obtain it.</p>
        <p>" Sit ASH THE RADICALS." [ Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart„ in proposing a Tao of</p>
      </div>
