      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Gilts BE</head>
        <p>Gilts BE</p>
        <p>Furnishing Co.</p>
        <p>(J. R. GIANT, Proprietor.) PEMBROKE PLAOE, Liverpool</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <p>thanks to Mr. Wyndham, said they would never be content until they had "smashed all the Free Traders and Radicals out of the town." "TUB CHAMBER OF HORRORS."_</p>
        <p>Mr. Duveen, one of the promoters of the Exhibition, said it had a gruesome as well as an interesting side, in lint he called it a charater of horrors,</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. PRESENT PROSPERITY DUE TO CAPITAL INVESTED ABROAD. Friday's opening ceremony was performed by Mr.J.S. Harmood Banner, M.P., who pleaded the advantages of Tariff " Reform " from a manufacturer's point of view. He said they had undoubtedly in Warrington a necessity for the protection of their trade, both at home and abroad, because their products went all over the world. Personally, he had not the smallest commileration with the consumer. Whilst the consumer as a very important person, it did cot matter a bit about hira unless he bad something in his pocket to consume. His sympathies were with the worker ; the man yaw was thrifty and put his bit in his pocket in order that he might be a consumer. They had to consider whether dumping added to their wages and prosperity. He was convinced that dumping did not help them to put money in their pockets or increase the balance at the Savings Bank. Tariff Reform was not altogether a question of protective duties. At present they were apparently in the zenith of a very prosperous time. We bad sent our money abroad and as a result the trade was coming to this country, but owing to the present fiscal system we were the last to benefit and</p>
        <p>ihe first to suffer. When trade went. down the dumping prk.ceas would be all powerful again and Lring us to. that position when we would nut get the orders we needed.</p>
        <p>in seconding a vote of thanks Alderman FORSITAW remarked that he did not say they ought to go the "whole hog," but he thought Tariff Reform ought to be given a trial.</p>
        <p>Sir 4131LnEnT GREENALL, in returning thanks, said he hoped all Free Traders in Warrington would be led by the nose to the exhibition before it closed on Saturday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>NERVE STORMS IN WOMEN. Weakness and Nerve Cullapse. ALARMINGLY ON THE INCREASE.</head>
        <p>NERVE STORMS IN WOMEN. Weakness and Nerve Cullapse. ALARMINGLY ON THE INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Hysteria is the result of a highly-strung temperament; such persons are extremely susceptible to excitement, worry or brain fag, and more especially to general weakness, swamis and indigestion. No treatment can succeed which does not restore the nerves ta a healthy and vigorous condition, and nothing can accomplish this so speedily or so successfully as Dr. Cassell's Tablets for Weak and Nervous People. Gueranteea perfectly safe, composed of the purest ingredients, and mant!facturei under the personal supervision of a fully qualified chemist, they are largely used all over the- world with unparelleled success in all cases of organic weakness, digestive and kidney troubles, and all diseases arising from physical debility in children or adults. Ask for Dr. Csssell's Tablets, price 101 d., I ill, and 2/9 at all chemists. A free sample bax sent on, receipt of 21. for postage, etc, ta Dr. Cassell's Ca. Ltd., King St. W., Alanchester.</p>
