      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WYCLIFFE ANNUAL CONGREGATIONAL TEA MEETING.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE ANNUAL CONGREGATIONAL TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Memorial Buildings at Wycliffe wore a very bright and cheery appearance on the occasion of the annual tea meting, held on Thursday evening last. A large and representative company sat down to tea at tables prettily arranged, well provided and decorated with fruit and flowers. The organisers, Mr and Mrs J Hawthorn and Mr and Mrs J Ball, had obtained the willing and able services of a band of ladies, and the result was a thoroughly sociable and enjoyable function. The following ladies presided over the tables :—Mesdames J W Slack, J S Garland, J Longland, E Wallington, E V Walker, Winskill, Farrar, J Jeffery, S W Jeffery, Pickton, J Hawthorn, .1 Sill and Miss Beswick, assisted by a bevy of young ladies. After tea the Rev. John Yonge occupied the chair and an excellent and varied pro— gramme arranged by Mr. W H Payton, Mus. F3ac., was rendered. Songs were given by Miss M Atkinson, Miss naviour, Mrs J Hawthorn, Miss Hewitt (encore). Mr W Clay, Mr W Booth (encore), Mr NY II Wallington (encore), and Mr H Hawthorn Violin solos were .endered by Mrs. Haviour with great acceptance. Mr. H. M. Ashton read some hnmorous remarks by " Mrs. Green "in his usual able manner and a capital and witty dialogue entitled "A. pair of lunatics " was cleverly presented by Miss Corney and Mr. Chedburn. A vote of thanks t) all those who had arranged the tea and to the presiding ladies and friends was moved by Mr. Law and seconded by Mr. Witts and a vote of thanks to the artistes who had Provided so tine an entertainment, was moved by Mr. S. W. Jeffery, and seconded by Mr. W. Brown. The proceedings terminated about 10 o'clock by the• singing of the Doxology, and it was generally felt that a mostenjoyable:and happy evening had been spent.</p>
        <p>DEATH. WHITHA3I.—On Friday, January '2,6th, Walter (Dag)s. after a long and painful illness. Aged 38 years fourth sou of Mary and Dyson Whitham.</p>
