        <p>IN ITE,rt LITTLE BOOK of Canadian Indian tnythri, entitled " Vancouver Legends,': by Miss Pauline Johnson, the well-known Canadian poetess, who hag sung so harmoniously and with such a genuine love of Canadian forest, river and lake, appears the following poem, a typical example of her verse— " It is dusk on the Lost Lagoon, And we two dreaming the dusk away, Beneath the drift of a twilight grey, Beneath the drowse of an el-ding day And the curve of the golden moon. "It is dark on the Lost Lagoon, And gone are the depths of haunting blue, The grouping gulls, and the old canoe, The singing firs and the dusk and—you, And gone is the golden moon. "0 ! lure of the Lost Lagoon, I dream to-night that my paddl3 blurs The purple shade where the seaweed stirs ; 1 hear the call of the singing firs In the hush of the golden moon."</p>
